      "content": "You are an expert in scientific analysis integration with extensive experience in refining and improving research analyses. Your task is to provide comprehensive refinement comments for dataset analysis, problem investigation, and baseline assessment, focusing on integration, consistency, and improvement with decision support.\n\n\nDECISION SUPPORT INFORMATION:\nModel Selection: {}\nEvaluation Strategy: {}\nData Preparation: {}\nImplementation Plan: {}\nRisk Assessment: {}\n\n\n1. Review Dataset Analysis:\n   - Evaluate biological relevance and interpretability\n   - Assess technical quality and reproducibility\n   - Check preprocessing recommendations\n   - Validate quality assessment\n   - Suggest improvements\n   - Consider decision support alignment\n\n2. Review Problem Investigation:\n   - Evaluate problem definition and scope\n   - Assess research questions and hypotheses\n   - Check analysis methods and validation\n   - Validate biological mechanisms\n   - Suggest improvements\n   - Consider decision support integration\n\n3. Review Baseline Assessment:\n   - Evaluate model analysis and comparison\n   - Assess evaluation framework\n   - Check performance analysis\n   - Validate improvement suggestions\n   - Suggest improvements\n   - Consider decision support recommendations\n\n4. Ensure Integration:\n   - Check consistency across analyses\n   - Validate biological relevance\n   - Assess technical feasibility\n   - Evaluate practical implementation\n   - Suggest improvements\n   - Integrate decision support insights\n\nDataset Analysis:\n{\n  \"content\": \"{\\n  \\\"experimental_design\\\": {\\n    \\\"setup\\\": {\\n      \\\"biological_conditions\\\": [\\n        \\\"K562 chronic myeloid leukemia cell line\\\",\\n        \\\"CRISPRi (dCas9-KRAB) transcriptional repression\\\",\\n        \\\"Unseen-perturbation prediction setting: held-out perturbations have zero training cells\\\",\\n        \\\"Control condition: non-targeting / safe-targeting guides (implied control group in cellforge_input.h5ad)\\\"\\n      ],\\n      \\\"experimental_protocol\\\": \\\"Pooled CRISPRi screen in K562 cells; single-cell RNA-seq (likely 10x-based) with perturbation identity recovered from guide capture/assignment. Expression stored as counts-per-10k followed by log1p. Predictions must be produced for each held-out perturbation's mean expression vector across all genes in the same order and normalization. Training restricted to cellforge_input.h5ad only; no external copies of the screen may be used.\\\",\\n      \\\"controls\\\": [\\n        \\\"Non-targeting control guides (NTC)\\\",\\n        \\\"Safe-targeting / intergenic control guides (if present)\\\",\\n        \\\"Control mean expression used as baseline for delta-based metrics (pearson_delta)\\\"\\n      ],\\n      \\\"replicates\\\": \\\"Likely multiple guide RNAs per target gene and pooled cells; biological replication across guides and possibly across experimental batches/lanes. Exact replicate structure must be confirmed from obs metadata (guide ID, target gene, batch/lane, replicate columns).\\\",\\n      \\\"biological_validation\\\": \\\"No wet-lab validation is performed in this task; validation is computational via held-out perturbations. Biological plausibility of predictions can be checked against known K562 CRISPRi effects and pathway-level coherence.\\\",\\n      \\\"decision_support_alignment\\\": \\\"Data preparation, implementation plan, and risk assessment blocks are empty; recommendations below are therefore generic and should be reconciled with the actual obs/var metadata before execution.\\\"\\n    },\\n    \\\"perturbation\\\": {\\n      \\\"design\\\": \\\"CRISPRi knockdown of individual genes, one perturbation per held-out key; perturbations are unseen at training time, so the model must generalize from gene-level features (e.g., gene embeddings, co-expression, network priors) rather than memorizing perturbation identities.\\\",\\n      \\\"types\\\": [\\n        \\\"CRISPRi (dCas9-KRAB) gene repression\\\",\\n        \\\"Single-gene perturbations (one target per held-out key)\\\",\\n        \\\"Control perturbations (non-targeting)\\\"\\n      ],\\n      \\\"doses\\\": [\\n        \\\"Not applicable / not reported; CRISPRi is expected to produce partial knockdown rather than complete knockout\\\",\\n        \\\"Effective knockdown strength varies by guide and target, introducing dose-like heterogeneity\\\"\\n      ],\\n      \\\"timepoints\\\": [\\n        \\\"Single endpoint (not specified); assume one harvest timepoint\\\",\\n        \\\"Possible batch/lane variation in culture duration should be checked in obs metadata\\\"\\n      ],\\n      \\\"biological_controls\\\": [\\n        \\\"Non-targeting controls define the baseline expression and delta reference\\\",\\n        \\\"Multiple guides per target provide internal replication\\\",\\n        \\\"Control cells should be matched for batch, lane, and cell-cycle composition where possible\\\"\\n      ],\\n      \\\"decision_support_considerations\\\": \\\"Because perturbations are unseen, the critical design consideration is feature representation of perturbations (gene identity, functional annotations, co-expression modules) and a robust control baseline. Empty decision-support blocks mean no pre-specified constraints on model class, but the evaluator's pearson_delta metric requires an accurate control mean, so control estimation should be treated as a first-class modeling component.\\\"\\n    },\\n    \\\"samples\\\": {\\n      \\\"size\\\": \\\"Not specified in the task metadata; must be read from cellforge_input.h5ad (n_obs, n_vars). Expected to be thousands to hundreds of thousands of cells across hundreds to thousands of perturbations, typical of the Adamson et al. K562 CRISPRi screen.\\\",\\n      \\\"distribution\\\": \\\"Cells are pooled and unevenly distributed across perturbations and guides; per-perturbation cell counts are expected to be highly variable (long-tailed). Control cells are typically a sizable fraction. Held-out perturbations have zero cells by construction.\\\",\\n      \\\"characteristics\\\": [\\n        \\\"Cell-level covariates: total counts, detected genes, guide assignment confidence, cell-cycle phase, batch/lane\\\",\\n        \\\"Perturbation-level covariates: target gene ID, guide count, target expression level\\\",\\n        \\\"Gene-level covariates: mean expression, detection rate, variance\\\"\\n      ],\\n      \\\"biological_variability\\\": \\\"Cell-to-cell heterogeneity in K562 (cell cycle, differentiation state, clonal variation) plus genuine gene-specific knockdown effects. Perturbation effect sizes vary widely; some perturbations produce minimal transcriptional change.\\\",\\n      \\\"technical_variability\\\": \\\"Batch/lane effects, guide-capture efficiency, library size, ambient RNA, doublet/multiple-guide assignment, and CRISPRi knockdown efficiency variation.\\\",\\n      \\\"decision_support_requirements\\\": \\\"Empty decision-support blocks provide no sample-size or power constraints; nonetheless, per-perturbation cell counts and guide counts should be summarized to weight training and to flag low-confidence perturbations when analyzing training data.\\\"\\n    },\\n    \\\"validation\\\": {\\n      \\\"biological\\\": {\\n        \\\"methods\\\": [\\n          \\\"Held-out perturbation evaluation (unseen-perturbation split)\\\",\\n          \\\"Pearson delta correlation between predicted and true change from control\\\",\\n          \\\"MSE on mean expression\\\",\\n          \\\"MSE / PCC / R2 on top-20 differentially expressed genes per perturbation\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"controls\\\": [\\n          \\\"Control mean expression as delta baseline\\\",\\n          \\\"Top-20 DE genes defined from true data as the high-signal evaluation subset\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"metrics\\\": [\\n          \\\"pearson_delta\\\",\\n          \\\"MSE\\\",\\n          \\\"top-20 MSE\\\",\\n          \\\"top-20 PCC\\\",\\n          \\\"top-20 R2\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"results\\\": \\\"Not yet computed; evaluator is Gene-Mila's external scorer. No results are available in the provided metadata.\\\",\\n        \\\"interpretation\\\": \\\"pearson_delta rewards correct direction and relative magnitude of transcriptional change; top-20 metrics reward recovery of the strongest biological effects. A model that predicts control mean for all perturbations will score near zero on pearson_delta and poorly on top-20 metrics.\\\",\\n        \\\"decision_support_validation\\\": \\\"No validation plan is supplied in the empty decision-support blocks; the evaluator's metrics should be mirrored in an internal held-out split of training perturbations to select model and hyperparameters before final prediction.\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"technical\\\": {\\n        \\\"methods\\\": [\\n          \\\"Held-out split within training perturbations (perturbation-level cross-validation)\\\",\\n          \\\"Inspection of guide-assignment confidence and cell-level QC\\\",\\n          \\\"Reproducibility check of normalization (CP10k + log1p)\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"controls\\\": [\\n          \\\"Non-targeting controls for baseline\\\",\\n          \\\"Multiple guides per target for within-target consistency\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"metrics\\\": [\\n          \\\"Per-perturbation cell counts\\\",\\n          \\\"Guide concordance\\\",\\n          \\\"QC pass rates (counts, genes detected, mitochondrial fraction if available)\\\",\\n          \\\"Correlation between replicate pseudo-bulks\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"results\\\": \\\"Not computed; requires loading cellforge_input.h5ad.\\\",\\n        \\\"interpretation\\\": \\\"Technical validation should confirm that the stored matrix is already CP10k + log1p (not raw counts) and that control cells are sufficient and well-matched for delta computation.\\\",\\n        \\\"decision_support_technical_validation\\\": \\\"Empty decision-support blocks; recommend documenting the exact normalization state and control definition before modeling.\\\"\\n      }\\n    }\\n  },\\n  \\\"data_properties\\\": {\\n    \\\"distribution\\\": {\\n      \\\"biological\\\": {\\n        \\\"patterns\\\": [\\n          \\\"Log1p CP10k expression with strong zero-inflation and overdispersion typical of scRNA-seq\\\",\\n          \\\"Gene-level mean-variance relationship dominated by a few highly expressed genes (e.g., ribosomal, MALAT1, GAPDH)\\\",\\n          \\\"Perturbation effects concentrated in target gene and functionally related genes/pathways\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"variability\\\": \\\"Biological variance arises from cell state, cell cycle, and perturbation effect size; effect sizes are expected to be modest for most CRISPRi knockdowns in K562.\\\",\\n        \\\"outliers\\\": [\\n          \\\"Cells with unusually high counts or mitochondrial content\\\",\\n          \\\"Perturbations with very few cells or low guide confidence\\\",\\n          \\\"Genes with extreme expression or detection artifacts\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"interpretation\\\": \\\"The data are sparse, log-normalized, and perturbation effects are typically small relative to cell-to-cell noise, so mean-based predictions must be estimated from sufficient cells per perturbation.\\\",\\n        \\\"biological_significance\\\": \\\"Recovering the target-gene knockdown and coherent pathway responses is the biological signal of interest; top-20 DE genes capture this signal.\\\",\\n        \\\"decision_support_implications\\\": \\\"Empty decision-support blocks; modeling should prioritize shrinkage of per-perturbation means and use of gene-level priors to handle small effect sizes.\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"technical\\\": {\\n        \\\"patterns\\\": [\\n          \\\"Library-size variation partially removed by CP10k but residual depth effects may remain\\\",\\n          \\\"Batch/lane effects on gene detection and expression\\\",\\n          \\\"Ambient RNA and doublets causing spurious guide assignments\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"variability\\\": \\\"Technical variance from sequencing depth, capture efficiency, and guide assignment confidence; may be confounded with perturbation if batches are unbalanced.\\\",\\n        \\\"artifacts\\\": [\\n          \\\"Doublets/multiplets with ambiguous guide identity\\\",\\n          \\\"Low-confidence guide calls\\\",\\n          \\\"Ambient contamination\\\",\\n          \\\"Batch-correlated expression shifts\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"interpretation\\\": \\\"Because the matrix is already normalized, additional batch correction must be applied cautiously to avoid removing perturbation signal; batch correction should be validated on control cells.\\\",\\n        \\\"mitigation\\\": [\\n          \\\"Filter low-confidence guide assignments and doublets\\\",\\n          \\\"Include batch as a covariate or use control-anchored correction\\\",\\n          \\\"Use robust per-perturbation mean estimation (e.g., trimmed means or pseudobulk)\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"decision_support_considerations\\\": \\\"No decision-support constraints provided; batch handling should be evaluated via held-out perturbation performance rather than assumed beneficial.\\\"\\n      }\\n    },\\n    \\\"features\\\": {\\n      \\\"biological\\\": {\\n        \\\"types\\\": [\\n          \\\"Protein-coding genes (primary feature space)\\\",\\n          \\\"Target genes of perturbations (knockdown signature)\\\",\\n          \\\"Co-expressed gene modules and pathways\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"relevance\\\": \\\"All genes are required in the output, but only a subset carries perturbation signal; target genes and pathway members are most relevant for delta and top-20 metrics.\\\",\\n        \\\"selection\\\": [\\n          \\\"Use all genes for output completeness\\\",\\n          \\\"For model training, consider highly variable genes or pathway/gene-set features as auxiliary inputs\\\",\\n          \\\"Prioritize genes with detectable expression in K562\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"interpretation\\\": \\\"Feature relevance is gene- and perturbation-dependent; a model must output the full gene vector while internally focusing on informative genes.\\\",\\n        \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Validate feature utility via held-out perturbation pearson_delta and top-20 metrics.\\\",\\n        \\\"decision_support_features\\\": \\\"Empty decision-support blocks; feature selection should be driven by cross-validated held-out perturbation performance.\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"technical\\\": {\\n        \\\"types\\\": [\\n          \\\"Gene identifiers and ordering (must match exactly)\\\",\\n          \\\"Detection rate and mean expression per gene\\\",\\n          \\\"Library-size and batch covariates in obs\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"quality\\\": \\\"Gene ordering and normalization must be preserved exactly; any reordering or renormalization will invalidate the submission.\\\",\\n        \\\"selection\\\": [\\n          \\\"Do not drop genes from the output\\\",\\n          \\\"Filter genes only for internal modeling, not for output\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"interpretation\\\": \\\"Technical feature quality hinges on correct gene alignment and normalization state.\\\",\\n        \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Check that var_names order matches the expected output and that a control-cell mean computed from the matrix is on the log1p CP10k scale.\\\",\\n        \\\"decision_support_technical\\\": \\\"No decision-support constraints; alignment checks are mandatory.\\\"\\n      }\\n    },\\n    \\\"quality\\\": {\\n      \\\"biological\\\": {\\n        \\\"metrics\\\": [\\n          \\\"Per-perturbation cell counts\\\",\\n          \\\"Guide-level concordance\\\",\\n          \\\"Target-gene knockdown magnitude\\\",\\n          \\\"Pathway coherence of top DE genes\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"assessment\\\": \\\"Biological quality is expected to be high for well-powered perturbations and low for perturbations with few cells or weak guides; held-out perturbations cannot be assessed directly and must be predicted.\\\",\\n        \\\"issues\\\": [\\n          \\\"Weak or variable CRISPRi knockdown\\\",\\n          \\\"Perturbations with no measurable transcriptional effect\\\",\\n          \\\"Cell-state confounding of perturbation effects\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"mitigation\\\": [\\n          \\\"Shrink predictions toward control for low-confidence perturbations\\\",\\n          \\\"Use gene-level priors and pathway information\\\",\\n          \\\"Weight training perturbations by cell count and effect reliability\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Internal held-out perturbation cross-validation on training perturbations.\\\",\\n        \\\"decision_support_quality\\\": \\\"Empty decision-support blocks; quality thresholds should be set from the training data distribution.\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"technical\\\": {\\n        \\\"metrics\\\": [\\n          \\\"Total counts per cell\\\",\\n          \\\"Genes detected per cell\\\",\\n          \\\"Mitochondrial fraction (if available)\\\",\\n          \\\"Guide assignment confidence\\\",\\n          \\\"Doublet scores\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"assessment\\\": \\\"Technical quality must be assessed from obs/var metadata; the matrix is pre-normalized, so QC should not renormalize but may filter cells for training.\\\",\\n        \\\"issues\\\": [\\n          \\\"Low-quality cells\\\",\\n          \\\"Ambiguous guide assignment\\\",\\n          \\\"Batch confounding\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"mitigation\\\": [\\n          \\\"Filter low-quality cells and low-confidence guides for training\\\",\\n          \\\"Use pseudobulk or robust means\\\",\\n          \\\"Control-anchored batch correction\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Compare QC-filtered vs unfiltered control means and held-out performance.\\\",\\n        \\\"decision_support_technical_quality\\\": \\\"No decision-support constraints; filtering choices should be validated against held-out perturbation metrics.\\\"\\n      }\\n    },\\n    \\\"noise\\\": {\\n      \\\"biological\\\": {\\n        \\\"sources\\\": [\\n          \\\"Cell-to-cell transcriptional heterogeneity\\\",\\n          \\\"Cell-cycle state\\\",\\n          \\\"Variable knockdown efficiency\\\",\\n          \\\"Clonal variation\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"impact\\\": \\\"Reduces signal-to-noise for per-perturbation mean estimation, especially for weakly expressed genes and small-effect perturbations.\\\",\\n        \\\"assessment\\\": \\\"Estimate via variance decomposition across cells and guides within a perturbation.\\\",\\n        \\\"mitigation\\\": [\\n          \\\"Aggregate to pseudobulk/mean over many cells\\\",\\n          \\\"Shrink toward control or gene-level priors\\\",\\n          \\\"Use guide-level replication\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Held-out perturbation cross-validation.\\\",\\n        \\\"decision_support_noise\\\": \\\"Empty decision-support blocks; noise handling should be tuned empirically.\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"technical\\\": {\\n        \\\"sources\\\": [\\n          \\\"Library-size variation\\\",\\n          \\\"Ambient RNA\\\",\\n          \\\"Doublets\\\",\\n          \\\"Batch effects\\\",\\n          \\\"Guide-capture dropout\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"impact\\\": \\\"Can bias per-perturbation means and inflate delta correlations if correlated with perturbation identity.\\\",\\n        \\\"assessment\\\": \\\"Check batch-perturbation confounding and guide-assignment confidence distributions.\\\",\\n        \\\"mitigation\\\": [\\n          \\\"Filter doublets and low-confidence guides\\\",\\n          \\\"Include batch covariates\\\",\\n          \\\"Use control-anchored normalization\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Compare predictions with and without technical corrections on held-out training perturbations.\\\",\\n        \\\"decision_support_technical_noise\\\": \\\"No decision-support constraints; corrections must be validated, not assumed.\\\"\\n      }\\n    }\\n  },\\n  \\\"preprocessing\\\": {\\n    \\\"normalization\\\": {\\n      \\\"strategies\\\": {\\n        \\\"recommendations\\\": [\\n          \\\"Do not renormalize the stored matrix; it is already CP10k + log1p\\\",\\n          \\\"Compute control mean on the same log1p CP10k scale\\\",\\n          \\\"For internal modeling, optionally standardize features (z-score) but invert to the original scale for output\\\",\\n          \\\"Use pseudobulk means or robust means per perturbation for training targets\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"rationale\\\": \\\"The evaluator expects predictions on the same normalization as cellforge_input.h5ad; any additional transformation must be inverted exactly.\\\",\\n        \\\"implementation\\\": \\\"Load h5ad with scanpy/anndata; verify max values and integer-like structure are consistent with log1p CP10k; compute control mean as the mean over control cells; train model to predict delta from control; add control mean back for final means.\\\",\\n        \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Check that predicted control perturbation (if included) reproduces the control mean; verify output shape n_perts x n_genes and gene order.\\\",\\n        \\\"biological_validation\\\": \\\"Confirm that predicted deltas for known training perturbations recapitulate target-gene knockdown.\\\",\\n        \\\"decision_support_normalization\\\": \\\"Empty decision-support blocks; normalization must match the evaluator exactly.\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"quality_control\\\": {\\n        \\\"procedures\\\": [\\n          \\\"Filter cells with low total counts / low genes detected (thresholds from training distribution)\\\",\\n          \\\"Filter cells with high mitochondrial fraction if mt genes are annotated\\\",\\n          \\\"Remove doublets/multiplets and low-confidence guide assignments\\\",\\n          \\\"Retain control cells for baseline estimation\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"criteria\\\": [\\n          \\\"Counts and genes-detected thresholds set at distribution tails (e.g., 1st/99th percentile or MAD-based)\\\",\\n          \\\"Guide assignment confidence threshold\\\",\\n          \\\"Doublet score threshold\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"implementation\\\": \\\"Apply filters to training cells only; do not alter the gene set or normalization. Recompute control mean after filtering and compare.\\\",\\n        \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Assess stability of control mean and held-out perturbation performance under different QC thresholds.\\\",\\n        \\\"biological_validation\\\": \\\"Ensure QC does not remove perturbation-responsive cell states disproportionately.\\\",\\n        \\\"decision_support_qc\\\": \\\"No decision-support constraints; QC thresholds should be data-driven and validated.\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"feature_selection\\\": {\\n        \\\"methods\\\": [\\n          \\\"Use all genes for output\\\",\\n          \\\"For model input, consider HVGs, pathway/gene-set scores, or gene embeddings\\\",\\n          \\\"Use target-gene identity and functional annotations as perturbation features\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"criteria\\\": [\\n          \\\"Retain genes with adequate detection in K562\\\",\\n          \\\"Prioritize genes with known perturbation responsiveness\\\",\\n          \\\"Avoid features that leak held-out perturbation identity\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"implementation\\\": \\\"Compute HVGs on training cells; build perturbation feature matrix from gene annotations/embeddings; ensure no held-out perturbation data are used.\\\",\\n        \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Compare held-out perturbation metrics across feature sets.\\\",\\n        \\\"biological_validation\\\": \\\"Check that selected features recover known K562 biology.\\\",\\n        \\\"decision_support_features\\\": \\\"Empty decision-support blocks; feature choice should be cross-validated.\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"batch_correction\\\": {\\n        \\\"methods\\\": [\\n          \\\"Control-anchored batch correction (e.g., regress out batch on controls, apply to all)\\\",\\n          \\\"Include batch as covariate in the model\\\",\\n          \\\"Harmony/ComBat only if batch is clearly confounded and validated on controls\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"criteria\\\": [\\n          \\\"Batch must not be confounded with perturbation in training\\\",\\n          \\\"Correction must preserve perturbation signal in training perturbations\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"implementation\\\": \\\"Identify batch columns in obs; apply correction to training data; recompute control mean per batch and pool.\\\",\\n        \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Held-out perturbation cross-validation comparing corrected vs uncorrected.\\\",\\n        \\\"biological_validation\\\": \\\"Confirm known perturbation effects remain after correction.\\\",\\n        \\\"\",\n  \"error\": \"Failed to parse JSON response\"\n}\n\nProblem Investigation:\n{\n  \"content\": \"{\\n  \\\"problem_definition\\\": {\\n    \\\"formal_definition\\\": {\\n      \\\"biological_context\\\": \\\"K562 chronic myeloid leukemia cells under CRISPRi (dCas9-KRAB) knockdown of individual genes. The training file cellforge_input.h5ad contains single-cell log1p CP10K expression for perturbations that are observed in training; the held-out perturbations in heldout_perturbations.json have zero cells in training. The task is to predict, for each unseen CRISPRi target, the mean expression vector over all genes in the same gene order and normalisation as the input.\\\",\\n      \\\"input_output_mapping\\\": \\\"Input: (i) annotated scRNA-seq AnnData with cells x genes, obs including perturbation label and control label, var gene names/order, X log1p(CP10K); (ii) heldout_perturbations.json list of unseen perturbation keys. Output: predictions.npz with perts (held-out keys in listed order) and means (n_perts x n_genes float), where means[p,g] = predicted mean log1p(CP10K) expression of gene g in K562 cells carrying perturbation p. Implicitly the evaluator compares delta = mean_pert - mean_control per perturbation.\\\",\\n      \\\"biological_constraints\\\": [\\n        \\\"All predictions must be on the same log1p(CP10K) scale as the input; no renormalisation.\\\",\\n        \\\"Gene order must exactly match var_names of cellforge_input.h5ad.\\\",\\n        \\\"CRISPRi acts primarily by transcriptional repression of the target gene and, through partial knockdown, of downstream regulons; predicted effects should be sparse and mostly non-positive for direct targets.\\\",\\n        \\\"K562-specific expression program, copy-number heterogeneity, and low per-cell counts constrain realistic effect sizes.\\\",\\n        \\\"Held-out perturbations are unseen; predictions must generalise from gene-level features and from observed perturbation effects, not from memorised labels.\\\",\\n        \\\"Control mean must be estimated from the provided control cells only; no external screen data may be used.\\\"\\n      ],\\n      \\\"evaluation_criteria\\\": [\\n        \\\"pearson_delta: per-perturbation Pearson correlation between predicted and true change-from-control vectors; rewards correct direction and relative magnitude of gene-level shifts.\\\",\\n        \\\"MSE: mean squared error of predicted vs true mean expression (or delta) across genes and perturbations; penalises scale and baseline errors.\\\",\\n        \\\"Top-20 DE metrics (MSE/PCC/R2): correctness of the strongest differentially expressed genes, which carry most biological signal.\\\",\\n        \\\"Biological validity: predicted target-gene knockdown, enrichment of known K562/CRISPRi regulon members, and consistency with essential-gene and pathway annotations.\\\",\\n        \\\"Reproducibility: deterministic pipeline, fixed seeds, documented preprocessing, and stable predictions under resampling.\\\"\\n      ],\\n      \\\"success_metrics\\\": [\\n        \\\"Mean pearson_delta across held-out perturbations substantially above zero and above a control-mean baseline.\\\",\\n        \\\"MSE lower than the variance of true perturbation means around control mean.\\\",\\n        \\\"Top-20 DE PCC and R2 above baseline and consistent with known K562 biology.\\\",\\n        \\\"Correct sign and magnitude of knockdown for the CRISPRi target gene where it is expressed.\\\",\\n        \\\"Stable rankings of top predicted DE genes across bootstrap resamples of training cells.\\\"\\n      ]\\n    },\\n    \\\"key_variables\\\": {\\n      \\\"biological\\\": [\\n        \\\"Perturbation identity (CRISPRi target gene) and its baseline expression in K562.\\\",\\n        \\\"Gene-gene regulatory relationships (transcription factor targets, co-expression modules, protein complexes).\\\",\\n        \\\"Cell-state heterogeneity in K562 (cell cycle, copy-number variation, differentiation state).\\\",\\n        \\\"Essentiality and network centrality of the target gene.\\\",\\n        \\\"Baseline control expression mean and variance per gene.\\\"\\n      ],\\n      \\\"technical\\\": [\\n        \\\"Library size and CP10K normalisation, log1p transform.\\\",\\n        \\\"Guide RNA efficiency and CRISPRi knockdown strength (unobserved per perturbation).\\\",\\n        \\\"Batch/experiment effects and donor/ replicate structure in obs.\\\",\\n        \\\"Gene filtering and var order in the AnnData.\\\",\\n        \\\"Number of cells per observed perturbation (uneven support).\\\"\\n      ],\\n      \\\"relationships\\\": [\\n        \\\"Delta_pert = f(target gene features, network neighbourhood, control expression).\\\",\\n        \\\"Perturbations targeting genes in the same complex/module produce correlated deltas.\\\",\\n        \\\"Effect magnitude scales with target expression and network centrality.\\\",\\n        \\\"Gene-level deltas are approximately additive in log space for weak perturbations.\\\"\\n      ],\\n      \\\"constraints\\\": [\\n        \\\"No held-out perturbation cells may be used at any stage, including feature construction.\\\",\\n        \\\"Control mean must be computed from control cells in the provided file.\\\",\\n        \\\"Predictions must be finite, non-negative after inverse transform considerations, and on log1p CP10K scale.\\\",\\n        \\\"Computational budget must allow full training on the provided AnnData without external downloads.\\\"\\n      ],\\n      \\\"validation_requirements\\\": [\\n        \\\"Held-out-perturbation cross-validation within the training file (simulate unseen perturbations by leaving out entire perturbation labels).\\\",\\n        \\\"Baseline comparisons: control mean, target-only knockdown, and simple linear models.\\\",\\n        \\\"Bootstrap stability of top-20 DE gene predictions.\\\",\\n        \\\"Biological sanity checks against known K562 CRISPRi hits and pathway annotations.\\\"\\n      ]\\n    },\\n    \\\"scope\\\": {\\n      \\\"biological_scope\\\": \\\"Unseen-perturbation prediction of mean transcriptional response to CRISPRi knockdown in a single cancer cell line (K562). Focus on gene-level mean shifts, not cell-state distributions or guide-level heterogeneity.\\\",\\n      \\\"technical_scope\\\": \\\"Train only on cellforge_input.h5ad; build gene and perturbation feature representations from training data; predict held-out perturbation mean vectors; output predictions.npz in the required format.\\\",\\n      \\\"limitations\\\": [\\n        \\\"Cannot model guide-specific knockdown efficiency for unseen perturbations.\\\",\\n        \\\"Cannot capture perturbation-specific cell-state composition changes beyond mean effects.\\\",\\n        \\\"Limited by the coverage and quality of observed perturbations in the training file.\\\",\\n        \\\"No external screen or literature-derived effect sizes may be used.\\\",\\n        \\\"Linear/additive assumptions may miss strong non-linear or synthetic interactions.\\\"\\n      ],\\n      \\\"assumptions\\\": [\\n        \\\"Control cells in the file are representative of the unperturbed K562 baseline.\\\",\\n        \\\"Gene order and normalisation are consistent between training and evaluation.\\\",\\n        \\\"Held-out perturbations share biological context (K562, CRISPRi) with training perturbations.\\\",\\n        \\\"Mean delta is the primary quantity of interest; higher moments are not scored.\\\",\\n        \\\"Gene features derived from training expression and co-expression are transferable to unseen perturbations.\\\"\\n      ],\\n      \\\"biological_validation\\\": \\\"Validate predicted deltas against known K562 CRISPRi biology: target-gene knockdown, enrichment of regulon members, essential-gene effects, and concordance with co-perturbation clusters. Use held-out-perturbation cross-validation as the primary internal proxy for the official evaluator.\\\"\\n    }\\n  },\\n  \\\"key_challenges\\\": {\\n    \\\"biological\\\": {\\n      \\\"challenges\\\": [\\n        \\\"Unseen perturbations have no cells, so no direct supervision of their effects.\\\",\\n        \\\"CRISPRi knockdown strength varies by target and guide and is unobserved.\\\",\\n        \\\"K562 has strong copy-number heterogeneity and cell-cycle structure that confound mean effects.\\\",\\n        \\\"Perturbation effects can be non-linear and context-dependent (e.g., essential genes).\\\",\\n        \\\"Many perturbations have weak or near-zero transcriptional effects, making delta prediction noisy.\\\"\\n      ],\\n      \\\"impact\\\": \\\"Naive models collapse to control mean and yield near-zero pearson_delta; over-predicting large effects harms MSE and top-20 metrics.\\\",\\n      \\\"mitigation\\\": [\\n        \\\"Use gene-level features (expression, co-expression, network centrality, essentiality proxies) to transfer information to unseen targets.\\\",\\n        \\\"Shrink predicted deltas toward zero with regularisation tuned by held-out-perturbation CV.\\\",\\n        \\\"Model effect magnitude as a function of target expression and network degree.\\\",\\n        \\\"Use robust control mean estimation and per-gene variance weighting.\\\"\\n      ],\\n      \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Leave-one-perturbation-out CV on observed perturbations, stratified by effect size, with pearson_delta and top-20 metrics computed identically to the official evaluator.\\\",\\n      \\\"biological_considerations\\\": \\\"Preserve the biology that CRISPRi primarily represses the target and its regulon; enforce non-positive direct-target effects where appropriate and allow downstream positive/negative changes.\\\"\\n    },\\n    \\\"technical\\\": {\\n      \\\"challenges\\\": [\\n        \\\"High-dimensional output (n_genes per perturbation) with limited number of observed perturbations.\\\",\\n        \\\"Sparse, zero-inflated single-cell data and log1p CP10K scale.\\\",\\n        \\\"Uneven cell numbers per perturbation and potential batch effects.\\\",\\n        \\\"Strict requirement to match gene order and normalisation exactly.\\\",\\n        \\\"No external data allowed, limiting pretrained transfer.\\\"\\n      ],\\n      \\\"impact\\\": \\\"Overfitting to observed perturbations, unstable predictions, and format errors that invalidate submission.\\\",\\n      \\\"mitigation\\\": [\\n        \\\"Low-rank / factor models and ridge-regularised regression from perturbation features to delta vectors.\\\",\\n        \\\"Dimensionality reduction (PCA, NMF, gene modules) before regression.\\\",\\n        \\\"Explicit format checks: perts order equals heldout list, means shape and dtype float, gene order equals var_names.\\\",\\n        \\\"Deterministic seeding and unit tests for preprocessing and output.\\\"\\n      ],\\n      \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Unit tests on output schema; CV metrics; sanity check that predicted control-mean baseline reproduces input control mean.\\\",\\n      \\\"implementation_requirements\\\": \\\"Python with anndata, scanpy, numpy, scipy, scikit-learn, pandas; CPU-feasible pipeline; fixed random seeds; logged versions.\\\"\\n    },\\n    \\\"data_quality\\\": {\\n      \\\"issues\\\": [\\n        \\\"Low and variable library sizes per cell.\\\",\\n        \\\"Zero inflation and dropout in log1p CP10K data.\\\",\\n        \\\"Potential doublets and low-quality cells.\\\",\\n        \\\"Batch/experiment structure in obs.\\\",\\n        \\\"Held-out perturbations may be enriched for weak or essential genes.\\\"\\n      ],\\n      \\\"impact\\\": \\\"Noisy control mean and noisy per-perturbation means in training, biasing feature learning and calibration.\\\",\\n      \\\"mitigation\\\": [\\n        \\\"Standard QC: filter low-count cells and rarely detected genes only if consistent with var order requirements (keep full gene set for output).\\\",\\n        \\\"Robust control mean via trimmed mean or median-of-means.\\\",\\n        \\\"Weight genes by detection rate and variance in loss/metrics.\\\",\\n        \\\"Batch-aware feature construction or batch as covariate in regression.\\\"\\n      ],\\n      \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Compare control mean estimates across batches and QC thresholds; bootstrap stability of gene features and predictions.\\\",\\n      \\\"biological_validation\\\": \\\"Check that top expressed genes and control mean match expected K562 markers (e.g., haemoglobin genes, BCR-ABL programme).\\\"\\n    },\\n    \\\"computational\\\": {\\n      \\\"challenges\\\": [\\n        \\\"Full gene set output requires predicting thousands of genes per perturbation.\\\",\\n        \\\"Cross-validation over perturbations multiplies training cost.\\\",\\n        \\\"Memory for dense cell x gene matrices and delta matrices.\\\",\\n        \\\"Hyperparameter tuning under strict no-leakage constraints.\\\"\\n      ],\\n      \\\"impact\\\": \\\"Long runtimes and risk of leakage if tuning uses held-out perturbations improperly.\\\",\\n      \\\"mitigation\\\": [\\n        \\\"Work in reduced gene space (PCA/modules) for modelling and project back to full gene space.\\\",\\n        \\\"Use efficient linear algebra (ridge regression, SVD) and sparse representations where possible.\\\",\\n        \\\"Nested CV: inner loop for hyperparameters, outer loop for evaluation.\\\",\\n        \\\"Cache control mean and feature matrices.\\\"\\n      ],\\n      \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Profile runtime and memory; verify no held-out perturbation cells enter any fit.\\\",\\n      \\\"resource_requirements\\\": \\\"Single workstation with >=32 GB RAM, multi-core CPU; optional GPU not required; total runtime target under a few hours.\\\"\\n    },\\n    \\\"interpretability\\\": {\\n      \\\"requirements\\\": [\\n        \\\"Predicted deltas should be attributable to target-gene features and network neighbourhood.\\\",\\n        \\\"Top-20 DE genes should be biologically plausible for each perturbation.\\\",\\n        \\\"Model should expose gene-module loadings and perturbation feature importances.\\\"\\n      ],\\n      \\\"challenges\\\": [\\n        \\\"Low-rank models can be opaque.\\\",\\n        \\\"Unseen perturbations lack direct validation of mechanism.\\\",\\n        \\\"Separating direct target knockdown from downstream effects.\\\"\\n      ],\\n      \\\"solutions\\\": [\\n        \\\"Use linear models with gene-module loadings and perturbation feature coefficients.\\\",\\n        \\\"Report per-perturbation top modules and enriched pathways.\\\",\\n        \\\"Explicitly model target-gene self-knockdown as a feature.\\\",\\n        \\\"Post-hoc enrichment analysis of predicted DE genes.\\\"\\n      ],\\n      \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Check that predicted target knockdown appears for expressed targets and that top modules match known regulons.\\\",\\n      \\\"biological_validation\\\": \\\"Compare predicted DE gene sets with known K562 CRISPRi hits and pathway annotations; verify coherence of co-perturbation clusters.\\\"\\n    }\\n  },\\n  \\\"research_questions\\\": {\\n    \\\"primary\\\": {\\n      \\\"questions\\\": [\\n        \\\"Can gene-level features and observed perturbation effects in K562 CRISPRi data predict the mean transcriptional response of entirely unseen perturbations?\\\",\\n        \\\"Which feature representation (expression, co-expression modules, network centrality, essentiality proxies) best transfers to unseen CRISPRi targets?\\\",\\n        \\\"How much shrinkage toward control mean is optimal for maximising pearson_delta while controlling MSE and top-20 DE errors?\\\"\\n      ],\\n      \\\"hypotheses\\\": [\\n        \\\"A regularised low-rank model mapping perturbation features to delta vectors outperforms control-mean and target-only baselines on unseen perturbations.\\\",\\n        \\\"Effect magnitude is predictable from target expression and network degree, enabling calibrated shrinkage.\\\",\\n        \\\"Top-20 DE genes are recoverable above chance using module-level features even when full-vector Pearson is modest.\\\"\\n      ],\\n      \\\"biological_significance\\\": \\\"Establishes whether transcriptional consequences of CRISPRi knockdown are predictable from baseline K562 network structure, informing functional genomics and perturbation modelling.\\\",\\n      \\\"validation_approach\\\": \\\"Leave-one-perturbation-out CV on observed perturbations with official-style metrics; baseline comparisons; bootstrap stability.\\\",\\n      \\\"expected_outcomes\\\": [\\n        \\\"Positive mean pearson_delta on held-out perturbations.\\\",\\n        \\\"Improved top-20 DE PCC/R2 over control-mean baseline.\\\",\\n        \\\"Identifiable gene modules driving predictions.\\\"\\n      ]\\n    },\\n    \\\"secondary\\\": {\\n      \\\"questions\\\": [\\n        \\\"Do perturbations targeting genes in the same complex or pathway yield correlated predicted deltas?\\\",\\n        \\\"How does prediction difficulty vary with target expression, essentiality, and network centrality?\\\",\\n        \\\"Can predicted deltas recover known K562 regulons and essential-gene signatures?\\\"\\n      ],\\n      \\\"hypotheses\\\": [\\n        \\\"Module-based features capture co-perturbation correlation structure.\\\",\\n        \\\"Weakly expressed targets are harder to predict and require stronger shrinkage.\\\",\\n        \\\"Predicted top DE genes are enriched for known pathway members.\\\"\\n      ],\\n      \\\"biological_significance\\\": \\\"Links prediction performance to biological properties of targets, guiding interpretation and future experimental design.\\\",\\n      \\\"validation_approach\\\": \\\"Stratified CV by target expression/centrality; enrichment analysis of predicted DE genes; clustering of predicted deltas.\\\",\\n      \\\"expected_outcomes\\\": [\\n        \\\"Performance gradients across biological strata.\\\",\\n        \\\"Enrichment of known regulons in predicted top DE genes.\\\",\\n        \\\"Coherent clusters of predicted responses for related targets.\\\"\\n      ]\\n    },\\n    \\\"biological_mechanisms\\\": {\\n      \\\"mechanisms\\\": [\\n        \\\"Direct CRISPRi knockdown of the target transcript.\\\",\\n        \\\"Downstream regulon repression via transcription factor targets.\\\",\\n        \\\"Compensatory and stress responses (e.g., ribosome biogenesis, proteostasis).\\\",\\n        \\\"Cell-cycle and copy-number-associated expression shifts.\\\"\\n      ],\\n      \\\"investigation_approach\\\": \\\"Decompose predicted deltas into target-gene component, module components, and residual; relate modules to known pathways.\\\",\\n      \\\"validation_methods\\\": [\\n        \\\"Target-gene sign/magnitude checks.\\\",\\n        \\\"Module enrichment (MSigDB/GO) of top predicted DE genes.\\\",\\n        \\\"Comparison with observed deltas of related perturbations in training.\\\"\\n      ],\\n      \\\"expected_insights\\\": [\\n        \\\"Relative contribution of direct vs downstream effects.\\\",\\n        \\\"Which modules are most predictable across perturbations.\\\",\\n        \\\"Biological basis of prediction errors.\\\"\\n      ],\\n      \\\"biological_validation\\\": \\\"Confirm that predicted mechanisms align with known K562 biology and CRISPRi directionality (predominant repression).\\\"\\n    },\\n    \\\"experimental_validation\\\": {\\n      \\\"requirements\\\": [\\n        \\\"Held-out-perturbation CV as internal validation.\\\",\\n        \\\"External wet-lab validation optional but desirable for a subset of held-out perturbations.\\\",\\n        \\\"Controls: non-targeting guides, control mean, and target-only baseline.\\\"\\n      ],\\n      \\\"methods\\\": [\\n        \\\"Internal: leave-one-perturbation-out CV with official metrics.\\\",\\n        \\\"External: CRISPRi knockdown followed by scRNA-seq or targeted qPCR for selected held-out targets.\\\",\\n        \\\"Enrichment and pathway analysis of predicted DE genes.\\\"\\n      ],\\n      \\\"controls\\\": [\\n        \\\"Non-targeting control cells from the training file.\\\",\\n        \\\"Permutation of perturbation labels to estimate null performance.\\\",\\n        \\\"Random gene-set predictions as negative control.\\\"\\n      ],\\n      \\\"metrics\\\": [\\n        \\\"pearson_delta, MSE, top-20 DE MSE/PCC/R2.\\\",\\n        \\\"Target knockdown accuracy.\\\",\\n        \\\"Enrichment significance of predicted regulons.\\\"\\n      ],\\n      \\\"biological_validation\\\": \\\"Wet-lab confirmation of target knockdown and a subset of predicted downstream genes for selected held-out perturbations would provide direct biological validation.\\\"\\n    }\\n  },\\n  \\\"analysis_methods\\\": {\\n    \\\"computational_approaches\\\": {\\n      \\\"methods\\\": [\\n        \\\"Control mean estimation from control cells (robust trimmed mean) and per-gene variance.\\\",\\n        \\\"Perturbation feature construction: target expression, detection rate, co-expression module loadings, network centrality, essentiality proxies, gene ontology/pathway membership.\\\",\\n        \\\"Dimensionality reduction of delta space via PCA/SVD or NMF on observed perturbation mean deltas.\\\",\\n        \\\"Regularised regression (ridge, elastic net) or low-rank factor model mapping perturbation features to delta components.\\\",\\n        \\\"Shrinkage calibration of predicted deltas tuned by held-out-perturbation CV.\\\",\\n        \\\"Optional non-linear model (gradient boosting or small MLP) on module-level features with strong regularisation.\\\",\\n        \\\"Projection back to full gene space using module loadings and control mean.\\\"\\n      ],\\n      \\\"rationale\\\": \\\"The number of observed perturbations is modest relative to gene dimension; low-rank and regularised models transfer gene-level structure to unseen targets while controlling overfitting. Feature-based mapping allows prediction for targets with no cells.\\\",\\n      \\\"implementation\\\": \\\"Python pipeline: load AnnData, compute control mean, aggregate per-perturbation means and deltas, build gene modules (PCA/NMF), construct perturbation features, fit ridge/factor model with nested CV, calibrate shrinkage, project to full gene space, write predictions.npz.\\\",\\n      \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Leave-one-perturbation-out CV with official metrics; baseline comparisons; bootstrap stability; permutation null.\\\",\\n      \\\"biological_validation\\\": \\\"Check target knockdown, regulon enrichment, and coherence with known K562 biology.\\\"\\n    },\\n    \\\"validation_strategies\\\": {\\n      \\\"strategies\\\": [\\n        \\\"Leave-one-perturbation-out cross-validation on observed perturbations.\\\",\\n        \\\"Stratified CV by effect size, target expression, and network centrality.\\\",\\n        \\\"Baseline\",\n  \"error\": \"Failed to parse JSON response\"\n}\n\nBaseline Assessment:\n{\n  \"content\": \"{\\n  \\\"baseline_models\\\": {\\n    \\\"literature_review\\\": {\\n      \\\"existing_methods\\\": {\\n        \\\"methods\\\": [\\n          \\\"Linear/additive baseline: per-gene linear regression of perturbation mean on one-hot perturbation identity, trained only on training perturbations; predicts held-out perturbations via gene-wise intercept/mean (control mean)\\\",\\n          \\\"Latent linear models: PCA on perturbation means and ridge regression from perturbation descriptors (if available) or nearest-neighbor transfer in latent space\\\",\\n          \\\"Nearest-neighbor baseline: match held-out perturbations to training perturbations using gene-level features (e.g., control-centered mean profiles) and transfer the closest training perturbation effect\\\",\\n          \\\"Autoencoder/latent-space models: train a VAE or AE on single-cell expression, then model perturbation effects in latent space with simple regressors\\\",\\n          \\\"Graph/network propagation baselines: build a gene co-expression or PPI graph and propagate known perturbation effects to unseen perturbations using network similarity\\\",\\n          \\\"Set-transformer/attention models: treat perturbation as a token and predict gene-wise deltas via cross-attention over training perturbations\\\",\\n          \\\"Simple delta-prediction baselines: predict zero delta (control mean) and global mean delta as sanity baselines\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"strengths\\\": [\\n          \\\"Simple baselines (control mean, global delta) are robust, fast, and establish a lower bound for Pearson delta and MSE\\\",\\n          \\\"Linear/latent models are interpretable and computationally cheap, suitable for high-dimensional gene space\\\",\\n          \\\"Nearest-neighbor methods can capture similarity between perturbations if informative features exist\\\",\\n          \\\"Graph-based methods incorporate biological prior knowledge and may generalize to unseen perturbations with similar network neighborhoods\\\",\\n          \\\"Attention/set models can in principle learn complex perturbation-gene interactions and scale to many perturbations\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"limitations\\\": [\\n          \\\"No major perturbation-prediction methods were identified in the retrieved literature, so the benchmark relies on generic baselines and must be constructed carefully\\\",\\n          \\\"Linear models cannot capture nonlinear or combinatorial perturbation effects\\\",\\n          \\\"Nearest-neighbor methods depend heavily on the availability of informative perturbation descriptors; in CRISPRi K562, held-out perturbations may have no close training neighbors\\\",\\n          \\\"Graph propagation requires high-quality networks and may over-smooth effects\\\",\\n          \\\"Deep generative models require large training data and are prone to overfitting and poor calibration on unseen perturbations\\\",\\n          \\\"Most methods struggle with unseen-perturbation generalization because perturbation effects are highly gene- and context-specific\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"biological_relevance\\\": \\\"CRISPRi perturbations in K562 target specific genes; prediction should capture downstream transcriptional consequences. Baselines that shrink toward control mean are biologically conservative but may miss real effects. Graph/network methods can leverage known regulatory relationships, but validation must focus on top DE genes and pathway-level effects rather than global correlation alone.\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"model_comparison\\\": {\\n        \\\"comparison_criteria\\\": [\\n          \\\"Pearson correlation of predicted vs. true delta from control per perturbation (primary)\\\",\\n          \\\"MSE on mean expression and on delta\\\",\\n          \\\"Top-20 DE gene recovery: MSE, PCC, R2 on top-20 DE genes\\\",\\n          \\\"Computational cost and scalability to ~10k genes and hundreds of perturbations\\\",\\n          \\\"Robustness to biological and technical variability (batch, cell-cycle, library size)\\\",\\n          \\\"Biological interpretability: whether predicted effects align with known pathways and gene function\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"performance_metrics\\\": [\\n          \\\"pearson_delta (per perturbation, then averaged)\\\",\\n          \\\"MSE (global and per perturbation)\\\",\\n          \\\"Top-20 DE MSE, PCC, R2\\\",\\n          \\\"Rank correlation of gene effects\\\",\\n          \\\"Precision/recall of top-k DE genes\\\",\\n          \\\"Calibration of predicted effect sizes\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"biological_interpretability\\\": \\\"Linear and nearest-neighbor models are most interpretable (effect = weighted sum of training effects or direct transfer). Graph methods provide pathway-level interpretation. Deep models are less interpretable unless attention or gene-level attributions are used. For benchmarking, prioritize methods whose predictions can be traced to biological mechanisms.\\\",\\n        \\\"computational_requirements\\\": \\\"Control-mean and global-delta baselines require negligible computation. Ridge/PCA and nearest-neighbor methods scale to thousands of genes and perturbations with standard CPU resources. Graph propagation may require network preprocessing but remains feasible. Deep attention/VAE models require GPU training and careful hyperparameter tuning; given the small number of perturbations, they risk overfitting and are not recommended as first-line baselines.\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"recommendations\\\": {\\n        \\\"model_selection\\\": [\\n          \\\"Start with control-mean (zero delta) and global-mean-delta baselines as mandatory sanity checks\\\",\\n          \\\"Implement ridge regression on perturbation means with gene-wise shrinkage as a strong linear baseline\\\",\\n          \\\"Implement nearest-neighbor transfer in a reduced latent space (PCA on training perturbation means) as a nonparametric baseline\\\",\\n          \\\"If perturbation descriptors (e.g., target gene embeddings, PPI features) are available, implement a descriptor-based ridge or kernel regression model\\\",\\n          \\\"Only then consider deep models (set transformer, VAE) if sufficient training perturbations exist and validation shows gains over linear baselines\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"implementation_priority\\\": [\\n          \\\"1. Reproduce the evaluator exactly: compute control mean from training cells, define delta as perturbation mean minus control mean, and evaluate on held-out perturbations using pearson_delta, MSE, and top-20 DE metrics\\\",\\n          \\\"2. Build a robust preprocessing pipeline: filter genes consistently, use log1p(CP10k) normalization, and ensure gene order matches cellforge_input.h5ad\\\",\\n          \\\"3. Implement and tune linear/ridge baselines with cross-validation over training perturbations\\\",\\n          \\\"4. Add nearest-neighbor and descriptor-based baselines\\\",\\n          \\\"5. Evaluate deep models only if they beat linear baselines in cross-validation\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"biological_validation\\\": \\\"Validate predictions by checking enrichment of known K562 CRISPRi effects (e.g., knockdown of essential genes, pathway members) in top predicted DE genes. Compare predicted deltas for held-out perturbations to known gene-function relationships where available. Use pathway enrichment and gene-set enrichment analysis as secondary biological checks.\\\"\\n      }\\n    },\\n    \\\"evaluation_framework\\\": {\\n      \\\"metrics\\\": {\\n        \\\"primary_metrics\\\": [\\n          \\\"pearson_delta: Pearson correlation between predicted and true delta from control, per held-out perturbation, averaged across perturbations\\\",\\n          \\\"MSE on mean expression (n_perts x n_genes)\\\",\\n          \\\"Top-20 DE gene MSE, PCC, and R2 (computed per perturbation and averaged)\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"secondary_metrics\\\": [\\n          \\\"MSE on delta (perturbation mean minus control mean)\\\",\\n          \\\"Spearman rank correlation of gene effects\\\",\\n          \\\"Precision@k and recall@k for top-k DE genes (k=20, 50, 100)\\\",\\n          \\\"Calibration error: slope/intercept of predicted vs. true delta\\\",\\n          \\\"Per-gene correlation across perturbations\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"biological_metrics\\\": [\\n          \\\"Pathway/gene-set enrichment of top predicted DE genes vs. known CRISPRi targets\\\",\\n          \\\"Recovery of known downstream targets of the perturbed gene\\\",\\n          \\\"Directional consistency of predicted effects for known activating/repressing relationships\\\",\\n          \\\"Network proximity of predicted DE genes to the perturbed gene in a PPI/regulatory network\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"validation_metrics\\\": [\\n          \\\"Cross-validation over training perturbations: hold out a subset of training perturbations and compute the same metrics\\\",\\n          \\\"Bootstrap confidence intervals for pearson_delta and top-20 DE metrics\\\",\\n          \\\"Sensitivity to normalization and gene filtering choices\\\",\\n          \\\"Robustness to control-cell subsampling and batch effects\\\"\\n        ]\\n      },\\n      \\\"validation_strategy\\\": {\\n        \\\"cross_validation\\\": \\\"Use leave-one-perturbation-out or k-fold cross-validation over the training perturbations to tune model hyperparameters and estimate generalization. Because held-out perturbations are entirely unseen, cross-validation must mimic this setting: never use the held-out perturbation's cells or mean in training. Report mean and standard deviation of metrics across folds.\\\",\\n        \\\"biological_validation\\\": \\\"For each held-out perturbation, compare predicted top DE genes to known biology of the targeted gene (e.g., if the target is a transcription factor, check enrichment of its known targets). Use independent pathway databases (MSigDB, Reactome) and, where possible, external CRISPRi screens in K562 as a sanity check, but do not use external data for training.\\\",\\n        \\\"independent_testing\\\": \\\"The provided held-out_perturbations.json is the primary independent test set. Additionally, create an internal held-out set from training perturbations (e.g., 20% of perturbations) to estimate performance before final submission. Ensure no data leakage: control mean must be computed only from training control cells, and gene filtering must be consistent across train and test.\\\",\\n        \\\"robustness_testing\\\": \\\"Evaluate model sensitivity to: (1) control-cell subsampling (bootstrap control mean), (2) gene filtering thresholds, (3) normalization choices (CP10k vs. library-size normalization), (4) random seeds for stochastic models, and (5) batch/plate effects if metadata are available. Report variance of metrics across these perturbations.\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"benchmarks\\\": {\\n        \\\"baseline_models\\\": [\\n          \\\"Control mean (zero delta)\\\",\\n          \\\"Global mean delta (average delta across all training perturbations)\\\",\\n          \\\"Gene-wise linear regression on perturbation one-hot encoding (predicts training mean, reduces to control mean for unseen)\\\",\\n          \\\"Ridge regression on perturbation means with gene-wise shrinkage\\\",\\n          \\\"Nearest-neighbor transfer in PCA space of training perturbation means\\\",\\n          \\\"Descriptor-based ridge regression if perturbation features are available\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"state_of_the_art\\\": [\\n          \\\"No state-of-the-art perturbation-prediction methods were identified in the retrieved literature; the benchmark should be constructed from the baselines above and any recent general-purpose methods (e.g., linear mixed models, matrix factorization) applied to this dataset\\\",\\n          \\\"If external methods are considered, they must be retrained only on cellforge_input.h5ad to respect the task constraints\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"biological_benchmarks\\\": [\\n          \\\"Known CRISPRi knockdown effects in K562 for housekeeping/essential genes\\\",\\n          \\\"Pathway-level enrichment of predicted DE genes for held-out perturbations\\\",\\n          \\\"Consistency with known gene regulatory relationships (e.g., transcription factor targets)\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"performance_targets\\\": [\\n          \\\"Beat control-mean baseline on pearson_delta (i.e., positive correlation on average)\\\",\\n          \\\"Achieve top-20 DE PCC significantly above zero and above the global-delta baseline\\\",\\n          \\\"Maintain MSE no worse than the control-mean baseline (since predicting zero delta minimizes MSE if effects are small)\\\",\\n          \\\"Demonstrate stable performance across cross-validation folds (low variance)\\\"\\n        ]\\n      }\\n    },\\n    \\\"performance_analysis\\\": {\\n      \\\"model_performance\\\": {\\n        \\\"accuracy_metrics\\\": [\\n          \\\"Expected: control-mean baseline yields pearson_delta near zero (undefined or zero) and low MSE; global-delta baseline may yield slightly positive pearson_delta if perturbations share common effects\\\",\\n          \\\"Linear/ridge models may achieve modest positive pearson_delta if perturbation effects are additive and training perturbations are representative\\\",\\n          \\\"Nearest-neighbor methods may outperform linear models if held-out perturbations have close training neighbors, but likely fail for distinct perturbations\\\",\\n          \\\"Top-20 DE metrics are expected to be challenging; even small positive PCC indicates some signal\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"biological_relevance\\\": \\\"Models that predict zero delta are biologically conservative but miss real effects. Models that capture shared stress/global responses may achieve higher pearson_delta but may not recover perturbation-specific DE genes. Biological relevance should be assessed by whether predicted DE genes are enriched for the perturbed gene's known pathway.\\\",\\n        \\\"generalization\\\": \\\"Generalization to unseen perturbations is the core challenge. Performance will likely be low for perturbations with no close training analogs. Cross-validation over training perturbations will overestimate performance if perturbations are clustered; use leave-cluster-out or leave-one-out to better mimic unseen setting.\\\",\\n        \\\"robustness\\\": \\\"Performance is expected to be sensitive to control-mean estimation (cell numbers), gene filtering, and normalization. Deep models may be less robust due to overfitting. Linear models with shrinkage are more stable.\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"comparative_analysis\\\": {\\n        \\\"baseline_comparison\\\": \\\"Control-mean and global-delta baselines are essential reference points. Any model that does not beat them on pearson_delta and top-20 DE metrics provides no added value. Ridge regression typically improves over ordinary least squares by stabilizing gene-wise estimates. Nearest-neighbor methods can outperform linear models when perturbation effects are locally similar in latent space.\\\",\\n        \\\"state_of_the_art_comparison\\\": \\\"Without identified state-of-the-art methods, comparison is against the constructed baselines. If deep models are implemented, they must be compared under identical preprocessing and evaluation; given limited training perturbations, they are unlikely to outperform well-tuned linear baselines.\\\",\\n        \\\"biological_comparison\\\": \\\"Biological comparison should focus on pathway-level recovery rather than global correlation. A model with lower pearson_delta but better recovery of known targets may be more biologically useful. Report both statistical and biological metrics.