es preserve known perturbation effects (e.g., target knockdown) in training data.\\\",\\n        \\\"decision_support_mitigation\\\": \\\"No decision support constraints are provided. Mitigation strategies should be validated empirically rather than assumed beneficial.\\\"\\n      }\\n    },\\n    \\\"questions\\\": {\\n      \\\"formulation\\\": {\\n        \\\"assessment\\\": \\\"The research questions are well-formulated and address the core problem: predictability of unseen perturbations, feature representation, and shrinkage calibration. However, the questions are not explicitly tied to the evaluation metrics.\\\",\\n        \\\"issues\\\": [\\n          \\\"The primary questions do not specify how success will be measured (e.g., pearson_delta, top-20 DE metrics).\\\",\\n          \\\"The secondary questions about co-perturbation correlation and performance gradients are not operationalized.\\\",\\n          \\\"The questions about biological mechanisms are broad and may not be answerable within the task constraints.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"improvements\\\": [\\n          \\\"Reframe primary questions to explicitly target the evaluation metrics: e.g., 'Can feature-based models achieve positive pearson_delta on held-out perturbations?'\\\",\\n          \\\"Operationalize secondary questions: e.g., 'Do perturbations targeting genes in the same complex yield correlated predicted deltas, as measured by correlation of predicted delta vectors\",\n  \"parse_error\": \"Failed to parse JSON response\"\n}\n\nRequirements:\n1. Keep the same high-level structure when possible.\n2. Resolve issues called out in the comments.\n3. Add explicit assumptions where information is missing.\n4. Return only valid JSON (no markdown).\n"
