bor methods can capture similarity between perturbations if informative features exist\\\",\\n          \\\"Graph-based methods incorporate biological prior knowledge and may generalize to unseen perturbations with similar network neighborhoods\\\",\\n          \\\"Attention/set models can in principle learn complex perturbation-gene interactions and scale to many perturbations\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"limitations\\\": [\\n          \\\"No major perturbation-prediction methods were identified in the retrieved literature, so the benchmark relies on generic baselines and must be constructed carefully\\\",\\n          \\\"Linear models cannot capture nonlinear or combinatorial perturbation effects\\\",\\n          \\\"Nearest-neighbor methods depend heavily on the availability of informative perturbation descriptors; in CRISPRi K562, held-out perturbations may have no close training neighbors\\\",\\n          \\\"Graph propagation requires high-quality networks and may over-smooth effects\\\",\\n          \\\"Deep generative models require large training data and are prone to overfitting and poor calibration on unseen perturbations\\\",\\n          \\\"Most methods struggle with unseen-perturbation generalization because perturbation effects are highly gene- and context-specific\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"biological_relevance\\\": \\\"CRISPRi perturbations in K562 target specific genes; prediction should capture downstream transcriptional consequences. Baselines that shrink toward control mean are biologically conservative but may miss real effects. Graph/network methods can leverage known regulatory relationships, but validation must focus on top DE genes and pathway-level effects rather than global correlation alone.\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"model_comparison\\\": {\\n        \\\"comparison_criteria\\\": [\\n          \\\"Pearson correlation of predicted vs. true delta from control per perturbation (primary)\\\",\\n          \\\"MSE on mean expression and 