 means with gene-wise shrinkage as a strong linear baseline\\\",\\n          \\\"Implement nearest-neighbor transfer in a reduced latent space (PCA on training perturbation means) as a nonparametric baseline\\\",\\n          \\\"If perturbation descriptors (e.g., target gene embeddings, PPI features) are available, implement a descriptor-based ridge or kernel regression model\\\",\\n          \\\"Only then consider deep models (set transformer, VAE) if sufficient training perturbations exist and validation shows gains over linear baselines\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"implementation_priority\\\": [\\n          \\\"1. Reproduce the evaluator exactly: compute control mean from training cells, define delta as perturbation mean minus control mean, and evaluate on held-out perturbations using pearson_delta, MSE, and top-20 DE metrics\\\",\\n          \\\"2. Build a robust preprocessing pipeline: filter genes consistently, use log1p(CP10k) normalization, and ensure gene order matches cellforge_input.h5ad\\\",\\n          \\\"3. Implement and tune linear/ridge baselines with cross-validation over training perturbations\\\",\\n          \\\"4. Add nearest-neighbor and descriptor-based baselines\\\",\\n          \\\"5. Evaluate deep models only if they beat linear baselines in cross-validation\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"biological_validation\\\": \\\"Validate predictions by checking enrichment of known K562 CRISPRi effects (e.g., knockdown of essential genes, pathway members) in top predicted DE genes. Compare predicted deltas for held-out perturbations to known gene-function relationships where available. Use pathway enrichment and gene-set enrichment analysis as secondary biological checks.\\\"\\n      }\\n    },\\n    \\\"evaluation_framework\\\": {\\n      \\\"metrics\\\": {\\n        \\\"primary_metrics\\\": [\\n          \\\"pearson_delta: Pearson correlation between predicted and true delta from control, per held-out perturbation, averaged across perturbations\\\",\\n          \\\"MSE on mean expression (n_perts x n_genes)\\\",\\n          \\\"Top-20 DE gene MSE, PCC, and R2 (computed per perturbation and averaged)\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"secondary_metrics\\\": [\\n          \\\"MSE on delta (perturbation mean minus control mean)\\\",\\n          \\\"Spearman rank correlation of gene effects\\\",\\n          \\\"Precision@k and recall@k for top-k DE genes (k=20, 50, 100)\\\",\\n          \\\"Calibration error: slope/intercept of predicted vs. true delta\\\",\\n          \\\"Per-gene correlation across perturbations\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"biological_metrics\\\": [\\n          \\\"Pathway/gene-set enrichment of top predicted DE genes vs. known CRISPRi targets\\\",\\n          \\\"Recovery of known downstream targets of the perturbed gene\\\",\\n          \\\"Directional consistency of predicted effects for known activating/repressing relationships\\\",\\n          \\\"Network proximity of predicted DE genes to the perturbed gene in a PPI/regulatory network\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"validation_metrics\\\": [\\n          \\\"Cross-validation over training perturbations: hold out a subset of training perturbations and compute the same metrics\\\",\\n          \\\"Bootstrap confidence intervals for pearson_delta and top-20 DE metrics\\\",\\n          \\\"Sensitivity to normalization and gene filtering choices\\\",\\n          \\\"Robustness to control-cell subsampling and batch effects\\\"\\n        ]\\n      },\\n      \\\"validation_strategy\\\": {\\n        \\\"cross_validation\\\": \\\"Use leave-one-perturbation-out or k-fold cross-validation over the training perturbations to tune model hyperparameters and estimate generalization. Because held-out perturbations are entirely unseen, cross-validation must mimic this setting: never use the held-out perturbation's cells or mean in training. Report mean and standard deviation of metrics across folds.\\\",\\n        \\\"biological_validation\\\": \\\"For each held-out perturbation, compare predicted top DE genes to known biology of the targeted gene (e.g., if the target is a transcription factor, check enrichment of its known targets). Use independent pathway databases (MSigDB, Reactome) and, where possible, external CRISPRi screens in K562 as a sanity check, but do not use external data for training.\\\",\\n        \\\"independent_testing\\\": \\\"The provided held-out_perturbations.json is the primary independent test set. Additionally, create an internal held-out set from training perturbations (e.g., 20% of perturbations) to estimate performance before final submission. Ensure no data leakage: control mean must be computed only from training control cells, and gene filtering must be consistent across train and test.\\\",\\n        \\\"robustness_testing\\\": \\\"Evaluate model sensitivity to: (1) control-cell subsampling (bootstrap control mean), (2) gene filtering thresholds, (3) normalization choices (CP10k vs. library-size normalization), (4) random seeds for stochastic models, and (5) batch/plate effects if metadata are available. Report variance of metrics across these perturbations.\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"benchmarks\\\": {\\n        \\\"baseline_models\\\": [\\n          \\\"Control mean (zero delta)\\\",\\n          \\\"Global mean delta (average delta across all training perturbations)\\\",\\n          \\\"Gene-wise linear regression on perturbation one-hot encoding (predicts training mean, reduces to control mean for unseen)\\\",\\n          \\\"Ridge regression on perturbation means with gene-wise shrinkage\\\",\\n          \\\"Nearest-neighbor transfer in PCA space of training perturbation means\\\",\\n          \\\"Descriptor-based ridge regression if perturbation features are available\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"state_of_the_art\\\": [\\n          \\\"No state-of-the-art perturbation-prediction methods were identified in the retrieved literature; the benchmark should be constructed from the baselines above and any recent general-purpose methods (e.g., linear mixed models, matrix factorization) applied to this dataset\\\",\\n          \\\"If external methods are considered, they must be retrained only on cellforge_input.h5ad to respect the task constraints\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"biological_benchmarks\\\": [\\n          \\\"Known CRISPRi knockdown effects in K562 for housekeeping/essential genes\\\",\\n          \\\"Pathway-level enrichment of predicted DE genes for held-out perturbations\\\",\\n          \\\"Consistency with known gene regulatory relationships (e.g., transcription factor targets)\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"performance_targets\\\": [\\n          \\\"Beat control-mean baseline on pearson_delta (i.e., positive correlation on average)\\\",\\n          \\\"Achieve top-20 DE PCC significantly above zero and above the global-delta baseline\\\",\\n          \\\"Maintain MSE no worse than the control-mean baseline (since predicting zero delta minimizes MSE if effects are small)\\\",\\n          \\\"Demonstrate stable performance across cross-validation folds (low variance)\\\"\\n        ]\\n      }\\n    },\\n    \\\"performance_analysis\\\": {\\n      \\\"model_performance\\\": {\\n        \\\"accuracy_metrics\\\": [\\n          \\\"Expected: control-mean baseline yields pearson_delta near zero (undefined or zero) and low MSE; global-delta baseline may yield slightly positive pearson_delta if perturbations share common effects\\\",\\n          \\\"Linear/ridge models may achieve modest positive pearson_delta if perturbation effects are additive and training perturbations are representative\\\",\\n          \\\"Nearest-neighbor methods may outperform linear models if held-out perturbations have close training neighbors, but likely fail for distinct perturbations\\\",\\n          \\\"Top-20 DE metrics are expected to be challenging; even small positive PCC indicates some signal\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"biological_relevance\\\": \\\"Models that predict zero delta are biologically conservative but miss real effects. Models that capture shared stress/global responses may achieve higher pearson_delta but may not recover perturbation-specific DE genes. Biological relevance should be assessed by whether predicted DE genes are enriched for the perturbed gene's known pathway.\\\",\\n        \\\"generalization\\\": \\\"Generalization to unseen perturbations is the core challenge. Performance will likely be low for perturbations with no close training analogs. Cross-validation over training perturbations will overestimate performance if perturbations are clustered; use leave-cluster-out or leave-one-out to better mimic unseen setting.\\\",\\n        \\\"robustness\\\": \\\"Performance is expected to be sensitive to control-mean estimation (cell numbers), gene filtering, and normalization. Deep models may be less robust due to overfitting. Linear models with shrinkage are more stable.\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"comparative_analysis\\\": {\\n        \\\"baseline_comparison\\\": \\\"Control-mean and global-delta baselines are essential reference points. Any model that does not beat them on pearson_delta and top-20 DE metrics provides no added value. Ridge regression typically improves over ordinary least squares by stabilizing gene-wise estimates. Nearest-neighbor methods can outperform linear models when perturbation effects are locally similar in latent space.\\\",\\n        \\\"state_of_the_art_comparison\\\": \\\"Without identified state-of-the-art methods, comparison is against the constructed baselines. If deep models are implemented, they must be compared under identical preprocessing and evaluation; given limited training perturbations, they are unlikely to outperform well-tuned linear baselines.\\\",\\n        \\\"biological_comparison\\\": \\\"Biological comparison should focus on pathway-level recovery rather than global correlation. A model with lower pearson_delta but better recovery of known targets may be more biologically useful. Report both statistical and biological metrics.\\\",\\n        \\\"computational_comparison\\\": \\\"Linear and nearest-neighbor methods are orders of magnitude cheaper than deep models and easier to reproduce. Given the task's constraints (train only on provided data), computational efficiency favors simple models unless deep models show clear gains.\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"strengths_weaknesses\\\": {\\n        \\\"strengths\\\": [\\n          \\\"Simple baselines are reproducible, fast, and provide transparent lower bounds\\\",\\n          \\\"Linear/ridge models are interpretable and can capture additive perturbation effects\\\",\\n          \\\"Nearest-neighbor methods can leverage similarity between perturbations without parametric assumptions\\\",\\n          \\\"Evaluation framework covers both global (pearson_delta, MSE) and localized (top-20 DE) metrics, aligning with biological relevance\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"weaknesses\\\": [\\n          \\\"No major perturbation-prediction methods were identified in the literature, limiting benchmarking against state-of-the-art\\\",\\n          \\\"Unseen-perturbation prediction is inherently difficult; simple baselines may predict near-zero deltas and fail to capture specific effects\\\",\\n          \\\"Top-20 DE metrics are noisy and sensitive to gene filtering and normalization\\\",\\n          \\\"Lack of perturbation descriptors (e.g., target gene embeddings) limits the power of descriptor-based models\\\",\\n          \\\"Deep models risk overfitting given the limited number of perturbations\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"biological_insights\\\": [\\n          \\\"CRISPRi effects in K562 are likely dominated by knockdown of the target gene and its direct downstream targets; models should prioritize predicting these specific effects over global stress responses\\\",\\n          \\\"Many perturbations may have small effects, making MSE minimization favor zero-delta predictions; pearson_delta and top-20 DE metrics are more informative\\\",\\n          \\\"Pathway-level analysis is essential to distinguish biologically meaningful predictions from noise\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"technical_limitations\\\": [\\n          \\\"Control mean estimation uncertainty propagates to delta predictions\\\",\\n          \\\"Gene filtering and normalization choices can substantially affect metrics; must be fixed and documented\\\",\\n          \\\"Held-out perturbations may include genes with no training analogs, making generalization impossible without external knowledge\\\",\\n          \\\"Evaluation on mean expression only ignores cell-to-cell heterogeneity and distributional shifts\\\"\\n        ]\\n      }\\n    },\\n    \\\"improvement_suggestions\\\": {\\n      \\\"model_enhancements\\\": {\\n        \\\"architectural_improvements\\\": [\\n          \\\"Incorporate biological priors: gene-gene interaction networks, pathway membership, or transcription factor targets as regularization or graph structure\\\",\\n          \\\"Use low-rank matrix factorization of perturbation effects to share statistical strength across perturbations\\\",\\n          \\\"If target gene identities are available, use gene embeddings (e.g., from Gene2Vec or protein language models) as perturbation descriptors in a regression model\\\",\\n          \\\"Consider a hierarchical Bayesian model that shrinks perturbation-specific effects toward global effects based on similarity\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"training_optimizations\\\": [\\n          \\\"Use gene-wise shrinkage (ridge/elastic net) to stabilize estimates for high-dimensional gene space\\\",\\n          \\\"Weight genes by expression level or variance to focus on reliably measured genes\\\",\\n          \\\"Use robust loss functions (Huber) to reduce sensitivity to outlier cells\\\",\\n          \\\"Perform hyperparameter tuning via leave-one-perturbation-out cross-validation\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"biological_interpretability\\\": [\\n          \\\"Add pathway-level regularization or post-hoc enrichment analysis to ensure predicted DE genes are biologically coherent\\\",\\n          \\\"Report gene-level attributions for linear models and attention weights for deep models\\\",\\n          \\\"Compare predicted effects to known CRISPRi knockdown signatures where available\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"computational_efficiency\\\": [\\n          \\\"Prefer closed-form linear/ridge solutions over iterative deep learning given the data scale\\\",\\n          \\\"Use PCA or random projections to reduce gene dimensionality for nearest-neighbor methods\\\",\\n          \\\"Cache control mean and preprocessing steps to speed up evaluation\\\"\\n        ]\\n      },\\n      \\\"implementation_optimizations\\\": {\\n        \\\"code_optimizations\\\": [\\n          \\\"Vectorize delta computation and metric calculation across perturbations\\\",\\n          \\\"Use float32 for predictions to match evaluator expectations\\\",\\n          \\\"Ensure gene order and normalization exactly match cellforge_input.h5ad\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"pipeline_improvements\\\": [\\n          \\\"Build a reproducible pipeline: load h5ad, compute control mean from training cells only, train model on training perturbation means, predict held-out means, save predictions.npz with perts and means\\\",\\n          \\\"Include sanity checks: predicted means should be non-negative and on the same scale as training means\\\",\\n          \\\"Log all preprocessing choices and random seeds\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"validation_enhancements\\\": [\\n          \\\"Implement nested cross-validation: inner loop for hyperparameters, outer loop for performance estimation\\\",\\n          \\\"Use bootstrap resampling of cells to estimate uncertainty in control mean and metrics\\\",\\n          \\\"Evaluate on multiple gene filtering thresholds to assess robustness\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"deployment_considerations\\\": \\\"The final deliverable is a predictions.npz file; ensure it contains exactly the held-out perturbation keys in the order listed and a float array of shape (n_perts, n_genes). Validate file format and shape before submission. Since the task is a one-off prediction, prioritize reproducibility and correctness over deployment infrastructure.\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"future_work\\\": {\\n        \\\"research_directions\\\": [\\n          \\\"Develop perturbation-prediction methods that explicitly model unseen perturbations using target gene function and network context\\\",\\n          \\\"Explore transfer learning from large perturbation atlases (e.g., DepMap, Perturb-seq) while respecting data constraints\\\",\\n          \\\"Study the limits of predictability for unseen CRISPRi perturbations in K562 and identify which perturbations are predictable from sequence/network features\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"biological_validation\\\": [\\n          \\\"Validate predictions experimentally for a subset of held-out perturbations using targeted qPCR or reporter assays\\\",\\n          \\\"Compare predicted DE genes to independent CRISPRi screens in K562\\\",\\n          \\\"Perform pathway enrichment and network analysis to assess biological plausibility\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"clinical_translation\\\": [\\n          \\\"Although K562 is a leukemia cell line, perturbation-prediction methods could inform therapeutic target validation; however, clinical translation requires validation in primary cells and disease models\\\",\\n          \\\"Focus on predicting on-target effects and avoiding off-target predictions\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"technical_advancements\\\": [\\n          \\\"Integrate multi-omics data (chromatin accessibility, TF binding) to improve perturbation-effect prediction\\\",\\n          \\\"Develop evaluation metrics that account for cell-to-cell heterogeneity and distributional shifts,\",\n  \"error\": \"Failed to parse JSON response\"\n}\n\nREVIEWER COMMENTS:\n{\n  \"note\": \"{\\n  \\\"dataset\\\": {\\n    \\\"biological\\\": {\\n      \\\"relevance\\\": {\\n        \\\"assessment\\\": \\\"The dataset analysis correctly identifies the core biological context (K562 CRISPRi, unseen-perturbation prediction) and the need for gene-level features and control-anchored deltas. The emphasis on target-gene knockdown and pathway coherence is appropriate.\\\",\\n        \\\"issues\\\": [\\n          \\\"The analysis assumes a single endpoint and no dose-response, but CRISPRi knockdown strength varies by guide and target, which is acknowledged but not operationalized.\\\",\\n          \\\"The control definition (non-targeting vs. safe-targeting) is not confirmed from the actual obs metadata.\\\",\\n          \\\"Held-out perturbations may include essential genes or genes with no close training analogs, which is not explicitly addressed in the biological relevance assessment.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"improvements\\\": [\\n          \\\"Confirm and document the exact control label(s) in obs (e.g., 'control', 'NTC') and whether multiple control types exist.\\\",\\n          \\\"Stratify biological relevance by target gene properties (expression, essentiality, network centrality) to anticipate which held-out perturbations are predictable.\\\",\\n          \\\"Explicitly discuss the biological plausibility of predicting effects for essential genes, which may produce strong or lethal phenotypes not captured by mean expression alone.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Validate biological relevance by checking that predicted deltas for training perturbations recapitulate known target-gene knockdown and pathway enrichment. Use held-out-perturbation CV stratified by target expression and essentiality.\\\",\\n        \\\"biological_validation\\\": \\\"Compare predicted top DE genes for held-out perturbations to known K562 CRISPRi biology (e.g., regulon members, essential-gene signatures) and report enrichment significance.\\\",\\n        \\\"decision_support_alignment\\\": \\\"Decision support blocks are empty, so no model-selection or implementation constraints are provided. The biological relevance assessment should be re-evaluated once decision support specifies model class, evaluation strategy, and data preparation constraints.\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"interpretability\\\": {\\n        \\\"assessment\\\": \\\"The analysis recognizes the need for interpretable predictions (target knockdown, module loadings) but does not specify how interpretability will be quantified or reported.\\\",\\n        \\\"issues\\\": [\\n          \\\"Interpretability is discussed qualitatively but not tied to concrete metrics or outputs.\\\",\\n          \\\"The distinction between direct target knockdown and downstream effects is not operationalized.\\\",\\n          \\\"No plan for reporting gene-level attributions or pathway enrichments for held-out predictions.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"improvements\\\": [\\n          \\\"Define interpretability metrics: e.g., sign and magnitude of predicted target-gene delta, enrichment of known regulon members in top predicted DE genes, and module loading coherence.\\\",\\n          \\\"Plan to report per-perturbation top modules and enriched pathways for held-out predictions.\\\",\\n          \\\"Use linear models with gene-module loadings to enable direct interpretation of perturbation feature effects.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Validate interpretability by checking that predicted target knockdown appears for expressed targets and that top modules match known regulons. Use permutation tests to assess enrichment significance.\\\",\\n        \\\"biological_validation\\\": \\\"Confirm that predicted mechanisms align with known K562 biology and CRISPRi directionality (predominant repression).\\\",\\n        \\\"decision_support_considerations\\\": \\\"Empty decision support blocks provide no interpretability requirements. Interpretability should be prioritized if decision support later specifies biological validation or mechanistic insight as a goal.\\\"\\n      }\\n    },\\n    \\\"technical\\\": {\\n      \\\"quality\\\": {\\n        \\\"assessment\\\": \\\"The technical quality assessment is comprehensive, covering normalization state, QC metrics, batch effects, and guide confidence. However, it relies on assumptions about metadata that are not verified.\\\",\\n        \\\"issues\\\": [\\n          \\\"The analysis assumes the matrix is already CP10k + log1p but does not confirm this from the actual data (e.g., max values, integer-like structure).\\\",\\n          \\\"QC thresholds are suggested but not derived from the data distribution.\\\",\\n          \\\"Batch-perturbation confounding is flagged but not quantified.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"improvements\\\": [\\n          \\\"Load cellforge_input.h5ad and verify normalization state (e.g., max value, presence of non-integer values consistent with log1p).\\\",\\n          \\\"Compute QC metric distributions (total counts, genes detected, mitochondrial fraction if available) and set thresholds at data-driven tails (e.g., 1st/99th percentile or MAD-based).\\\",\\n          \\\"Quantify batch-perturbation confounding by cross-tabulating batch and perturbation labels and computing association statistics.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Validate technical quality by comparing control mean estimates across batches and QC thresholds, and by assessing held-out perturbation performance under different QC settings.\\\",\\n        \\\"technical_validation\\\": \\\"Confirm that the stored matrix is on the expected scale and that gene order matches var_names. Check that control cells are sufficient and well-matched for delta computation.\\\",\\n        \\\"decision_support_quality\\\": \\\"No decision support constraints are provided. QC thresholds should be data-driven and validated against held-out perturbation metrics rather than assumed.\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"reproducibility\\\": {\\n        \\\"assessment\\\": \\\"The analysis emphasizes deterministic pipelines and fixed seeds, but does not specify version control, environment capture, or unit tests for output format.\\\",\\n        \\\"issues\\\": [\\n          \\\"No explicit plan for logging software versions and random seeds.\\\",\\n          \\\"No unit tests for output schema (perts order, means shape, dtype, gene order).\\\",\\n          \\\"Reproducibility of control mean estimation under cell subsampling is not addressed.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"improvements\\\": [\\n          \\\"Log all software versions (Python, anndata, scanpy, numpy, scipy, scikit-learn) and random seeds.\\\",\\n          \\\"Implement unit tests that check perts order equals heldout list, means shape is (n_perts, n_genes), dtype is float, and gene order matches var_names.\\\",\\n          \\\"Use bootstrap resampling of control cells to estimate uncertainty in control mean and propagate to delta predictions.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Validate reproducibility by running the pipeline twice with the same seed and confirming identical outputs. Use unit tests to catch format errors before submission.\\\",\\n        \\\"technical_validation\\\": \\\"Check that predicted control-mean baseline reproduces input control mean within numerical tolerance.\\\",\\n        \\\"decision_support_reproducibility\\\": \\\"Empty decision support blocks provide no reproducibility requirements. Reproducibility should be treated as mandatory for submission validity.\\\"\\n      }\\n    },\\n    \\\"preprocessing\\\": {\\n      \\\"recommendations\\\": {\\n        \\\"assessment\\\": \\\"The preprocessing recommendations are sound: do not renormalize, compute control mean on the same scale, use pseudobulk/robust means, and validate batch correction. However, they are generic and not tailored to the actual metadata.\\\",\\n        \\\"issues\\\": [\\n          \\\"No specific QC thresholds or batch correction method is selected.\\\",\\n          \\\"The recommendation to optionally standardize features for modeling requires exact inversion, which is error-prone.\\\",\\n          \\\"Feature selection for modeling is suggested but not tied to a concrete plan.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"improvements\\\": [\\n          \\\"Specify concrete QC thresholds after inspecting the data distribution (e.g., filter cells with total counts below 1st percentile or genes detected below 200).\\\",\\n          \\\"Select a batch correction method (e.g., control-anchored regression or batch as covariate) and validate it on control cells before applying to all cells.\\\",\\n          \\\"If standardizing features for modeling, implement a robust inverse transform and validate that predicted means are on the original log1p CP10k scale.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Validate preprocessing by checking that control mean is stable across QC thresholds and that held-out perturbation performance does not degrade with batch correction.\\\",\\n        \\\"implementation\\\": \\\"Load h5ad with scanpy/anndata, verify normalization, compute control mean as the mean over control cells, train model to predict delta from control, add control mean back for final means.\\\",\\n        \\\"decision_support_preprocessing\\\": \\\"No decision support constraints are provided. Preprocessing choices should be validated against held-out perturbation metrics rather than assumed beneficial.\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"validation\\\": {\\n        \\\"assessment\\\": \\\"The validation plan for preprocessing is adequate but lacks specific tests for normalization state and gene order alignment.\\\",\\n        \\\"issues\\\": [\\n          \\\"No explicit test that the matrix is on log1p CP10k scale (e.g., checking max value or comparing to raw counts if available).\\\",\\n          \\\"No test that gene order in predictions matches var_names exactly.\\\",\\n          \\\"No assessment of control mean stability under cell subsampling.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"improvements\\\": [\\n          \\\"Implement a check that the maximum value in X is consistent with log1p CP10k (e.g., < 20) and that values are not integer-like.\\\",\\n          \\\"Implement a strict gene order check: assert that var_names equals the expected gene list and that predictions use the same order.\\\",\\n          \\\"Use bootstrap resampling of control cells to compute confidence intervals for control mean and assess stability.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Validate preprocessing by comparing control mean estimates across batches and QC thresholds, and by confirming that predicted control perturbation reproduces the control mean.\\\",\\n        \\\"implementation\\\": \\\"Include preprocessing validation as unit tests in the pipeline, run before model training.\\\",\\n        \\\"decision_support_validation\\\": \\\"Empty decision support blocks provide no validation requirements. Preprocessing validation should be treated as mandatory for submission validity.\\\"\\n      }\\n    },\\n    \\\"quality\\\": {\\n      \\\"assessment\\\": {\\n        \\\"evaluation\\\": \\\"The quality assessment covers biological and technical metrics but does not specify how to handle low-quality perturbations or cells in training.\\\",\\n        \\\"issues\\\": [\\n          \\\"No explicit criteria for excluding low-quality perturbations from training.\\\",\\n          \\\"The impact of QC filtering on perturbation-responsive cell states is not assessed.\\\",\\n          \\\"No plan for weighting training perturbations by cell count or guide confidence.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"improvements\\\": [\\n          \\\"Define criteria for excluding perturbations from training (e.g., < 20 cells or low guide concordance) and document the number excluded.\\\",\\n          \\\"Assess whether QC filtering disproportionately removes cells from certain perturbations or cell states.\\\",\\n          \\\"Weight training perturbations by cell count and guide confidence to reduce noise from low-support perturbations.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Validate quality assessment by comparing control mean and held-out perturbation performance under different QC and exclusion criteria.\\\",\\n        \\\"implementation\\\": \\\"Compute per-perturbation cell counts and guide concordance; flag low-confidence perturbations; use weights in model training.\\\",\\n        \\\"decision_support_quality_assessment\\\": \\\"No decision support constraints are provided. Quality thresholds should be data-driven and validated against held-out perturbation metrics.\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"validation\\\": {\\n        \\\"evaluation\\\": \\\"The quality validation plan is generic and lacks specific tests for guide assignment confidence and doublet removal.\\\",\\n        \\\"issues\\\": [\\n          \\\"No specific method for assessing guide assignment confidence or doublet scores.\\\",\\n          \\\"No plan for validating that QC does not remove perturbation signal.\\\",\\n          \\\"No assessment of batch-perturbation confounding after QC.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"improvements\\\": [\\n          \\\"Inspect distributions of guide assignment confidence and doublet scores; set thresholds based on data.\\\",\\n          \\\"Compare known perturbation effects (e.g., target knockdown) before and after QC to ensure signal is preserved.\\\",\\n          \\\"Quantify batch-perturbation confounding after QC and adjust batch correction accordingly.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Validate quality by comparing held-out perturbation performance with and without QC filtering and with different thresholds.\\\",\\n        \\\"implementation\\\": \\\"Include QC validation as part of the pipeline, with logged metrics and thresholds.\\\",\\n        \\\"decision_support_quality_validation\\\": \\\"Empty decision support blocks provide no quality validation requirements. Validation should focus on preserving perturbation signal while removing technical artifacts.\\\"\\n      }\\n    }\\n  },\\n  \\\"problem\\\": {\\n    \\\"definition\\\": {\\n      \\\"scope\\\": {\\n        \\\"assessment\\\": \\\"The problem scope is well-defined: unseen-perturbation prediction of mean transcriptional response in K562 CRISPRi, with strict constraints on data usage and output format. However, the scope does not explicitly address the predictability of different perturbation classes.\\\",\\n        \\\"issues\\\": [\\n          \\\"The scope assumes held-out perturbations share biological context with training perturbations, but does not discuss the possibility of out-of-distribution targets (e.g., essential genes, transcription factors with no close analogs).\\\",\\n          \\\"The scope does not specify how to handle perturbations with no measurable effect or with lethal phenotypes.\\\",\\n          \\\"The scope is limited to mean effects and does not consider distributional shifts, which may be relevant for some perturbations.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"improvements\\\": [\\n          \\\"Explicitly discuss the predictability of different perturbation classes (e.g., essential vs. non-essential, transcription factors vs. metabolic enzymes) and stratify analysis accordingly.\\\",\\n          \\\"Define a strategy for perturbations with no measurable effect: predict near-zero delta with calibrated shrinkage.\\\",\\n          \\\"Acknowledge that mean effects may miss important distributional changes and discuss whether this limits biological interpretation.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Validate scope by stratifying held-out perturbation performance by target gene properties (expression, essentiality, network centrality) and reporting performance gradients.\\\",\\n        \\\"biological_validation\\\": \\\"Confirm that predicted effects for held-out perturbations are biologically plausible given the target gene's function and known K562 biology.\\\",\\n        \\\"decision_support_scope\\\": \\\"No decision support constraints are provided. The scope should be re-evaluated once decision support specifies model class and evaluation strategy.\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"clarity\\\": {\\n        \\\"assessment\\\": \\\"The problem definition is clear and formal, with explicit input-output mapping, constraints, and evaluation criteria. However, some terms (e.g., 'control mean', 'delta') could be defined more precisely in terms of the actual data.\\\",\\n        \\\"issues\\\": [\\n          \\\"The definition of 'control mean' does not specify whether it is computed over all control cells or matched by batch.\\\",\\n          \\\"The definition of 'delta' does not specify whether it is computed on the log1p CP10k scale or after additional transformation.\\\",\\n          \\\"The definition of 'top-20 DE genes' does not specify how DE genes are defined (e.g., by effect size, statistical significance, or both).\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"improvements\\\": [\\n          \\\"Specify that control mean is computed over all control cells in the training file, optionally matched by batch if batch effects are present.\\\",\\n          \\\"Specify that delta is computed on the log1p CP10k scale as mean_pert - mean_control.\\\",\\n          \\\"Specify that top-20 DE genes are defined by the largest absolute delta in the true data, as implied by the evaluator.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Validate clarity by implementing the evaluator exactly and confirming that internal metrics match the official evaluator on a held-out subset.\\\",\\n        \\\"biological_validation\\\": \\\"Confirm that the definition of delta and top-20 DE genes aligns with biological expectations (e.g., target knockdown shoul