es for training\\\",\\n          \\\"Use pseudobulk or robust means\\\",\\n          \\\"Control-anchored batch correction\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Compare QC-filtered vs unfiltered control means and held-out performance.\\\",\\n        \\\"decision_support_technical_quality\\\": \\\"No decision-support constraints; filtering choices should be validated against held-out perturbation metrics.\\\"\\n      }\\n    },\\n    \\\"noise\\\": {\\n      \\\"biological\\\": {\\n        \\\"sources\\\": [\\n          \\\"Cell-to-cell transcriptional heterogeneity\\\",\\n          \\\"Cell-cycle state\\\",\\n          \\\"Variable knockdown efficiency\\\",\\n          \\\"Clonal variation\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"impact\\\": \\\"Reduces signal-to-noise for per-perturbation mean estimation, especially for weakly expressed genes and small-effect perturbations.\\\",\\n        \\\"assessment\\\": \\\"Estimate via variance decomposition across cells and guides within a perturbation.\\\",\\n        \\\"mitigation\\\": [\\n          \\\"Aggregate to pseudobulk/mean over many cells\\\",\\n          \\\"Shrink toward control or gene-level priors\\\",\\n          \\\"Use guide-level replication\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Held-out perturbation cross-validation.\\\",\\n        \\\"decision_support_noise\\\": \\\"Empty decision-support blocks; noise handling should be tuned empirically.\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"technical\\\": {\\n        \\\"sources\\\": [\\n          \\\"Library-size variation\\\",\\n          \\\"Ambient RNA\\\",\\n          \\\"Doublets\\\",\\n          \\\"Batch effects\\\",\\n          \\\"Guide-capture dropout\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"impact\\\": \\\"Can bias per-perturbation means and inflate delta correlations if correlated with perturbation identity.\\\",\\n        \\\"assessment\\\": \\\"Check batch-perturbation confounding and guide-assignment confidence distributions.\\\",\\n        \\\"mitigation\\\": [\\n          \\\"Filter doublets and low-confidence guides\\\",\\n          \\\"Include batch covariates\\\",\\n          \\\"Use control-anchored normalization\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Compare predictions with and without technical corrections on held-out training perturbations.\\\",\\n        \\\"decision_support_technical_noise\\\": \\\"No decision-support constraints; corrections must be validated, not assumed.\\\"\\n      }\\n    }\\n  },\\n  \\\"preprocessing\\\": {\\n    \\\"normalization\\\": {\\n      \\\"strategies\\\": {\\n        \\\"recommendations\\\": [\\n          \\\"Do not renormalize the stored matrix; it is already CP10k + log1p\\\",\\n          \\\"Compute control mean on the same log1p CP10k scale\\\",\\n          \\\"For internal modeling, optionally standardize features (z-score) but invert to the original scale for output\\\",\\n          \\\"Use pseudobulk means or robust means per perturbation for training targets\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"rationale\\\": \\\"The evaluator expects predictions on the same normalization as cellforge_input.h5ad; any additional transformation must be inverted exactly.\\\",\\n        \\\"implementation\\\": \\\"Load h5ad with scanpy/anndata; verify max values and integer-like structure are consistent with log1p CP10k; compute control mean as the mean over control cells; train model to predict delta from control; add control mean back for final means.\\\",\\n        \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Check that predicted control perturbation (if included) reproduces the control mean; verify output shape n_perts x n_genes and gene order.\\\",\\n        \\\"biological_validation\\\": \\\"Confirm that predicted deltas for known training perturbations recapitulate target-gene knockdown.\\\",\\n        \\\"decision_support_normalization\\\": \\\"Empty decision-support blocks; normalization must match the evaluator exactly.\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"quality_control\\\": {\\n        \\\"procedures\\\": [\\n          \\\"Filter cells with low total counts / low genes detected (thresholds from training distribution)\\\",\\n          \\\"Filter cells with high mitochondrial fraction if mt genes are annotated\\\",\\n          \\\"Remove doublets/multiplets and low-confidence guide assignments\\\",\\n          \\\"Retain control cells for baseline estimation\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"criteria\\\": [\\n          \\\"Counts and genes-detected thresholds set at distribution tails (e.g., 1st/99th percentile or MAD-based)\\\",\\n          \\\"Guide assignment confidence threshold\\\",\\n          \\\"Doublet score threshold\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"implementation\\\": \\\"Apply filters to training cells only; do not alter the gene set or normalization. Recompute control mean after filtering and compare.\\\",\\n        \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Assess stability of control mean and held-out perturbation performance under different QC thresholds.\\\",\\n        \\\"biological_validation\\\": \\\"Ensure QC does not remove perturbation-responsive cell states disproportionately.\\\",\\n        \\\"decision_support_qc\\\": \\\"No decision-support constraints; QC thresholds should be data-driven and validated.\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"feature_selection\\\": {\\n        \\\"methods\\\": [\\n          \\\"Use all genes for output\\\",\\n          \\\"For model input, consider HVGs, pathway/gene-set scores, or gene embeddings\\\",\\n          \\\"Use target-gene identity and functional annotations as perturbation features\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"criteria\\\": [\\n          \\\"Retain genes with adequate detection in K562\\\",\\n          \\\"Prioritize genes with known perturbation responsiveness\\\",\\n          \\\"Avoid features that leak held-out perturbation identity\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"implementation\\\": \\\"Compute HVGs on training cells; build perturbation feature matrix from gene annotations/embeddings; ensure no held-out perturbation data are used.\\\",\\n        \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Compare held-out perturbation metrics across feature sets.\\\",\\n        \\\"biological_validation\\\": \\\"Check that selected features recover known K562 biology.\\\",\\n        \\\"decision_support_features\\\": \\\"Empty decision-support blocks; feature choice should be cross-validated.\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"batch_correction\\\": {\\n        \\\"methods\\\": [\\n          \\\"Control-anchored batch correction (e.g., regress out batch on controls, apply to all)\\\",\\n          \\\"Include batch as covariate in the model\\\",\\n          \\\"Harmony/ComBat only if batch is clearly confounded and validated on controls\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"criteria\\\": [\\n          \\\"Batch must not be confounded with perturbation in training\\\",\\n          \\\"Correction must preserve perturbation signal in training perturbations\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"implementation\\\": \\\"Identify batch columns in obs; apply correction to training data; recompute control mean per batch and pool.\\\",\\n        \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Held-out perturbation cross-validation\",\n  \"error\": \"Failed to parse JSON response\"\n}\n\nProblem Investigation:\n{\n  \"content\": \"{\\n  \\\"content\\\": \\\"{\\\\n  \\\\\\\"problem_definition\\\\\\\": {\\\\n    \\\\\\\"formal_definition\\\\\\\": {\\\\n      \\\\\\\"biological_context\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"K562 chronic myeloid leukemia cells under CRISPRi (dCas9-KRAB) knockdown of individual genes. The training file cellforge_input.h5ad contains single-cell log1p CP10K expression for perturbations that are observed in training; the held-out perturbations in heldout_perturbations.json have zero cells in training. The task is to predict, for each unseen CRISPRi target, the mean expression vector over all genes in the same gene order and normalisation as the input. Control label(s) in obs must be confirmed from metadata (e.g., 'control', 'NTC', 'non-targeting'); if multiple control types exist, document them and use the union of control cells unless batch-matching indicates otherwise. CRISPRi knockdown strength varies by guide and target; this is an unobserved per-perturbation latent variable that must be approximated via target-gene features and shrinkage.\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"input_output_mapping\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Input: (i) annotated scRNA-seq AnnData with cells x genes, obs including perturbation label and control label(s), var gene names/order, X log1p(CP10K); (ii) heldout_perturbations.json list of unseen perturbation keys. Output: predictions.npz with perts (held-out keys in listed order) and means (n_perts x n_genes float), where means[p,g] = predicted mean log1p(CP10K) expression of gene g in K562 cells carrying perturbation p. Delta is defined explicitly on the log1p(CP10K) scale as delta[p,g] = mean_pert[p,g] - mean_control[g], where mean_control is computed over all control cells in the training file (optionally batch-matched if batch effects are detected). The evaluator implicitly compares this delta; top-20 DE genes are defined by the largest absolute true delta per perturbation.\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"biological_constraints\\\\\\\": [\\\\n        \\\\\\\"All predictions must be on the same log1p(CP10K) scale as the input; no renormalisation.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Gene order must exactly match var_names of cellforge_input.h5ad.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"CRISPRi acts primarily by transcriptional repression of the target gene and, through partial knockdown, of downstream regulons; predicted effects should be sparse and mostly non-positive for direct targets.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"K562-specific expression program, copy-number heterogeneity, and low per-cell counts constrain realistic effect sizes.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Held-out perturbations are unseen; predictions must generalise from gene-level features and from observed perturbation effects, not from memorised labels.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Control mean must be estimated from the provided control cells only; no external screen data may be used.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Essential genes may produce strong or lethal phenotypes not fully captured by mean expression; predictions for essential targets should be treated as lower-confidence and shrunk more aggressively.\\\\\\\"\\\\n      ],\\\\n      \\\\\\\"evaluation_criteria\\\\\\\": [\\\\n        \\\\\\\"pearson_delta: per-perturbation Pearson correlation between predicted and true change-from-control vectors; rewards correct direction and relative magnitude of gene-level shifts.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"MSE: mean squared error of predicted vs true mean expression (or delta) across genes and perturbations; penalises scale and baseline errors.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Top-20 DE metrics (MSE/PCC/R2): correctness of the strongest differentially expressed genes (largest absolute true delta), which carry most biological signal.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Biological validity: predicted target-gene knockdown, enrichment of known K562/CRISPRi regulon members, and consistency with essential-gene and pathway annotations.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Reproducibility: deterministic pipeline, fixed seeds, documented preprocessing, and stable predictions under resampling.\\\\\\\"\\\\n      ],\\\\n      \\\\\\\"success_metrics\\\\\\\": [\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Mean pearson_delta across held-out perturbations substantially above zero and above a control-mean baseline.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"MSE lower than the variance of true perturbation means around control mean.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Top-20 DE PCC and R2 above baseline and consistent with known K562 biology.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Correct sign and magnitude of knockdown for the CRISPRi target gene where it is expressed.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Stable rankings of top predicted DE genes across bootstrap resamples of training cells.\\\\\\\"\\\\n      ]\\\\n    },\\\\n    \\\\\\\"key_variables\\\\\\\": {\\\\n      \\\\\\\"biological\\\\\\\": [\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Perturbation identity (CRISPRi target gene) and its baseline expression in K562.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Gene-gene regulatory relationships (transcription factor targets, co-expression modules, protein complexes).\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Cell-state heterogeneity in K562 (cell cycle, copy-number variation, differentiation state).\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Essentiality and network centrality of the target gene.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Baseline control expression mean and variance per gene.\\\\\\\"\\\\n      ],\\\\n      \\\\\\\"technical\\\\\\\": [\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Library size and CP10K normalisation, log1p transform.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Guide RNA efficiency and CRISPRi knockdown strength (unobserved per perturbation).\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Batch/experiment effects and donor/replicate structure in obs.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Gene filtering and var order in the AnnData.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Number of cells per observed perturbation (uneven support).\\\\\\\"\\\\n      ],\\\\n      \\\\\\\"relationships\\\\\\\": [\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Delta_pert = f(target gene features, network neighbourhood, control expression).\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Perturbations targeting genes in the same complex/module produce correlated deltas.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Effect magnitude scales with target expression and network centrality.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Gene-level deltas are approximately additive in log space for weak perturbations.\\\\\\\"\\\\n      ],\\\\n      \\\\\\\"constraints\\\\\\\": [\\\\n        \\\\\\\"No held-out perturbation cells may be used at any stage, including feature construction.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Control mean must be computed from control cells in the provided file.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Predictions must be finite, non-negative after inverse transform considerations, and on log1p CP10K scale.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Computational budget must allow full training on the provided AnnData without external downloads.\\\\\\\"\\\\n      ],\\\\n      \\\\\\\"validation_requirements\\\\\\\": [\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Held-out-perturbation cross-validation within the training file (simulate unseen perturbations by leaving out entire perturbation labels).\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Baseline comparisons: control mean, target-only knockdown, and simple linear models.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Bootstrap stability of top-20 DE gene predictions.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Biological sanity checks against known K562 CRISPRi hits and pathway annotations.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Stratified CV by target expression, essentiality, and network centrality to report performance gradients.\\\\\\\"\\\\n      ]\\\\n    },\\\\n    \\\\\\\"scope\\\\\\\": {\\\\n      \\\\\\\"biological_scope\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Unseen-perturbation prediction of mean transcriptional response to CRISPRi knockdown in a single cancer cell line (K562). Focus on gene-level mean shifts, not cell-state distributions or guide-level heterogeneity. Explicitly acknowledge that predictability varies by perturbation class: essential genes, transcription factors, and genes with no close training analogs are expected to be harder; predictions for these should be lower-confidence and more shrunk. Mean effects may miss distributional changes (e.g., cell-state composition shifts), which limits biological interpretation for some perturbations.\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"technical_scope\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Train only on cellforge_input.h5ad; build gene and perturbation feature representations from training data; predict held-out perturbation mean vectors; output predictions.npz in the required format.\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"limitations\\\\\\\": [\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Cannot model guide-specific knockdown efficiency for unseen perturbations; knockdown strength is approximated via target features and calibrated shrinkage.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Cannot capture perturbation-specific cell-state composition changes beyond mean effects.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Limited by the coverage and quality of observed perturbations in the training file.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"No external screen or literature-derived effect sizes may be used.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Linear/additive assumptions may miss strong non-linear or synthetic interactions.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Perturbations with no measurable effect or lethal phenotypes are not explicitly modelled; strategy is to predict near-zero delta with calibrated shrinkage.\\\\\\\"\\\\n      ],\\\\n      \\\\\\\"assumptions\\\\\\\": [\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Control cells in the file are representative of the unperturbed K562 baseline; control label(s) are documented from obs metadata.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Gene order and normalisation are consistent between training and evaluation.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Held-out perturbations share biological context (K562, CRISPRi) with training perturbations, but may include out-of-distribution targets (essential genes, TFs with no close analogs).\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Mean delta is the primary quantity of interest; higher moments are not scored.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Gene features derived from training expression and co-expression are transferable to unseen perturbations.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"If batch effects are present, control mean can be batch-matched without leakage.\\\\\\\"\\\\n      ],\\\\n      \\\\\\\"biological_validation\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Validate predicted deltas against known K562 CRISPRi biology: target-gene knockdown, enrichment of regulon members, essential-gene effects, and concordance with co-perturbation clusters. Use held-out-perturbation cross-validation as the primary internal proxy for the official evaluator, stratified by target expression, essentiality, and network centrality.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    }\\\\n  },\\\\n  \\\\\\\"key_challenges\\\\\\\": {\\\\n    \\\\\\\"biological\\\\\\\": {\\\\n      \\\\\\\"challenges\\\\\\\": [\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Unseen perturbations have no cells, so no direct supervision of their effects.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"CRISPRi knockdown strength varies by target and guide and is unobserved.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"K562 has strong copy-number heterogeneity and cell-cycle structure that confound mean effects.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Perturbation effects can be non-linear and context-dependent (e.g., essential genes).\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Many perturbations have weak or near-zero transcriptional effects, making delta prediction noisy.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Held-out perturbations may have no close training analogs, limiting feature-based transfer.\\\\\\\"\\\\n      ],\\\\n      \\\\\\\"impact\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Naive models collapse to control mean and yield near-zero pearson_delta; over-predicting large effects harms MSE and top-20 metrics.\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"mitigation\\\\\\\": [\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Use gene-level features (expression, co-expression, network centrality, essentiality proxies) to transfer information to unseen targets.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Shrink predicted deltas toward zero with regularisation tuned by held-out-perturbation CV; specify a shrinkage factor alpha in [0,1] multiplied to predicted deltas and tuned to maximise pearson_delta subject to MSE constraints.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Model effect magnitude as a function of target expression and network degree.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Use robust control mean estimation and per-gene variance weighting; compare mean, trimmed mean, and median-of-means via bootstrap.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Quantify similarity between held-out and training perturbations using gene-level features to estimate predictability and set shrinkage per perturbation.\\\\\\\"\\\\n      ],\\\\n      \\\\\\\"validation\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Leave-one-perturbation-out CV on observed perturbations, stratified by effect size, target expression, essentiality, and network centrality, with pearson_delta and top-20 metrics computed identically to the official evaluator.\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"biological_considerations\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Preserve the biology that CRISPRi primarily represses the target and its regulon; enforce non-positive direct-target effects where appropriate and allow downstream positive/negative changes. Predictions for essential genes should be flagged as lower-confidence.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    },\\\\n    \\\\\\\"technical\\\\\\\": {\\\\n      \\\\\\\"challenges\\\\\\\": [\\\\n        \\\\\\\"High-dimensional output (n_genes per perturbation) with limited number of observed perturbations.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Sparse, zero-inflated single-cell data and log1p CP10K scale.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Uneven cell numbers per perturbation and potential batch effects.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Strict requirement to match gene order and normalisation exactly.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"No external data allowed, limiting pretrained transfer.\\\\\\\"\\\\n      ],\\\\n      \\\\\\\"impact\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Overfitting to observed perturbations, unstable predictions, and format errors that invalidate submission.\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"mitigation\\\\\\\": [\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Low-rank / factor models and ridge-regularised regression from perturbation features to delta vectors.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Dimensionality reduction (PCA, NMF, gene modules) before regression.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Explicit format checks: perts order equals heldout list, means shape and dtype float, gene order equals var_names.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Deterministic seeding and unit tests for preprocessing and output.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Verify normalisation state from data (max value consistent with log1p CP10K, non-integer values) before modelling.\\\\\\\"\\\\n      ],\\\\n      \\\\\\\"validation\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Unit tests on output schema; CV metrics; sanity check that predicted control-mean baseline reproduces input control mean within numerical tolerance.\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"implementation_requirements\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Python with anndata, scanpy, numpy, scipy, scikit-learn, pandas; CPU-feasible pipeline; fixed random seeds; logged versions of Python, anndata, scanpy, numpy, scipy, scikit-learn; unit tests for output format and gene order.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    },\\\\n    \\\\\\\"data_quality\\\\\\\": {\\\\n      \\\\\\\"issues\\\\\\\": [\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Low and variable library sizes per cell.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Zero inflation and dropout in log1p CP10K data.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Potential doublets and low-quality cells.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Batch/experiment structure in obs; batch-perturbation confounding not quantified.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Held-out perturbations may be enriched for weak or essential genes.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"QC thresholds not derived from data distribution.\\\\\\\"\\\\n      ],\\\\n      \\\\\\\"impact\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Noisy control mean and noisy per-perturbation means in training, biasing feature learning and calibration.\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"mitigation\\\\\\\": [\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Standard QC: filter low-count cells and rarely detected genes only if consistent with var order requirements (keep full gene set for output); set thresholds at data-driven tails (e.g., 1st/99th percentile or MAD-based) after inspecting distributions of total counts, genes detected, and mitochondrial fraction if available.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Robust control mean via trimmed mean or median-of-means; select method by bootstrap stability.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Weight genes by detection rate and variance in loss/metrics.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Batch-aware feature construction or batch as covariate in regression; quantify batch-perturbation confounding by cross-tabulation and association statistics.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Define criteria for excluding low-quality perturbations from training (e.g., <20 cells or low guide concordance) and document excluded perturbations; weight training perturbations by cell count and guide confidence.\\\\\\\"\\\\n      ],\\\\n      \\\\\\\"validation\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Compare control mean estimates across batches and QC thresholds; bootstrap stability of gene features and predictions; compare held-out perturbation performance with and without QC filtering and with different thresholds; assess whether QC disproportionately removes cells from certain perturbations or cell states.\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"biological_validation\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Check that top expressed genes and control mean match expected K562 markers (e.g., haemoglobin genes, BCR-ABL programme); compare known perturbation effects (e.g., target knockdown) before and after QC to ensure signal is preserved.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    },\\\\n    \\\\\\\"computational\\\\\\\": {\\\\n      \\\\\\\"challenges\\\\\\\": [\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Full gene set output requires predicting thousands of genes per perturbation.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Cross-validation over perturbations multiplies training cost.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Memory for dense cell x gene matrices and delta matrices.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Hyperparameter tuning under strict no-leakage constraints.\\\\\\\"\\\\n      ],\\\\n      \\\\\\\"impact\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Long runtimes and risk of leakage if tuning uses held-out perturbations improperly.\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"mitigation\\\\\\\": [\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Work in reduced gene space (PCA/modules) for modelling and project back to full gene space.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Use efficient linear algebra (ridge regression, SVD) and sparse representations where possible.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Nested CV: inner loop for hyperparameters, outer loop for evaluation.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Cache control mean and feature matrices.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Pilot cross-validation on a subset of training perturbations to estimate runtime and memory before full runs.\\\\\\\"\\\\n      ],\\\\n      \\\\\\\"validation\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Profile runtime and memory; verify no held-out perturbation cells enter any fit; run pipeline twice with same seed to confirm identical outputs.\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"resource_requirements\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Single workstation with >=32 GB RAM, multi-core CPU; optional GPU not required; total runtime target under a few hours.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    },\\\\n    \\\\\\\"interpretability\\\\\\\": {\\\\n      \\\\\\\"requirements\\\\\\\": [\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Predicted deltas should be attributable to target-gene features and network neighbourhood.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Top-20 DE genes should be biologically plausible for each perturbation.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Model should expose gene-module loadings and perturbation feature importances.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Interpretability must be quantified: sign and magnitude of predicted target-gene delta, enrichment significance of known regulon members in top predicted DE genes (permutation test), and module loading coherence.\\\\\\\"\\\\n      ],\\\\n      \\\\\\\"challenges\\\\\\\": [\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Low-rank models can be opaque.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Unseen perturbations lack direct validation of mechanism.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Separating direct target knockdown from downstream effects.\\\\\\\"\\\\n      ],\\\\n      \\\\\\\"solutions\\\\\\\": [\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Use linear models with gene-module loadings and perturbation feature coefficients; report per-perturbation top modules and enriched pathways for held-out predictions.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Explicitly model target-gene self-knockdown as a feature and decompose predicted deltas into target-gene component, module components, and residual.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Post-hoc enrichment analysis of predicted DE genes with permutation-based significance.\\\\\\\"\\\\n      ],\\\\n      \\\\\\\"validation\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Check that predicted target knockdown appears for expressed targets and that top modules match known regulons; use permutation tests to assess enrichment significance; confirm predicted mechanisms align with known K562 biology and CRISPRi directionality (predominant repression).\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"biological_validation\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Compare predicted DE gene sets with known K562 CRISPRi hits and pathway annotations; verify coherence of co-perturbation clusters.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    }\\\\n  },\\\\n  \\\\\\\"research_questions\\\\\\\": {\\\\n    \\\\\\\"primary\\\\\\\": {\\\\n      \\\\\\\"questions\\\\\\\": [\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Can feature-based models achieve positive pearson_delta on held-out perturbations, above a control-mean baseline, in K562 CRISPRi data?\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Which feature representation (expression, co-expression modules, network centrality, essentiality proxies) best transfers to unseen CRISPRi targets, as measured by pearson_delta and top-20 DE metrics?\\\\\\\",\\\\\",\n  \"error\": \"Failed to parse JSON response\"\n}\n\nBaseline Assessment:\n{\n  \"content\": \"{\\n  \\\"content\\\": \\\"{\\\\n  \\\\\\\"baseline_models\\\\\\\": {\\\\n    \\\\\\\"literature_review\\\\\\\": {\\\\n      \\\\\\\"existing_methods\\\\\\\": {\\\\n        \\\\\\\"methods\\\\\\\": [\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Control-mean (zero-delta) baseline: predict the mean expression of control cells (defined by the exact control label(s) in obs, e.g., 'control' or 'NTC') for every held-out perturbation; delta prediction is zero\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Global-mean-delta baseline: predict the average delta (perturbation mean minus control mean) across all training perturbations for every held-out perturbation\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Per-gene linear/ridge regression on perturbation one-hot encoding: fit gene-wise models on training perturbation means; for unseen perturbations, predictions reduce to the control mean (or training-perturbation mean) with gene-wise shrinkage\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Latent linear models: PCA on training perturbation means followed by ridge regression from perturbation descriptors (if available) or nearest-neighbor transfer in PCA space\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Nearest-neighbor baseline: match held-out perturbations to training perturbations using gene-level features (e.g., control-centered mean profiles, target-gene embeddings) and transfer the closest training perturbation effect; quantify neighbor distance to estimate predictability\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Descriptor-based ridge/kernel regression: if target-gene features are available (e.g., expression level, essentiality, network centrality, gene embeddings), regress perturbation effects on descriptors\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Low-rank matrix factorization (e.g., truncated SVD, NMF) of the perturbation-by-gene effect matrix to share statistical strength across perturbations\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Graph/network propagation baselines: build a gene co-expression or PPI graph and propagate known perturbation effects to unseen perturbations using network similarity; validate against over-smoothing\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Autoencoder/VAE latent-space models: train on single-cell expression, then model perturbation effects in latent space with simple regressors (only if sufficient perturbations exist)\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Set-transformer/attention models: treat perturbation as a token and predict gene-wise deltas via cross-attention over training perturbations (only if sufficient training perturbations exist)\\\\\\\"\\\\n        ],\\\\n        \\\\\\\"strengths\\\\\\\": [\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Control-mean and global-delta baselines are robust, fast, and establish lower bounds for pearson_delta and MSE\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Linear/ridge and low-rank models are interpretable and computationally cheap, suitable for high-dimensional gene space with limited perturbations\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Nearest-neighbor methods can capture similarity between perturbations if informative features exist; neighbor distance provides a predictability estimate\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Graph-based methods incorporate biological prior knowledge and may generalize to unseen perturbations with similar network neighborhoods\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Descriptor-based models can leverage target-gene properties (expression, essentiality, network centrality) to stratify predictability\\\\\\\"\\\\n        ],\\\\n        \\\\\\\"limitations\\\\\\\": [\\\\n          \\\\\\\"No major perturbation-prediction methods were identified in the retrieved literature, so the benchmark relies on generic baselines and must be constructed carefully\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Linear models cannot capture nonlinear or combinatorial perturbation effects\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Nearest-neighbor methods depend heavily on informative perturbation descriptors; in CRISPRi K562, held-out perturbations may have no close training neighbors, especially essential genes or transcription factors with no analogs\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Graph propagation requires high-quality networks and may over-smooth effects\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Deep generative/attention models require large training data and risk overfitting given the limited number of perturbations\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Most methods struggle with unseen-perturbation generalization because perturbation effects are highly gene- and context-specific; perturbations with no measurable effect or lethal phenotypes are particularly challenging\\\\\\\"\\\\n        ],\\\\n        \\\\\\\"biological_relevance\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"CRISPRi perturbations in K562 target specific genes; prediction should capture downstream transcriptional consequences. Baselines that shrink toward control mean are biologically conservative but may miss real effects. Predictability should be stratified by target-gene properties (expression, essentiality, network centrality) because essential genes may produce strong or lethal phenotypes not captured by mean expression alone. Graph/network methods can leverage known regulatory relationships, but validation must focus on top DE genes and pathway-level effects rather than global correlation alone.\\\\\\\"\\\\n      },\\\\n      \\\\\\\"model_comparison\\\\\\\": {\\\\n        \\\\\\\"comparison_criteria\\\\\\\": [\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Pearson correlation of predicted vs. true delta from control per perturbation (primary)\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"MSE on mean expression and on delta, with calibration (slope/intercept) reported\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Top-20 DE gene recovery: MSE, PCC, R2 on top-20 DE genes, where top-20 DE genes are defined by the largest absolute true delta as implied by the evaluator\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Computational cost and scalability to ~10k genes and hundreds of perturbations\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Robustness to biological and technical variability (batch, cell-cycle, library size) and to control-cell subsampling\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Biological interpretability: whether predicted effects align with known pathways and gene function, including sign and magnitude of predicted target-gene delta\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Stratified performance by target-gene properties (expression, essentiality, network centrality) and by neighbor distance to training perturbations\\\\\\\"\\\\n        ],\\\\n        \\\\\\\"performance_metrics\\\\\\\": [\\\\n          \\\\\\\"pearson_delta (per perturbation, then averaged; report undefined/zero handling)\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"MSE (global and per perturbation) and calibration slope/intercept of predicted vs. true delta\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Top-20 DE MSE, PCC, R2\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Spearman rank correlation of gene effects\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Precision@k and recall@k for top-k DE genes (k=20, 50, 100)\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Per-gene correlation across perturbations\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Bootstrap confidence intervals for pearson_delta and top-20 DE metrics\\\\\\\"\\\\n        ],\\\\n        \\\\\\\"biological_interpretability\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Linear, low-rank, and nearest-neighbor models are most interpretable (effect = weighted sum of training effects, module loadings, or direct transfer). Graph methods provide pathway-level interpretation. Deep models are less interpretable unless attention or gene-level attributions are used. Define interpretability metrics: sign and magnitude of predicted target-gene delta, enrichment of known regulon members in top predicted DE genes, and module loading coherence. Report per-perturbation top modules and enriched pathways.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"computational_requirements\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Control-mean and global-delta baselines require negligible computation. Ridge/PCA, low-rank, and nearest-neighbor methods scale to thousands of genes and hundreds of perturbations with standard CPU resources. Graph propagation may require network preprocessing but remains feasible. Deep attention/VAE models require GPU training and careful hyperparameter tuning; given the small number of perturbations, they risk overfitting and are not recommended as first-line baselines.\\\\\\\"\\\\n      },\\\\n      \\\\\\\"recommendations\\\\\\\": {\\\\n        \\\\\\\"model_selection\\\\\\\": [\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Start with control-mean (zero delta) and global-mean-delta baselines as mandatory sanity checks\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Implement ridge regression on perturbation means with gene-wise shrinkage as a strong linear baseline\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Implement low-rank matrix factorization (e.g., truncated SVD) of the perturbation-by-gene effect matrix to share statistical strength\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Implement nearest-neighbor transfer in a reduced latent space (PCA on training perturbation means) and report neighbor distances to estimate predictability\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"If perturbation descriptors (e.g., target-gene expression, essentiality, network centrality, gene embeddings) are available, implement descriptor-based ridge or kernel regression\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Only then consider deep models (set transformer, VAE) if sufficient training perturbations exist and validation shows gains over linear baselines\\\\\\\"\\\\n        ],\\\\n        \\\\\\\"implementation_priority\\\\\\\": [\\\\n          \\\\\\\"1. Reproduce the evaluator exactly: compute control mean from training control cells (document exact control label(s) from obs), define delta as perturbation mean minus control mean on the log1p CP10k scale, and evaluate on held-out perturbations using pearson_delta, MSE, and top-20 DE metrics\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"2. Build a robust preprocessing pipeline: verify normalization state (max value consistent with log1p CP10k, non-integer values), filter genes consistently, and ensure gene order matches cellforge_input.h5ad var_names\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"3. Implement and tune linear/ridge and low-rank baselines with leave-one-perturbation-out cross-validation over training perturbations\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"4. Add nearest-neighbor and descriptor-based baselines; quantify neighbor distance to training perturbations\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"5. Evaluate deep models only if they beat linear baselines in cross-validation\\\\\\\"\\\\n        ],\\\\n        \\\\\\\"biological_validation\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Validate predictions by checking enrichment of known K562 CRISPRi effects (e.g., knockdown of essential genes, pathway members) in top predicted DE genes. Confirm that predicted target knockdown appears for expressed targets and that top modules match known regulons. Compare predicted deltas for held-out perturbations to known gene-function relationships where available. Use pathway enrichment and gene-set enrichment analysis (MSigDB, Reactome) as secondary biological checks with permutation tests for enrichment significance.\\\\\\\"\\\\n      }\\\\n    },\\\\n    \\\\\\\"evaluation_framework\\\\\\\": {\\\\n      \\\\\\\"metrics\\\\\\\": {\\\\n        \\\\\\\"primary_metrics\\\\\\\": [\\\\n          \\\\\\\"pearson_delta: Pearson correlation between predicted and true delta from control, per held-out perturbation, averaged across perturbations; handle undefined correlations (e.g., zero-variance predictions) explicitly\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"MSE on mean expression (n_perts x n_genes)\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Top-20 DE gene MSE, PCC, and R2 (computed per perturbation and averaged), where top-20 DE genes are defined by the largest absolute true delta\\\\\\\"\\\\n        ],\\\\n        \\\\\\\"secondary_metrics\\\\\\\": [\\\\n          \\\\\\\"MSE on delta (perturbation mean minus control mean)\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Spearman rank correlation of gene effects\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Precision@k and recall@k for top-k DE genes (k=20, 50, 100)\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Calibration error: slope/intercept of predicted vs. true delta\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Per-gene correlation across perturbations\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Bootstrap confidence intervals for pearson_delta and top-20 DE metrics\\\\\\\"\\\\n        ],\\\\n        \\\\\\\"biological_metrics\\\\\\\": [\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Pathway/gene-set enrichment of top predicted DE genes vs. known CRISPRi targets, with permutation-based significance\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Recovery of known downstream targets of the perturbed gene\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Directional consistency of predicted effects for known activating/repressing relationships (predominant repression expected for CRISPRi)\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Network proximity of predicted DE genes to the perturbed gene in a PPI/regulatory network\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Sign and magnitude of predicted target-gene delta for expressed targets\\\\\\\"\\\\n        ],\\\\n        \\\\\\\"validation_metrics\\\\\\\": [\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Cross-validation over training perturbations: hold out a subset of training perturbations and compute the same metrics\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Bootstrap confidence intervals for pearson_delta and top-20 DE metrics\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Sensitivity to normalization and gene filtering choices\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Robustness to control-cell subsampling and batch effects\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Stratified performance by target-gene expression, essentiality, network centrality, and neighbor distance to training perturbations\\\\\\\"\\\\n        ]\\\\n      },\\\\n      \\\\\\\"validation_strategy\\\\\\\": {\\\\n        \\\\\\\"cross_validation\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Use leave-one-perturbation-out or k-fold cross-validation over the training perturbations to tune model hyperparameters and estimate generalization. Because held-out perturbations are entirely unseen, cross-validation must mimic this setting: never use the held-out perturbation's cells or mean in training. Stratify folds by target-gene expression, essentiality, and network centrality to assess performance gradients. Report mean and standard deviation of metrics across folds.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"biological_validation\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"For each held-out perturbation, compare predicted top DE genes to known biology of the targeted gene (e.g., if the target is a transcription factor, check enrichment of its known targets). Use independent pathway databases (MSigDB, Reactome) and, where possible, external CRISPRi screens in K562 as a sanity check, but do not use external data for training. Use permutation tests to assess enrichment significance.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"independent_testing\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"The provided held-out_perturbations.json is the primary independent test set. Additionally, create an internal held-out set from training perturbations (e.g., 20% of perturbations) to estimate performance before final submission. Ensure no data leakage: control mean must be computed only from training control cells, and gene filtering must be consistent across train and test.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"robustness_testing\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Evaluate model sensitivity to: (1) control-cell subsampling (bootstrap control mean), (2) gene filtering thresholds, (3) normalization choices (CP10k vs. library-size normalization), (4) random seeds for stochastic models, and (5) batch/plate effects if metadata are available. Quantify batch-perturbation confounding by cross-tabulating batch and perturbation labels and computing association statistics. Report variance of metrics across these perturbations.\\\\\\\"\\\\n      },\\\\n      \\\\\\\"benchmarks\\\\\\\": {\\\\n        \\\\\\\"baseline_models\\\\\\\": [\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Control mean (zero delta)\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Global mean delta (average delta across all training perturbations)\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Gene-wise linear regression on perturbation one-hot encoding (predicts training mean, reduces to control mean for unseen)\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Ridge regression on perturbation means with gene-wise shrinkage\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Low-rank matrix factorization (e.g., truncated SVD) of the perturbation-by-gene effect matrix\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Nearest-neighbor transfer in PCA space of training perturbation means, with neighbor distance reported\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Descriptor-based ridge/kernel regression if perturbation features (target expression, essentiality, network centrality, gene embeddings) are available\\\\\\\"\\\\n        ],\\\\n        \\\\\\\"state_of_the_art\\\\\\\": [\\\\n          \\\\\\\"No state-of-the-art perturbation-prediction methods were identified in the retrieved literature; the benchmark should be constructed from the baselines above and any recent general-purpose methods (e.g., linear mixed models, matrix factorization) applied to this dataset\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"If external methods are considered, they must be retrained only on cellforge_input.h5ad to respect the task constraints\\\\\\\"\\\\n        ],\\\\n        \\\\\\\"biological_benchmarks\\\\\\\": [\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Known CRISPRi knockdown effects in K562 for housekeeping/essential genes\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Pathway-level enrichment of predicted DE genes for held-out perturbations with permutation-based significance\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Consistency with known gene regulatory relationships (e.g., transcription factor targets)\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Sign and magnitude of predicted target-gene delta for expressed targets\\\\\\\"\\\\n        ],\\\\n        \\\\\\\"performance_targets\\\\\\\": [\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Beat control-mean baseline on pearson_delta (i.e., positive correlation on average)\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Achieve top-20 DE PCC significantly above zero and above the global-delta baseline\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Maintain MSE no worse than the control-mean baseline (since predicting zero delta minimizes MSE if effects are small), or report calibrated shrinkage that trades MSE for pearson_delta\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Demonstrate stable performance across cross-validation folds (low variance) and across bootstrap control subsamples\\\\\\\"\\\\n        ]\\\\n      }\\\\n    },\\\\n    \\\\\\\"performance_analysis\\\\\\\": {\\\\n      \\\\\\\"model_performance\\\\\\\": {\\\\n        \\\\\\\"accuracy_metrics\\\\\\\": [\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Expected: control-mean baseline yields pearson_delta near zero (undefined or zero) and low MSE; global-delta baseline may yield slightly positive pearson_delta if perturbations share common effects\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Linear/ridge and low-rank models may achieve modest positive pearson_delta if perturbation effects are additive or low-rank and training perturbations are representative\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Nearest-neighbor methods may outperform linear models if held-out perturbations have close training neighbors, but likely fail for distinct perturbations; report neighbor distance as a predictability estimate\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Top-20 DE metrics are expected to be challenging; even small positive PCC indicates some signal\\\\\\\"\\\\n        ],\\\\n        \\\\\\\"biological_relevance\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Models that predict zero delta are biologically conservative but miss real effects. Models that capture shared stress/global responses may achieve higher pearson_delta but may not recover perturbation-specific DE genes. Biological relevance should be assessed by whether predicted DE genes are enriched for the perturbed gene's known pathway and whether predicted target knockdown appears for expressed targets.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"generalization\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Generalization to unseen perturbations is the core challenge. Performance will likely be low for perturbations with no close training analogs, especially essential genes or transcription factors with no analogs. Cross-validation over training perturbations will overestimate performance if perturbations are clustered; use leave-cluster-out or leave-one-out and stratify by target-gene properties to better mimic unseen setting.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"robustness\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Performance is expected to be sensitive to control-mean estimation (cell numbers), gene filtering, and normalization. Deep models may be less robust due to overfitting. Linear models with shrinkage and low-rank models are more stable. Use bootstrap resampling of control cells to estimate uncertainty in control mean and propagate to delta predictions.\\\\\\\"\\\\n      },\\\\n      \\\\\\\"comparative_analysis\\\\\\\": {\\\\n        \\\\\\\"baseline_comparison\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Control-mean and global-delta baselines are essential reference points. Any model that does not beat them on pearson_delta and top-20 DE metrics provides no added value. Ridge regression typically improves over ordinary least squares by stabilizing gene-wise estimates. Low-rank models share statistical strength across perturbations. Nearest-neighbor methods can outperform linear models when perturbation effects are locally similar in latent space; report neighbor distances.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"state_of_the_art_comparison\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Without identified state-of-the-art methods, comparison is against the constructed baselines. If deep models are implemented, they must be compared under identical preprocessing and evaluation; given limited training perturbations, they are unlikely to outperform well-tuned linear baselines.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"biological_comparison\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Biological comparison should focus on pathway-level recovery rather than global correlation. A model with lower pearson_delta but better recovery of known targets may be more biologically useful. Report both statistical and biological metrics, stratified by target-gene properties.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"computational_comparison\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Linear, low-rank, and nearest-neighbor methods are orders of magnitude cheaper than deep models and easier to reproduce. Given the task's constraints (train only on provided data), computational efficiency favors simple models unless deep models show clear gains.\\\\\\\"\\\\n      },\\\\n      \\\\\\\"strengths_weaknesses\\\\\\\": {\\\\n        \\\\\\\"strengths\\\\\\\": [\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Simple baselines are reproducible, fast, and provide transparent lower bounds\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Linear/ridge and low-rank models are interpretable and can capture additive or low-rank perturbation effects\\\\\\\",\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Nearest-neighbor methods can leverage similarity between perturbations without parametric assumptions and provide neighbor-distance predictability estimates\\\\\\\",\",\n  \"error\": \"Failed to parse JSON response\"\n}\n\nPlease provide refinement comments in the following JSON format, incorporating decision support considerations:\n{\n    \"dataset\": {\n        \"biological\": {\n            \"relevance\": {\n                \"assessment\": \"string\",\n                \"issues\": [\"string\"],\n                \"improvements\": [\"string\"],\n                \"validation\": \"string\",\n                \"biological_validation\": \"string\",\n                \"decision_support_alignment\": \"string\"\n            },\n            \"interpretability\": {\n                \"assessment\": \"string\",\n                \"issues\": [\"string\"],\n                \"improvements\": [\"string\"],\n                \"validation\": \"string\",\n                \"biological_validation\": \"string\",\n                \"decision_support_considerations\": \"string\"\n            }\n        },\n        \"technical\": {\n            \"quality\": {\n                \"assessment\": \"string\",\n                \"issues\": [\"string\"],\n                \"improvements\": [\"string\"],\n                \"validation\": \"string\",\n                \"technical_validation\": \"string\",\n                \"decision_support_quality\": \"string\"\n            },\n            \"reproducibility\": {\n                \"assessment\": \"string\",\n                \"issues\": [\"string\"],\n                \"improvements\": [\"string\"],\n                \"validation\": \"string\",\n                \"technical_validation\": \"string\",\n                \"decision_support_reproducibility\": \"string\"\n            }\n        },\n        \"preprocessing\": {\n            \"recommendations\": {\n                \"assessment\": \"string\",\n                \"issues\": [\"string\"],\n                \"improvements\": [\"string\"],\n                \"validation\": \"string\",\n                \"implementation\