n        \\\\\\\"Number of cells per observed perturbation (uneven support).\\\\\\\"\\\\n      ],\\\\n      \\\\\\\"relationships\\\\\\\": [\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Delta_pert = f(target gene features, network neighbourhood, control expression).\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Perturbations targeting genes in the same complex/module produce correlated deltas.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Effect magnitude scales with target expression and network centrality.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Gene-level deltas are approximately additive in log space for weak perturbations.\\\\\\\"\\\\n      ],\\\\n      \\\\\\\"constraints\\\\\\\": [\\\\n        \\\\\\\"No held-out perturbation cells may be used at any stage, including feature construction.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Control mean must be computed from control cells in the provided file.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Predictions must be finite, non-negative after inverse transform considerations, and on log1p CP10K scale.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Computational budget must allow full training on the provided AnnData without external downloads.\\\\\\\"\\\\n      ],\\\\n      \\\\\\\"validation_requirements\\\\\\\": [\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Held-out-perturbation cross-validation within the training file (simulate unseen perturbations by leaving out entire perturbation labels).\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Baseline comparisons: control mean, target-only knockdown, and simple linear models.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Bootstrap stability of top-20 DE gene predictions.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Biological sanity checks against known K562 CRISPRi hits and pathway annotations.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Stratified CV by target expression, essentiality, and network centrality to report performance gradients.\\\\\\\"\\\\n      ]\\\\n    },\\\\n    \\\\\\\"scope\\\\\\\": {\\\\n      \\\\\\\"biological_scope\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Unseen-perturbation prediction of mean transcriptional response to CRISPRi knockdown in a single cancer cell line (K562). Focus on gene-level mean shifts, not cell-state distributions or guide-level heterogeneity. Explicitly acknowledge that predictability varies by perturbation class: essential genes, transcription factors, and genes with no close training analogs are expected to be harder; predictions for these should be lower-confidence and more shrunk. Mean effects may miss distributional changes (e.g., cell-state composition shifts), which limits biological interpretation for some perturbations.\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"technical_scope\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Train only on cellforge_input.h5ad; build gene and perturbation feature representations from training data; predict held-out perturbation mean vectors; output predictions.npz in the required format.\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"limitations\\\\\\\": [\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Cannot model guide-specific knockdown efficiency for unseen perturbations; knockdown strength is approximated via target features and calibrated shrinkage.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Cannot capture perturbation-specific cell-state composition changes beyond mean effects.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Limited by the coverage and quality of observed perturbations in the training file.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"No external screen or literature-derived effect sizes may be used.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Linear/additive assumptions may miss strong non-linear or synthetic interactions.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Perturbations with no measurable effect or lethal phenotypes are not explicitly modelled; strategy is to predict near-zero delta with calibrated shrinkage.\\\\\\\"\\\\n      ],\\\\n      \\\\\\\"assumptions\\\\\\\": [\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Control cells in the file are representative of the unperturbed K562 baseline; control label(s) are documented from obs metadata.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Gene order and normalisation are consistent between training and evaluation.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Held-out perturbations share biological context (K562, CRISPRi) with training perturbations, but may include out-of-distribution targets (essential genes, TFs with no close analogs).\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Mean delta is the primary quantity of interest; higher moments are not scored.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Gene features derived from training expression and co-expression are transferable to unseen perturbations.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"If batch effects are present, control mean can be batch-matched without leakage.\\\\\\\"\\\\n      ],\\\\n      \\\\\\\"biological_validation\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Validate predicted deltas against known K562 CRISPRi biology: target-gene knockdown, enrichment of regulon members, essential-gene effects, and concordance with co-perturbation clusters. Use held-out-perturbation cross-validation as the primary internal proxy for the official evaluator, stratified by target expression, essentiality, and network centrality.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    }\\\\n  },\\\\n  \\\\\\\"key_challenges\\\\\\\": {\\\\n    \\\\\\\"biological\\\\\\\": {\\\\n      \\\\\\\"challenges\\\\\\\": [\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Unseen perturbations have no cells, so no direct supervision of their effects.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"CRISPRi knockdown strength varies by target and guide and is unobserved.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"K562 has strong copy-number heterogeneity and cell-cycle structure that confound mean effects.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Perturbation effects can be non-linear and context-dependent (e.g., essential genes).\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Many perturbations have weak or near-zero transcriptional effects, making delta prediction noisy.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Held-out perturbations may have no close training analogs, limiting feature-based transfer.\\\\\\\"\\\\n      ],\\\\n      \\\\\\\"impact\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Naive models collapse to control mean and yield near-zero pearson_delta; over-predicting large effects harms MSE and top-20 metrics.\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"mitigation\\\\\\\": [\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Use gene-level features (expression, co-expression, network centrality, essentiality proxies) to transfer information to unseen targets.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Shrink predicted deltas toward zero with regularisation tuned by held-out-perturbation CV; specify a shrinkage factor alpha in [0,1] multiplied to predicted deltas and tuned to maximise pearson_delta subject to MSE constraints.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Model effect magnitude as a function of target expression and network degree.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Use robust control mean estimation and per-gene variance weighting; compare mean, trimmed mean, and median-of-means via bootstrap.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Quantify similarity between held-out and training perturbations using gene-level features to estimate predictability and set shrinkage per perturbation.\\\\\\\"\\\\n      ],\\\\n      \\\\\\\"validation\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Leave-one-perturbation-out CV on observed perturbations, stratified by effect size, target expression, essentiality, and network centrality, with pearson_delta and top-20 metrics computed identically to the official evaluator.\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"biological_considerations\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Preserve the biology that CRISPRi primarily represses the target and its regulon; enforce non-positive direct-target effects where appropriate and allow downstream positive/negative changes. Predictions for essential genes should be flagged as lower-confidence.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    },\\\\n    \\\\\\\"technical\\\\\\\": {\\\\n      \\\\\\\"challenges\\\\\\\": [\\\\n        \\\\\\\"High-dimensional output (n_genes per perturbation) with limited number of observed perturbations.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Sparse, zero-inflated single-cell data and log1p CP10K scale.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Uneven cell numbers per perturbation and potential batch effects.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Strict requirement to match gene order and normalisation exactly.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"No external data allowed, limiting pretrained transfer.\\\\\\\"\\\\n      ],\\\\n      \\\\\\\"impact\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Overfitting to observed perturbations, unstable predictions, and format errors that invalidate submission.\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"mitigation\\\\\\\": [\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Low-rank / factor models and ridge-regularised regression from perturbation features to delta vectors.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Dimensionality reduction (PCA, NMF, gene modules) before regression.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Explicit format checks: perts order equals heldout list, means shape and dtype float, gene order equals var_names.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Deterministic seeding and unit tests for preprocessing and output.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Verify normalisation state from data (max value consistent with log1p CP10K, non-integer values) before modelling.\\\\\\\"\\\\n      ],\\\\n      \\\\\\\"validation\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Unit tests on output schema; CV metrics; sanity check that predicted control-mean baseline reproduces input control mean within numerical tolerance.\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"implementation_requirements\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Python with anndata, scanpy, numpy, scipy, scikit-learn, pandas; CPU-feasible pipeline; fixed random seeds; logged versions of Python, anndata, scanpy, numpy, scipy, scikit-learn; unit tests for output format and gene order.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    },\\\\n    \\\\\\\"data_quality\\\\\\\": {\\\\n      \\\\\\\"issues\\\\\\\": [\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Low and variable library sizes per cell.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Zero inflation and dropout in log1p CP10K data.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Potential doublets and low-quality cells.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Batch/experiment structure in obs; batch-perturbation confounding not quantified.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Held-out perturbations may be enriched for weak or essential genes.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"QC thresholds not derived from data distribution.\\\\\\\"\\\\n      ],\\\\n      \\\\\\\"impact\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Noisy control mean and noisy per-perturbation means in training, biasing feature learning and calibration.\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"mitigation\\\\\\\": [\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Standard QC: filter low-count cells and rarely detected genes only if consistent with var order requirements (keep full gene set for output); set thresholds at data-driven tails (e.g., 1st/99th percentile or MAD-based) after inspecting distributions of total counts, genes detected, and mitochondrial fraction if available.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Robust control mean via trimmed mean or median-of-means; select method by bootstrap stability.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Weight genes by detection rate and variance in loss/metrics.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Batch-aware feature construction or batch as covariate in regression; quantify batch-perturbation confounding by cross-tabulation and association statistics.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Define criteria for excluding low-quality perturbations from training (e.g., <20 cells or low guide concordance) and document excluded perturbations; weight training perturbations by cell count and guide confidence.\\\\\\\"\\\\n      ],\\\\n      \\\\\\\"validation\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Compare control mean estimates across batches and QC thresholds; bootstrap stability of gene features and predictions; compare held-out perturbation performance with and without QC filtering and with different thresholds; assess whether QC disproportionately removes cells from certain perturbations or cell states.\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"biological_validation\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Check that top expressed genes and control mean match expected K562 markers (e.g., haemoglobin genes, BCR-ABL programme); compare known perturbation effects (e.g., target knockdown) before and after QC to ensure signal is preserved.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    },\\\\n    \\\\\\\"computational\\\\\\\": {\\\\n      \\\\\\\"challenges\\\\\\\": [\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Full gene set output requires predicting thousands of genes per perturbation.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Cross-validation over perturbations multiplies training cost.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Memory for dense cell x gene matrices and delta matrices.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Hyperparameter tuning under strict no-leakage constraints.\\\\\\\"\\\\n      ],\\\\n      \\\\\\\"impact\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Long runtimes and risk of leakage if tuning uses held-out perturbations improperly.\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"mitigation\\\\\\\": [\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Work in reduced gene space (PCA/modules) for modelling and project back to full gene space.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Use efficient linear algebra (ridge regression, SVD) and sparse representations where possible.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Nested CV: inner loop for hyperparameters, outer loop for evaluation.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Cache control mean and feature matrices.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Pilot cross-validation on a subset of training perturbations to estimate runtime and memory before full runs.\\\\\\\"\\\\n      ],\\\\n      \\\\\\\"validation\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Profile runtime and memory; verify no held-out perturbation cells enter any fit; run pipeline twice with same seed to confirm identical outputs.\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"resource_requirements\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Single workstation with >=32 GB RAM, multi-core CPU; optional GPU not required; total runtime target under a few hours.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    },\\\\n    \\\\\\\"interpretability\\\\\\\": {\\\\n      \\\\\\\"requirements\\\\\\\": [\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Predicted deltas should be attributable to target-gene features and network neighbourhood.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Top-20 DE genes should be biologically plausible for each perturbation.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Model should expose gene-module loadings and perturbation feature importances.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Interpretability must be quantified: sign and magnitude of predicted target-gene delta, enrichment significance of known regulon members in top predicted DE genes (permutation test), and module loading coherence.\\\\\\\"\\\\n      ],\\\\n      \\\\\\\"challenges\\\\\\\": [\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Low-rank models can be opaque.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Unseen perturbations lack direct validation of mechanism.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Separating direct target knockdown from downstream effects.\\\\\\\"\\\\n      ],\\\\n      \\\\\\\"solutions\\\\\\\": [\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Use linear models with gene-module loadings and perturbation feature coefficients; report per-perturbation top modules and enriched pathways for held-out predictions.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Explicitly model target-gene self-knockdown as a feature and decompose predicted deltas into target-gene component, module components, and residual.\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Post-hoc enrichment analysis of predicted DE genes with permutation-based significance.\\\\\\\"\\\\n      ],\\\\n      \\\\\\\"validation\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Check that predicted target knockdown appears for expressed targets and that top modules match known regulons; use permutation tests to assess enrichment significance; confirm predicted mechanisms align with known K562 biology and CRISPRi directionality (predominant repression).\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"biological_validation\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Compare predicted DE gene sets with known K562 CRISPRi hits and pathway annotations; verify coherence of co-perturbation clusters.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    }\\\\n  },\\\\n  \\\\\\\"research_questions\\\\\\\": {\\\\n    \\\\\\\"primary\\\\\\\": {\\\\n      \\\\\\\"questions\\\\\\\": [\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Can feature-based models achieve positive pearson_delta on held-out perturbations, above a control-mean baseline, in K562 CRISPRi data?\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Which feature representation (expression, co-expression modules, network centrality, essentiality proxies) best transfers to unseen CRISPRi targets, as measured by pearson_delta and top-20 DE metrics?\\\\\\\",\\\\\",\n  \"error\": \"Failed to parse JSON response\"\n}\n\nREVIEWER COMMENTS:\n{\n  \"note\": \"{\\n  \\\"dataset\\\": {\\n    \\\"biological\\\": {\\n      \\\"relevance\\\": {\\n        \\\"assessment\\\": \\\"The dataset analysis correctly frames the K562 CRISPRi unseen-perturbation prediction task and emphasizes biological relevance via target-gene knockdown, pathway coherence, and top-20 DE gene recovery. The biological context is well described, and the analysis appropriately notes that output must cover all genes while modeling can focus on informative genes.\\\",\\n        \\\"issues\\\": [\\n          \\\"The control label(s) in obs are not confirmed; the analysis assumes 'control' or 'NTC' exists, which could lead to incorrect delta computation if the actual label differs.\\\",\\n          \\\"Biological relevance is asserted but not tied to concrete K562-specific markers or known CRISPRi effect sizes.\\\",\\n          \\\"The distinction between direct target repression and downstream regulon effects is mentioned but not operationalized in the feature or validation plan.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"improvements\\\": [\\n          \\\"Explicitly confirm and document the exact control label(s) from obs metadata before any analysis; if multiple control types exist, define the union and justify the choice.\\\",\\n          \\\"Add K562-specific biological checks: expected expression of BCR-ABL programme genes, haemoglobin genes, and known essential genes; verify these in control cells.\\\",\\n          \\\"Operationalize direct vs. downstream effects by separating target-gene self-knockdown from regulon modules in the model and validation.\\\",\\n          \\\"Stratify biological relevance assessment by target-gene class (essential, TF, housekeeping, low-expression) to set expectations for predictability.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Validate biological relevance by checking that control cells express expected K562 markers, that training perturbations show target-gene knockdown where the target is expressed, and that top predicted DE genes for held-out perturbations are enriched for known regulon members using permutation tests.\\\",\\n        \\\"biological_validation\\\": \\\"Use pathway enrichment (MSigDB, Reactome) and network proximity of predicted DE genes to the perturbed gene as secondary biological checks; require enrichment significance via permutation rather than relying on global correlation alone.\\\",\\n        \\\"decision_support_alignment\\\": \\\"The decision support blocks for model selection, evaluation strategy, data preparation, implementation plan, and risk assessment are empty. The dataset analysis should explicitly flag that biological relevance criteria (e.g., required target knockdown sign, pathway enrichment thresholds) must be defined once decision support is populated, and that current recommendations are provisional.\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"interpretability\\\": {\\n        \\\"assessment\\\": \\\"The analysis recognizes that interpretability is gene- and perturbation-dependent and that a model must output the full gene vector while internally focusing on informative genes. It appropriately suggests module loadings and perturbation feature importances.\\\",\\n        \\\"issues\\\": [\\n          \\\"Interpretability is described qualitatively without concrete metrics or thresholds.\\\",\\n          \\\"The analysis does not specify how to separate target-gene knockdown from downstream effects in predictions.\\\",\\n          \\\"No plan is given for reporting per-perturbation top modules or enriched pathways for held-out predictions.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"improvements\\\": [\\n          \\\"Define interpretability metrics: sign and magnitude of predicted target-gene delta, enrichment significance of known regulon members in top predicted DE genes (permutation test), and module loading coherence.\\\",\\n          \\\"Decompose predicted deltas into target-gene component, module components, and residual to separate direct from downstream effects.\\\",\\n          \\\"Report per-perturbation top modules and enriched pathways for held-out predictions as a standard output.\\\",\\n          \\\"Use linear models with gene-module loadings and perturbation feature coefficients where possible to preserve interpretability.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Validate interpretability by confirming predicted target knockdown appears for expressed targets, that top modules match known regulons, and that enrichment of known regulon members in top predicted DE genes is significant by permutation.\\\",\\n        \\\"biological_validation\\\": \\\"Compare predicted DE gene sets with known K562 CRISPRi hits and pathway annotations; verify coherence of co-perturbation clusters and directional consistency (predominant repression expected for CRISPRi).\\\",\\n        \\\"decision_support_considerations\\\": \\\"With empty decision support blocks, interpretability requirements cannot be prioritized against performance or computational constraints. The analysis should flag that interpretability metrics must be reconciled with the eventual model selection and evaluation strategy once decision support is defined.\\\"\\n      }\\n    },\\n    \\\"technical\\\": {\\n      \\\"quality\\\": {\\n        \\\"assessment\\\": \\\"The technical quality assessment is thorough in identifying QC metrics, normalization state, and batch effects. It correctly notes that the matrix is pre-normalized (CP10k + log1p) and that renormalization would invalidate the submission.\\\",\\n        \\\"issues\\\": [\\n          \\\"QC thresholds are described as data-driven but not specified; no concrete procedure for setting thresholds is given.\\\",\\n          \\\"Batch-perturbation confounding is not quantified; the analysis assumes batch correction may be needed without evidence.\\\",\\n          \\\"The analysis does not address how to handle genes with zero expression in controls, which affects delta computation and correlation metrics.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"improvements\\\": [\\n          \\\"Specify concrete QC threshold procedures: e.g., MAD-based filters on total counts and genes detected, mitochondrial fraction thresholds if mt genes are annotated, and doublet score cutoffs.\\\",\\n          \\\"Quantify batch-perturbation confounding by cross-tabulation and association statistics before deciding on batch correction.\\\",\\n          \\\"Define handling of zero-expression genes: exclude from correlation metrics where variance is zero, but retain in output; document this in the evaluation plan.\\\",\\n          \\\"Verify normalization state empirically (max value, non-integer values) before modeling and document the check.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Validate technical quality by comparing control mean estimates across batches and QC thresholds, assessing bootstrap stability of gene features and predictions, and comparing held-out perturbation performance with and without QC filtering.\\\",\\n        \\\"technical_validation\\\": \\\"Run unit tests on output schema (perts order, means shape, dtype, gene order equals var_names) and sanity checks that predicted control-mean baseline reproduces input control mean within numerical tolerance.\\\",\\n        \\\"decision_support_quality\\\": \\\"The empty decision support blocks provide no quality thresholds or power constraints. The analysis should state that QC thresholds and exclusion criteria for low-quality perturbations must be set from the training data distribution and validated against held-out perturbation metrics, pending decision support guidance.\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"reproducibility\\\": {\\n        \\\"assessment\\\": \\\"The analysis mentions deterministic seeding and unit tests for output format, which is good. However, reproducibility is not treated as a first-class requirement with concrete procedures.\\\",\\n        \\\"issues\\\": [\\n          \\\"No explicit version pinning or environment specification is described.\\\",\\n          \\\"The analysis does not specify how to ensure no leakage of held-out perturbations into feature construction.\\\",\\n          \\\"No procedure is given for verifying that repeated runs produce identical outputs.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"improvements\\\": [\\n          \\\"Pin versions of Python, anndata, scanpy, numpy, scipy, scikit-learn, and pandas; log them with the submission.\\\",\\n          \\\"Implement unit tests for output format and gene order, and run the pipeline twice with the same seed to confirm identical outputs.\\\",\\n          \\\"Add explicit leakage checks: verify that held-out perturbation labels and cells are excluded from all fits, including feature construction and hyperparameter tuning.\\\",\\n          \\\"Use nested cross-validation (inner loop for hyperparameters, outer loop for evaluation) to avoid leakage during tuning.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Validate reproducibility by running the full pipeline end-to-end twice with fixed seeds and comparing outputs bitwise; run unit tests on output schema and gene order.\\\",\\n        \\\"technical_validation\\\": \\\"Technical validation should include a check that the control mean computed from the provided file matches the expected scale (log1p CP10k) and that the predicted control-mean baseline reproduces it within numerical tolerance.\\\",\\n        \\\"decision_support_reproducibility\\\": \\\"With empty decision support blocks, reproducibility requirements (e.g., required logging, version pinning) are not specified. The analysis should flag that reproducibility standards must be confirmed against the eventual implementation plan and risk assessment.\\\"\\n      }\\n    },\\n    \\\"preprocessing\\\": {\\n      \\\"recommendations\\\": {\\n        \\\"assessment\\\": \\\"The preprocessing recommendations are generally sound: do not renormalize, compute control mean on the same scale, use pseudobulk or robust means, and validate batch correction on controls. The analysis correctly emphasizes that any transformation must be inverted exactly.\\\",\\n        \\\"issues\\\": [\\n          \\\"The recommendation to optionally standardize features for internal modeling is not accompanied by a procedure to invert to the original scale for output.\\\",\\n          \\\"Feature selection recommendations (HVGs, pathway scores) are not tied to a validation plan that ensures no information leakage from held-out perturbations.\\\",\\n          \\\"Batch correction methods are listed without criteria for when to apply them or how to validate them against held-out perturbation performance.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"improvements\\\": [\\n          \\\"Specify the exact inversion procedure for any internal standardization: store per-gene means and standard deviations, and apply the inverse transform to predicted means before output.\\\",\\n          \\\"Tie feature selection to cross-validated held-out perturbation performance; compare feature sets (all genes, HVGs, modules) under identical evaluation.\\\",\\n          \\\"Define batch correction decision criteria: apply only if batch-perturbation confounding is quantified and correction improves held-out perturbation metrics on training perturbations.\\\",\\n          \\\"Document the control mean computation procedure precisely, including handling of zero-expression genes and batch matching.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Validate preprocessing by checking that predicted control perturbation (if included) reproduces the control mean, verifying output shape and gene order, and comparing held-out perturbation metrics across preprocessing choices.\\\",\\n        \\\"implementation\\\": \\\"Load h5ad with scanpy/anndata, verify max values and non-integer structure consistent with log1p CP10k, compute control mean over control cells, train model to predict delta from control, add control mean back for final means, and invert any internal standardization exactly.\\\",\\n        \\\"decision_support_preprocessing\\\": \\\"The empty data preparation block provides no constraints on preprocessing. The analysis should flag that normalization, QC, and batch correction choices must be reconciled with the eventual data preparation plan and validated against the evaluator's metrics.\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"validation\\\": {\\n        \\\"assessment\\\": \\\"The preprocessing validation plan is reasonable but incomplete: it mentions stability of control mean and held-out performance under different QC thresholds but does not specify how to propagate uncertainty from control mean estimation to delta predictions.\\\",\\n        \\\"issues\\\": [\\n          \\\"No bootstrap procedure is specified for control mean uncertainty.\\\",\\n          \\\"The analysis does not address sensitivity of predictions to gene filtering choices.\\\",\\n          \\\"Batch correction validation is mentioned but not operationalized.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"improvements\\\": [\\n          \\\"Add bootstrap resampling of control cells to estimate uncertainty in control mean and propagate to delta predictions.\\\",\\n          \\\"Evaluate sensitivity to gene filtering thresholds by comparing held-out perturbation metrics across thresholds.\\\",\\n          \\\"Operationalize batch correction validation: compare predictions with and without correction on held-out training perturbations, and report whether correction improves pearson_delta and top-20 metrics.\\\",\\n          \\\"Include a check that QC does not disproportionately remove cells from certain perturbations or cell states.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Validate preprocessing choices by comparing held-out perturbation performance across QC thresholds, normalization states, and batch correction options, reporting mean and standard deviation of metrics.\\\",\\n        \\\"implementation\\\": \\\"Implement bootstrap control mean estimation and sensitivity analyses as part of the preprocessing validation pipeline; log all choices and results.\\\",\\n        \\\"decision_support_validation\\\": \\\"The empty evaluation strategy block provides no guidance on how to validate preprocessing. The analysis should state that preprocessing validation must mirror the evaluator's metrics and be reconciled with the eventual evaluation strategy.\\\"\\n      }\\n    },\\n    \\\"quality\\\": {\\n      \\\"assessment\\\": {\\n        \\\"evaluation\\\": \\\"The quality assessment identifies relevant biological and technical metrics and correctly notes that biological quality is expected to be high for well-powered perturbations and low for those with few cells or weak guides.\\\",\\n        \\\"issues\\\": [\\n          \\\"The assessment does not specify how to handle perturbations with no measurable transcriptional effect.\\\",\\n          \\\"The analysis does not define criteria for excluding low-quality perturbations from training.\\\",\\n          \\\"Quality assessment is described qualitatively without concrete thresholds.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"improvements\\\": [\\n          \\\"Define criteria for excluding low-quality perturbations from training (e.g., <20 cells, low guide concordance) and document excluded perturbations.\\\",\\n          \\\"Specify how to handle perturbations with no measurable effect: predict near-zero delta with calibrated shrinkage and flag as lower-confidence.\\\",\\n          \\\"Set quality thresholds from the training data distribution (e.g., 1st/99th percentile or MAD-based) and validate against held-out perturbation metrics.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Validate quality assessment by comparing control mean estimates across QC thresholds, assessing bootstrap stability of gene features and predictions, and comparing held-out perturbation performance with and without QC filtering.\\\",\\n        \\\"implementation\\\": \\\"Implement QC filters on training cells only; retain full gene set for output; recompute control mean after filtering and compare; log all thresholds and excluded perturbations.\\\",\\n        \\\"decision_support_quality_assessment\\\": \\\"The empty decision support blocks provide no quality thresholds or exclusion criteria. The analysis should flag that quality assessment criteria must be set from the training data distribution and validated against held-out perturbation metrics, pending decision support guidance.\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"validation\\\": {\\n        \\\"evaluation\\\": \\\"The quality validation plan mentions comparing QC-filtered vs unfiltered control means and held-out performance, which is appropriate, but it lacks concrete procedures for biological validation of QC effects.\\\",\\n        \\\"issues\\\": [\\n          \\\"No procedure is given for checking that QC does not remove perturbation-responsive cell states disproportionately.\\\",\\n          \\\"Biological validation of QC effects is mentioned but not operationalized.\\\",\\n          \\\"The analysis does not specify how to assess batch-perturbation confounding.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"improvements\\\": [\\n          \\\"Add a procedure to compare cell-state composition (e.g., cell-cycle phase, clustering) before and after QC for each perturbation.\\\",\\n          \\\"Operationalize biological validation of QC: check that known perturbation effects (e.g., target knockdown) are preserved after QC.\\\",\\n          \\\"Quantify batch-perturbation confounding by cross-tabulation and association statistics before deciding on batch correction.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Validate quality by comparing held-out perturbation performance with and without QC filtering and with different thresholds, and by assessing whether QC disproportionately removes cells from certain perturbations or cell states.\\\",\\n        \\\"implementation\\\": \\\"Implement QC validation as part of the pipeline: compute metrics before and after QC, log results, and use them to select thresholds that maximize held-out perturbation performance.\\\",\\n        \\\"decision_support_quality_validation\\\": \\\"The empty decision support blocks provide no guidance on quality validation. The analysis should state that quality validation must be reconciled with the eventual evaluation strategy and risk assessment.\\\"\\n      }\\n    }\\n  },\\n  \\\"problem\\\": {\\n    \\\"definition\\\": {\\n      \\\"scope\\\": {\\n        \\\"assessment\\\": \\\"The problem scope is well defined: unseen-perturbation prediction of mean transcriptional response to CRISPRi knockdown in K562, with explicit acknowledgment that predictability varies by perturbation class. The scope appropriately limits the task to gene-level mean shifts and notes limitations regarding cell-state distributions.\\\",\\n        \\\"issues\\\": [\\n          \\\"The scope does not explicitly define how to handle perturbations with no measurable effect or lethal phenotypes beyond shrinkage.\\\",\\n          \\\"The analysis does not specify the expected number of held-out perturbations or their biological composition, which affects feasibility.\\\",\\n          \\\"The scope does not address whether predictions should be constrained to biologically plausible ranges (e.g., non-negative after inverse transform).\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"improvements\\\": [\\n          \\\"Explicitly define a strategy for perturbations with no measurable effect: predict near-zero delta with calibrated shrinkage and flag as lower-confidence.\\\",\\n          \\\"Specify the expected number and biological composition of held-out perturbations (if known) to set feasibility expectations.\\\",\\n          \\\"Add constraints on predicted values: ensure predictions are finite and on the log1p CP10k scale, and consider non-negativity after inverse transform.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Validate scope by confirming that the evaluation metrics (pearson_delta, MSE, top-20 DE metrics) are computed exactly as the evaluator does, and that the scope's limitations are reflected in stratified performance reporting.\\\",\\n        \\\"biological_validation\\\": \\\"Validate scope by checking that predictions for essential genes and transcription factors are flagged as lower-confidence and that biological plausibility is assessed via pathway enrichment and target knockdown.\\\",\\n        \\\"decision_support_scope\\\": \\\"The empty decision support blocks provide no constraints on scope. The analysis should flag that scope decisions (e.g., handling of essential genes, prediction constraints) must be reconciled with the eventual model selection and risk assessment.\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"clarity\\\": {\\n        \\\"assessment\\\": \\\"The problem definition is generally clear: input/output mapping, biological constraints, evaluation criteria, and success metrics are well specified. The delta definition on the log1p CP10k scale is explicit.\\\",\\n        \\\"issues\\\": [\\n          \\\"The control label(s) are not confirmed, which introduces ambiguity in the delta definition.\\\",\\n          \\\"The analysis does not specify how to handle undefined correlations (e.g., zero-variance predictions) in pearson_delta.\\\",\\n          \\\"The success metrics are qualitative in places (e.g., 'substantially above zero') and lack concrete thresholds.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"improvements\\\": [\\n          \\\"Confirm and document the exact control label(s) from obs metadata before finalizing the problem definition.\\\",\\n          \\\"Specify how to handle undefined correlations: e.g., assign zero or exclude and report the fraction of undefined cases.\\\",\\n          \\\"Replace qualitative success metrics with concrete thresholds where possible (e.g., pearson_delta > 0.1, top-20 PCC > 0.2) based on baseline performance.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Validate clarity by having an independent reviewer check that the problem definition, input/output mapping, and evaluation criteria are unambiguous and reproducible.\\\",\\n        \\\"biological_validation\\\": \\\"Validate clarity by confirming that biological constraints (e.g., predominant repression for CRISPRi) are reflected in the success metrics and validation plan.\\\",\\n        \\\"decision_support_clarity\\\": \\\"The empty decision support blocks provide no clarity on model selection or evaluation strategy. The analysis should flag that clarity of the problem definition must be maintained when decision support is populated.\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"feasibility\\\": {\\n        \\\"assessment\\\": \\\"The problem is feasible given the constraints: train only on cellforge_input.h5ad, predict held-out perturbation means, and output in the required format. The analysis correctly notes computational requirements and the need for deterministic pipelines.\\\",\\n        \\\"issues\\\": [\\n          \\\"The analysis does not estimate the number of training perturbations, which affects the feasibility of deep models.\\\",\\n          \\\"Memory requirements for dense cell x gene matrices are mentioned but not quantified.\\\",\\n          \\\"The analysis does not address how to handle the high-dimensional output (n_genes per perturbation) with limited observed perturbations.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"improvements\\\": [\\n          \\\"Estimate the number of training perturbations and cells from the data to set expectations for model complexity.\\\",\\n          \\\"Quantify memory requirements and recommend sparse representations or reduced gene space for modeling.\\\",\\n          \\\"Specify a plan for high-dimensional output: use low-rank models, PCA, or gene modules to share statistical strength, and project back to full gene space.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Validate feasibility by profiling runtime and memory on a pilot subset of training perturbations before full runs, and by confirming that the pipeline completes within the computational budget.\\\",\\n        \\\"biological_validation\\\": \\\"Validate feasibility by confirming that the model can capture biologically plausible effect sizes given the number of training perturbations and the noise level in the data.\\\",\\n        \\\"decision_support_feasibility\\\": \\\"The empty implementation plan and risk assessment blocks provide no constraints on computational resources or timeline. The analysis should flag that feasibility must be reassessed once decision support is populated.\\\"\\n      }\\n    },\\n    \\\"challenges\\\": {\\n      \\\"identification\\\": {\\n        \\\"assessment\\\": \\\"The challenges are well identified: unseen perturbations have no direct supervision, knockdown strength is unobserved, K562 has copy\",\n  \"parse_error\": \"Failed to parse JSON response\"\n}\n\nRequirements:\n1. Keep the same high-level structure when possible.\n2. Resolve issues called out in the comments.\n3. Add explicit assumptions where information is missing.\n4. Return only valid JSON (no markdown).\n"
