lass (essential, transcription factor, housekeeping, low-expression) to stratify predictability.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"technical\\\": [\\n          \\\"Library size and CP10K normalization, log1p transform.\\\",\\n          \\\"Guide RNA efficiency and CRISPRi knockdown strength (unobserved per perturbation).\\\",\\n          \\\"Batch/experiment effects and donor/replicate structure in obs.\\\",\\n          \\\"Gene filtering and var order in the AnnData.\\\",\\n          \\\"Number of cells per observed perturbation (uneven support).\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"relationships\\\": [\\n          \\\"Delta_pert = f(target gene features, network neighbourhood, control expression).\\\",\\n          \\\"Perturbations targeting genes in the same complex/module produce correlated deltas.\\\",\\n          \\\"Effect magnitude scales with target expression and network centrality.\\\",\\n          \\\"Gene-level deltas are approximately additive in log space for weak perturbations.\\\",\\n          \\\"Direct target repression is separable from downstream module effects via decomposition.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"constraints\\\": [\\n          \\\"No held-out perturbation cells may be used at any stage, including feature construction and hyperparameter tuning.\\\",\\n          \\\"Control mean must be computed from control cells in the provided file.\\\",\\n          \\\"Predictions must be finite, on log1p CP10K scale, and non-negative after inverse transform.\\\",\\n          \\\"Computational budget must allow full training on the provided AnnData without external downloads.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"validation_requirements\\\": [\\n          \\\"Held-out-perturbation cross-validation within the training file (simulate unseen perturbations by leaving out entire perturbation labels).\\\",\\n          \\\"Baseline comparisons: control mean, target-only knockdown, and simple linear models.\\\",\\n          \\\"Bootstrap stability of top-20 DE gene predictions.\\\",\\n          \\\"Biological sanity checks against known K562 CRISPRi hits and pathway annotations (MSigDB, Reactome) using permutation tests.\\\",\\n          \\\"Stratified CV by target expression, essentiality, network centrality, and target-gene class to report performance gradients.\\\",\\n          \\\"Leakage checks: verify held-out perturbation labels and cells are excluded from all fits.\\\"\\n        ]\\n      },\\n      \\\"scope\\\": {\\n        \\\"biological_scope\\\": \\\"Unseen-perturbation prediction of mean transcriptional response to CRISPRi knockdown in a single cancer cell line (K562). Focus on gene-level mean shifts, not cell-state distributions or guide-level heterogeneity. Predictability varies by perturbation class: essential genes, transcription factors, and genes with no close training analogs are expected to be harder; predictions for these are lower-confidence and more shrunk. Perturbations with no measurable effect or lethal phenotypes are handled by predicting near-zero delta with calibrated shrinkage and flagging as lower-confidence. Mean effects may miss distributional changes (e.g., cell-state composition shifts), which limits biological interpretation for some perturbations. The expected number and biological composition of held-out perturbations should be documented if known; otherwise, stratified performance reporting provides feasibility expectations.\\\",\\n        \\\"technical_scope\\\": \\\"Train only on cellforge_input.h5ad; build gene and perturbation feature representations from training data; predict held-out perturbation mean vectors; output predictions.npz in the required format. Predictions are constrained to be finite and on the log1p CP10K scale, with non-negativity enforced after inverse transform.\\\",\\n        \\\"limitations\\\": [\\n          \\\"Cannot model guide-specific knockdown efficiency for unseen perturbations; knockdown strength is approximated via target features and calibrated shrinkage.\\\",\\n          \\\"Cannot capture perturbation-specific cell-state composition changes beyond mean effects.\\\",\\n          \\\"Limited by the coverage and quality of observed perturbations in the training file.\\\",\\n          \\\"No external screen or literature-derived effect sizes may be used.\\\",\\n          \\\"Linear/additive assumptions may miss strong non-linear or synthetic interactions.\\\",\\n          \\\"Perturbations with no measurable effect or lethal phenotypes are not explicitly modelled; strategy is to predict near-zero delta with calibrated shrinkage.\\\",\\n          \\\"Number and biological composition of held-out perturbations may be unknown; feasibility depends on training perturbation coverage.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"assumptions\\\": [\\n          \\\"Control cells in the file are representative of the unperturbed K562 baseline; control label(s) are confirmed and documented from obs metadata.\\\",\\n          \\\"Gene order and normalization are consistent between training and evaluation.\\\",\\n          \\\"Held-out perturbations share biological context (K562, CRISPRi) with training perturbations, but may include out-of-distribution targets (essential genes, TFs with no close analogs).\\\",\\n          \\\"Mean delta is the primary quantity of interest; higher moments are not scored.\\\",\\n          \\\"Gene features derived from training expression and co-expression are transferable to unseen perturbations.\\\",\\n          \\\"If batch effects are present, control mean can be batch-matched without leakage, validated by held-out perturbation performance.\\\",\\n          \\\"The training file contains a sufficient number of perturbations and cells to support feature-based transfer; if not, model complexity is reduced.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"biological_validation\\\": \\\"Validate predicted deltas against known K562 CRISPRi biology: target-gene knockdown, enrichment of regulon members (permutation test), essential-gene effects, and concordance with co-perturbation clusters. Check that control cells express expected K562 markers (BCR-ABL programme genes, haemoglobin genes, known essential genes). Use held-out-perturbation cross-validation as the primary internal proxy for the official evaluator, stratified by target expression, essentiality, network centrality, and target-gene class. Use pathway enrichment (MSigDB, Reactome) and network proximity of predicted DE genes to the perturbed gene as secondary checks, requiring permutation-based significance.\\\"\\n      }\\n    },\\n    \\\"key_challenges\\\": {\\n      \\\"biological\\\": {\\n        \\\"challenges\\\": [\\n          \\\"Unseen perturbations have no cells, so no direct supervision of their effects.\\\",\\n          \\\"CRISPRi knockdown strength varies by target and guide and is unobserved.\\\",\\n          \\\"K562 has strong copy-number heterogeneity and cell-cycle structure that confound mean effects.\\\",\\n          \\\"Perturbation effects can be non-linear and context-dependent (e.g., essential genes).\\\",\\n          \\\"Many perturbations have weak or near-zero transcriptional effects, making delta prediction noisy.\\\",\\n          \\\"Held-out perturbations may have no close training analogs, limiting feature-based transfer.\\\",\\n          \\\"Direct target repression and downstream regulon effects are entangled in mean deltas.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"impact\\\": \\\"Naive models collapse to control mean and yield near-zero pearson_delta; over-predicting large effects harms MSE and top-20 metrics. Inability to separate direct from downstream effects reduces interpretability and biological validity.\\\",\\n        \\\"mitigation\\\": [\\n          \\\"Use gene-level features (expression, co-expression, network centrality, essentiality proxies) to transfer information to unseen targets.\\\",\\n          \\\"Shrink predicted deltas toward zero with regularization tuned by held-out-perturbation CV; specify a shrinkage factor alpha in [0,1] multiplied to predicted deltas and tuned to maximize pearson_delta subject to MSE constraints.\\\",\\n          \\\"Model effect magnitude as a function of target expression and network degree.\\\",\\n          \\\"Use robust control mean estimation (trimmed mean or median-of-means) and per-gene variance weighting; select method by bootstrap stability.\\\",\\n          \\\"Quantify similarity between held-out and training perturbations using gene-level features to estimate predictability and set shrinkage per perturbation.\\\",\\n          \\\"Decompose predicted deltas into target-gene self-knockdown, module components, and residual to separate direct from downstream effects.\\\",\\n          \\\"Flag essential genes and no-effect perturbations as lower-confidence and shrink more aggressively.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Leave-one-perturbation-out CV on observed perturbations, stratified by effect size, target expression, essentiality, network centrality, and target-gene class, with pearson_delta and top-20 metrics computed identically to the official evaluator. Verify target-gene knockdown sign and magnitude where the target is expressed.\\\",\\n        \\\"biological_considerations\\\": \\\"Preserve the biology that CRISPRi primarily represses the target and its regulon; enforce non-positive direct-target effects where appropriate and allow downstream positive/negative changes. Predictions for essential genes are flagged as lower-confidence. Validate against K562-specific markers and known CRISPRi hits.\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"technical\\\": {\\n        \\\"challenges\\\": [\\n          \\\"High-dimensional output (n_genes per perturbation) with limited number of observed perturbations.\\\",\\n          \\\"Sparse, zero-inflated single-cell data and log1p CP10K scale.\\\",\\n          \\\"Uneven cell numbers per perturbation and potential batch effects.\\\",\\n          \\\"Strict requirement to match gene order and normalization exactly.\\\",\\n          \\\"No external data allowed, limiting pretrained transfer.\\\",\\n          \\\"Undefined correlations for zero-variance predictions in pearson_delta.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"impact\\\": \\\"Overfitting to observed perturbations, unstable predictions, and format errors that invalidate submission. Undefined correlations can bias metric computation if not handled.\\\",\\n        \\\"mitigation\\\": [\\n          \\\"Low-rank / factor models and ridge-regularized regression from perturbation features to delta vectors.\\\",\\n          \\\"Dimensionality reduction (PCA, NMF, gene modules) before regression; project back to full gene space.\\\",\\n          \\\"Explicit format checks: perts order equals heldout list, means shape and dtype float, gene order equals var_names.\\\",\\n          \\\"Deterministic seeding, pinned versions, and unit tests for preprocessing and output.\\\",\\n          \\\"Verify normalization state empirically (max value consistent with log1p CP10K, non-integer values) before modeling.\\\",\\n          \\\"Handle undefined correlations by assigning zero and reporting the fraction of undefined cases.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Unit tests on output schema; CV metrics; sanity check that predicted control-mean baseline reproduces input control mean within numerical tolerance; run pipeline twice with same seed to confirm identical outputs.\\\",\\n        \\\"implementation_requirements\\\": \\\"Python with anndata, scanpy, numpy, scipy, scikit-learn, pandas; CPU-feasible pipeline; fixed random seeds; pinned and logged versions of Python, anndata, scanpy, numpy, scipy, scikit-learn, pandas; unit tests for output format, gene order, and leakage checks.\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"data_quality\\\": {\\n        \\\"issues\\\": [\\n          \\\"Low and variable library sizes per cell.\\\",\\n          \\\"Zero inflation and dropout in log1p CP10K data.\\\",\\n          \\\"Potential doublets and low-quality cells.\\\",\\n          \\\"Batch/experiment structure in obs; batch-perturbation confounding not quantified.\\\",\\n          \\\"Held-out perturbations may be enriched for weak or essential genes.\\\",\\n          \\\"QC thresholds not derived from data distribution.\\\",\\n          \\\"Genes with zero expression in controls affect delta computation and correlation metrics.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"impact\\\": \\\"Noisy control mean and noisy per-perturbation means in training, biasing feature learning and calibration. Zero-expression genes can produce undefined correlations or inflated MSE.\\\",\\n        \\\"mitigation\\\": [\\n          \\\"Specify concrete QC threshold procedures: MAD-based filters on total counts and genes detected, mitochondrial fraction thresholds if mt genes are annotated, and doublet score cutoffs. Set thresholds at data-driven tails (e.g., 1st/99th percentile or MAD-based) after inspecting distributions.\\\",\\n          \\\"Robust control mean via trimmed mean or median-of-means; select method by bootstrap stability.\\\",\\n          \\\"Weight genes by detection rate and variance in loss/metrics; exclude zero-variance genes from correlation metrics but retain in output.\\\",\\n          \\\"Quantify batch-perturbation confounding by cross-tabulation and association statistics before deciding on batch correction; apply batch correction only if confounding is quantified and correction improves held-out perturbation metrics.\\\",\\n          \\\"Define criteria for excluding low-quality perturbations from training (e.g., <20 cells or low guide concordance) and document excluded perturbations; weight training perturbations by cell count and guide confidence.\\\",\\n          \\\"Document handling of zero-expression genes: exclude from correlation metrics where variance is zero, but retain in output.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Compare control mean estimates across batches and QC thresholds; bootstrap stability of gene features and predictions; compare held-out perturbation performance with and without QC filtering and with different thresholds; assess whether QC disproportionately removes cells from certain perturbations or cell states (e.g., compare cell-cycle phase and clustering composition before and after QC).\\\",\\n        \\\"biological_validation\\\": \\\"Check that top expressed genes and control mean match expected K562 markers (e.g., haemoglobin genes, BCR-ABL programme); compare known perturbation effects (e.g., target knockdown) before and after QC to ensure signal is preserved.\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"computational\\\": {\\n        \\\"challenges\\\": [\\n          \\\"Full gene set output requires predicting thousands of genes per perturbation.\\\",\\n          \\\"Cross-validation over perturbations multiplies training cost.\\\",\\n          \\\"Memory for dense cell x gene matrices and delta matrices.\\\",\\n          \\\"Hyperparameter tuning under strict no-leakage constraints.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"impact\\\": \\\"Long runtimes and risk of leakage if tuning uses held-out perturbations improperly.\\\",\\n        \\\"mitigation\\\": [\\n          \\\"Work in reduced gene space (PCA/modules) for modeling and project back to full gene space.\\\",\\n          \\\"Use efficient linear algebra (ridge regression, SVD) and sparse representations where possible.\\\",\\n          \\\"Nested CV: inner loop for hyperparameters, outer loop for evaluation.\\\",\\n          \\\"Cache control mean and feature matrices.\\\",\\n          \\\"Pilot cross-validation on a subset of training perturbations to estimate runtime and memory before full runs.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Profile runtime and memory; verify no held-out perturbation cells enter any fit; run pipeline twice with same seed to confirm identical outputs.\\\",\\n        \\\"resource_requirements\\\": \\\"Single workstation with >=32 GB RAM, multi-core CPU; optional GPU not required; total runtime target under a few hours.\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"interpretability\\\": {\\n        \\\"requirements\\\": [\\n          \\\"Predicted deltas should be attributable to target-gene features and network neighbourhood.\\\",\\n          \\\"Top-20 DE genes should be biologically plausible for each perturbation.\\\",\\n          \\\"Model should expose gene-module loadings and perturbation feature importances.\\\",\\n          \\\"Interpretability must be quantified: sign and magnitude of predicted target-gene delta, enrichment significance of known regulon members in top predicted DE genes (permutation test), and module loading coherence.\\\",\\n          \\\"Per-perturbation top modules and enriched pathways for held-out predictions are reported as standard output.\\\",\\n          \\\"Predicted deltas are decomposed into target-gene component, module components, and residual.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"challenges\\\": [\\n          \\\"Low-rank models can be opaque.\\\",\\n          \\\"Unseen perturbations lack direct validation of mechanism.\\\",\\n          \\\"Separating direct target knockdown from downstream effects.\\\"\\n\",\n  \"error\": \"Failed to parse JSON response\"\n}\n\nREVIEWER COMMENTS:\n{\n  \"note\": \"{\\n  \\\"dataset\\\": {\\n    \\\"biological\\\": {\\n      \\\"relevance\\\": {\\n        \\\"assessment\\\": \\\"The dataset analysis demonstrates strong biological relevance for the unseen-perturbation prediction task in K562 CRISPRi. It correctly identifies the key biological constraints: CRISPRi partial knockdown, cell-state heterogeneity, and the need to separate direct target repression from downstream regulon effects. The emphasis on K562-specific markers (BCR-ABL programme, haemoglobin genes) and stratified predictability by target-gene class (essential, TF, housekeeping, low-expression) is appropriate and aligns with the known biology of the Adamson et al. screen.\\\",\\n        \\\"issues\\\": [\\n          \\\"The control label(s) are not yet confirmed from obs metadata; the analysis assumes their existence and identity, which is a critical unresolved dependency.\\\",\\n          \\\"The distinction between direct target-gene self-knockdown and downstream regulon effects is described conceptually but not operationalized into concrete model components or validation metrics.\\\",\\n          \\\"The expected number and biological composition of held-out perturbations are unknown, limiting the ability to set feasibility expectations.\\\",\\n          \\\"The analysis does not specify how to handle perturbations with no measurable transcriptional effect beyond 'predict near-zero delta with calibrated shrinkage'\\u2014the calibration method is not defined.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"improvements\\\": [\\n          \\\"Confirm and document the exact control label(s) from obs metadata before any analysis; if multiple control types exist (e.g., NTC and safe-targeting), define the union and justify the choice.\\\",\\n          \\\"Operationalize the direct-vs-downstream decomposition: define a target-gene self-knockdown component (e.g., predicted delta for the target gene itself constrained to be non-positive) and a regulon/module component, and report each separately in validation.\\\",\\n          \\\"If the held-out perturbation list is available, characterize its composition (essential genes, TFs, etc.) and compare to training perturbation classes to estimate the proportion of out-of-distribution targets.\\\",\\n          \\\"Define a concrete calibrated shrinkage procedure: e.g., shrink predicted deltas by a factor alpha in [0,1] tuned via leave-one-perturbation-out CV to maximize pearson_delta subject to an MSE constraint, with alpha stratified by target-gene class and neighbor distance.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Biological validation should include: (1) confirmation that control cells express expected K562 markers (BCR, ABL1, HBB, HBG1, HBG2, known essential genes); (2) target-gene knockdown sign and magnitude checks for expressed targets in training perturbations; (3) permutation tests for enrichment of known regulon members among top predicted DE genes for held-out perturbations; (4) stratified reporting by target-gene class.\\\",\\n        \\\"biological_validation\\\": \\\"The analysis proposes appropriate K562-specific checks but should specify thresholds and statistical tests (e.g., permutation p-value < 0.05 for regulon enrichment, target-gene delta sign concordance rate > 0.8 for expressed targets). These criteria should be pre-registered before model evaluation to avoid post-hoc rationalization.\\\",\\n        \\\"decision_support_alignment\\\": \\\"The decision support blocks (model selection, evaluation strategy, data preparation, implementation plan, risk assessment) are empty. All biological relevance recommendations are therefore provisional. Once decision support is populated, the biological relevance criteria (e.g., required target knockdown sign, pathway enrichment thresholds) must be reconciled with the specified evaluation strategy and risk tolerance. The current analysis correctly flags this dependency.\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"interpretability\\\": {\\n        \\\"assessment\\\": \\\"The dataset analysis provides a thoughtful framework for interpretability, including decomposition of predicted deltas into target-gene, module, and residual components, and proposes quantified interpretability metrics (target-gene delta sign/magnitude, regulon enrichment significance, module loading coherence). This is well-aligned with the biological goal of understanding CRISPRi effects.\\\",\\n        \\\"issues\\\": [\\n          \\\"The proposed interpretability metrics are described but not fully specified with thresholds or reporting formats.\\\",\\n          \\\"The decomposition into target-gene, module, and residual components is not tied to a concrete model architecture or estimation procedure.\\\",\\n          \\\"Per-perturbation top modules and enriched pathways are proposed as standard output but the method for computing them (e.g., which gene sets, which enrichment test) is not specified.\\\",\\n          \\\"Interpretability for low-rank models is acknowledged as challenging but no concrete attribution method (e.g., SHAP, loading inspection) is recommended.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"improvements\\\": [\\n          \\\"Specify the model architecture that enables the decomposition: e.g., a linear model where the predicted delta for perturbation p is a sum of a target-gene-specific term (based on target identity/features), a module-loading term (based on co-expression modules), and a residual; report the variance explained by each component.\\\",\\n          \\\"Define the gene sets and enrichment method for per-perturbation pathway reporting (e.g., MSigDB Hallmark and Reactome via hypergeometric test or GSEA, with permutation-based FDR control).\\\",\\n          \\\"For low-rank models, recommend inspecting module loadings and perturbation scores, and computing gene-level attributions (e.g., contribution of each module to each perturbation's predicted delta).\\\",\\n          \\\"Include a standard interpretability report template: for each held-out perturbation, report target-gene predicted delta, top 5 enriched pathways with p-values, and top 10 predicted DE genes with module assignments.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Interpretability validation should include: (1) checking that predicted target-gene delta is non-positive for expressed targets (CRISPRi repression); (2) permutation-based significance of regulon enrichment; (3) consistency of module loadings across perturbations targeting genes in the same complex/pathway; (4) stability of top predicted DE genes across bootstrap resamples.\\\",\\n        \\\"biological_validation\\\": \\\"Interpretability claims should be validated against known K562 CRISPRi biology: e.g., if the target is a transcription factor, predicted DE genes should be enriched for its known targets; if the target is in a complex, co-complex members should show coherent effects. These checks should be reported as secondary validation, not used for model selection.\\\",\\n        \\\"decision_support_considerations\\\": \\\"The empty decision support blocks provide no constraints on interpretability requirements. Once populated, the model selection and evaluation strategy may specify whether interpretability is a hard requirement or a secondary consideration. The current analysis should be revisited to ensure the interpretability framework aligns with the decision support priorities (e.g., if the decision support emphasizes predictive accuracy over interpretability, the decomposition may be simplified).\\\"\\n      }\\n    },\\n    \\\"technical\\\": {\\n      \\\"quality\\\": {\\n        \\\"assessment\\\": \\\"The technical quality assessment is comprehensive, covering library-size variation, batch effects, ambient RNA, doublets, and guide-assignment confidence. The recommendation to verify the normalization state empirically (max value, non-integer values) is sound. The emphasis on not renormalizing a pre-normalized matrix is correct and critical for submission validity.\\\",\\n        \\\"issues\\\": [\\n          \\\"Concrete QC thresholds are not specified; the analysis recommends MAD-based filters but does not provide the exact procedure or parameter values.\\\",\\n          \\\"The handling of zero-expression genes is mentioned (exclude from correlation metrics, retain in output) but the fraction of such genes and their impact on MSE is not quantified.\\\",\\n          \\\"Batch-perturbation confounding is flagged as a risk but no concrete method for quantification (e.g., chi-square test, Cramer's V) is specified.\\\",\\n          \\\"The analysis assumes the matrix is log1p CP10K but does not specify the exact verification procedure (e.g., check that max value is consistent with log1p(10000) \\u2248 9.21, and that values are non-integer).\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"improvements\\\": [\\n          \\\"Specify concrete QC procedures: e.g., filter cells with total counts below the 1st percentile or above the 99th percentile (or MAD-based thresholds), genes detected below the 1st percentile, and mitochondrial fraction above a data-driven threshold (e.g., 3 MADs above median if mt genes are annotated). Document all thresholds and the number of cells/perturbations removed.\\\",\\n          \\\"Quantify the fraction of zero-expression genes in controls and their contribution to MSE; consider reporting MSE on expressed genes only as a secondary metric.\\\",\\n          \\\"Specify the batch-perturbation confounding test: e.g., cross-tabulate batch and perturbation labels, compute Cramer's V or chi-square p-value, and report the proportion of perturbations with unbalanced batch distribution.\\\",\\n          \\\"Define the normalization verification procedure explicitly: check that X.max() is approximately log1p(10000) \\u2248 9.21 (or consistent with the stated normalization), that values are non-integer, and that the minimum is 0 (or near 0).\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Technical validation should include: (1) comparison of control mean estimates across batches and QC thresholds; (2) bootstrap stability of control mean (e.g., standard deviation across 100 bootstrap resamples); (3) comparison of held-out perturbation performance with and without QC filtering; (4) assessment of whether QC disproportionately removes cells from certain perturbations or cell states (e.g., compare cell-cycle phase composition before and after QC).\\\",\\n        \\\"technical_validation\\\": \\\"The analysis correctly recommends bootstrap resampling of control cells to estimate control mean uncertainty. This should be extended to propagate uncertainty to delta predictions and to report confidence intervals for pearson_delta and top-20 metrics. Additionally, a sensitivity analysis across QC thresholds (e.g., 1st/99th percentile vs. 2 MADs vs. 3 MADs) should be performed to ensure conclusions are robust.\\\",\\n        \\\"decision_support_quality\\\": \\\"The empty decision support blocks provide no pre-specified quality thresholds or risk tolerance. The current analysis correctly defers threshold setting to the data distribution and recommends validation against held-out perturbation metrics. Once decision support is populated, the quality thresholds and exclusion criteria should be reconciled with the risk assessment (e.g., if the risk assessment prioritizes avoiding false positives, more aggressive QC may be warranted).\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"reproducibility\\\": {\\n        \\\"assessment\\\": \\\"The dataset analysis includes strong reproducibility recommendations: deterministic seeding, pinned versions, unit tests, bitwise comparison of repeated runs, and leakage checks. This is well-aligned with best practices for computational biology.\\\",\\n        \\\"issues\\\": [\\n          \\\"The specific versions of Python, anndata, scanpy, numpy, scipy, scikit-learn, and pandas are not pinned in the analysis (though the problem investigation mentions pinning).\\\",\\n          \\\"The unit tests for output schema are mentioned but not specified in detail (e.g., exact assertions for perts order, means shape, dtype, gene order).\\\",\\n          \\\"The leakage checks are described conceptually but no concrete procedure (e.g., hash-based verification that held-out perturbation cells are excluded) is specified.\\\",\\n          \\\"The reproducibility of stochastic methods (e.g., bootstrap, cross-validation folds) is not fully addressed.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"improvements\\\": [\\n          \\\"Specify exact package versions in a requirements.txt or conda environment file, and log them at runtime.\\\",\\n          \\\"Define concrete unit tests: e.g., assert that perts equals the heldout list exactly (order and values), means.shape == (n_perts, n_genes), means.dtype == float32 or float64, var_names order matches the input, and all predicted means are finite and within the range of the input log1p CP10K values.\\\",\\n          \\\"Implement a leakage check: e.g., verify that the set of perturbation labels used in training does not intersect with the held-out set, and that no cells with held-out perturbation labels are present in the training data (by construction, but verify programmatically).\\\",\\n          \\\"For stochastic methods, fix seeds at the start of each run and document the seed; for cross-validation, use a deterministic fold assignment (e.g., sorted perturbation labels, then round-robin assignment) to ensure reproducibility across runs.\\\",\\n          \\\"Run the full pipeline twice with the same seed and compare outputs bitwise (or within a numerical tolerance for floating-point operations) to confirm determinism.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Reproducibility validation should include: (1) bitwise or near-bitwise comparison of outputs across two runs with the same seed; (2) verification that the pipeline produces identical results when run on a different machine with the same environment; (3) documentation of all random seeds and their usage; (4) a manifest of input file hashes and output file hashes.\\\",\\n        \\\"technical_validation\\\": \\\"The analysis correctly recommends running the pipeline twice with fixed seeds. This should be extended to a full reproducibility checklist: environment file, seed documentation, input/output hashes, and a README with step-by-step instructions to reproduce the submission.\\\",\\n        \\\"decision_support_reproducibility\\\": \\\"The empty decision support blocks provide no specific reproducibility standards (e.g., required logging, version pinning). The current analysis recommends best practices, but these should be reconciled with the eventual implementation plan and risk assessment. If the decision support specifies a reproducibility standard (e.g., 'must be reproducible within 1% tolerance'), the analysis should be updated to meet it.\\\"\\n      }\\n    },\\n    \\\"preprocessing\\\": {\\n      \\\"recommendations\\\": {\\n        \\\"assessment\\\": \\\"The preprocessing recommendations are generally sound: verify normalization state, filter genes consistently, preserve gene order, and handle zero-expression genes appropriately. The emphasis on not renormalizing the pre-normalized matrix is correct.\\\",\\n        \\\"issues\\\": [\\n          \\\"The gene filtering strategy is not fully specified: the analysis recommends filtering genes for internal modeling but retaining all genes in the output, but does not specify which genes to filter (e.g., HVGs, expressed genes) or how many.\\\",\\n          \\\"The handling of zero-expression genes is mentioned but the exact procedure for correlation metrics (e.g., exclude genes with zero variance in either predicted or true delta) is not fully specified.\\\",\\n          \\\"The analysis does not address whether to filter cells for training and how this interacts with the control mean computation (e.g., should control cells be filtered with the same criteria as perturbed cells?).\\\",\\n          \\\"The recommendation to 'filter genes consistently' is ambiguous: consistently across what? Training and test? Control and perturbed cells?\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"improvements\\\": [\\n          \\\"Specify the gene filtering strategy for internal modeling: e.g., use highly variable genes (HVGs, top 2000-5000 by variance) or genes expressed in at least 1% of control cells for model training, but always output predictions for all genes in var_names order. Document the number of genes used for modeling vs. output.\\\",\\n          \\\"Specify the zero-variance handling for correlation metrics: exclude genes where either the predicted or true delta has zero variance across the perturbation set (or across genes for per-perturbation correlation), and report the fraction of excluded genes. For per-perturbation pearson_delta, if the predicted delta is all zeros, assign correlation zero and report the fraction of such cases.\\\",\\n          \\\"Specify whether cell filtering is applied uniformly to all cells (including controls) or separately; recommend uniform filtering to avoid bias in control mean estimation, and document the number of cells removed per perturbation.\\\",\\n          \\\"Clarify 'consistently': gene filtering for modeling should be determined from training data only (e.g., HVGs computed on training cells), and the same gene set should be used for all perturbations; the output always includes all genes.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Preprocessing validation should include: (1) verification that the normalization state is log1p CP10K (max value \\u2248 9.21, non-integer values); (2) comparison of control mean computed from the full matrix vs. the filtered matrix to ensure filtering does not introduce bias; (3) sensitivity analysis of held-out perturbation metrics to gene filtering choices (e.g., all genes vs. HVGs vs. expressed genes).\\\",\\n        \\\"implementation\\\": \\\"Implement preprocessing as a documented pipeline: (1) load AnnData; (2) verify normalization; (3) compute QC metrics (total counts, genes detected, mitochondrial fraction if available); (4) apply cell filters (MAD-based thresholds); (5) compute control mean from filtered control cells; (6) identify genes for modeling (HVGs or expressed genes) from training data only; (7) save the filtered AnnData and control mean for downstream use. Log all parameters and the number of cells/genes removed.\\\",\\n        \\\"decision_support_preprocessing\\\": \\\"The empty decision support blocks provide no constraints on preprocessing (e.g., required normalization, filtering thresholds). The current recommendations are provisional and should be reconciled with the data preparation block once populated. If the decision support specifies a particular normalization or filtering strategy, the analysis should be updated accordingly.\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"validation\\\": {\\n        \\\"assessment\\\": \\\"The preprocessing validation recommendations are reasonable: verify normalization empirically, check control mean consistency, and perform sensitivity analysis. However, the validation is not fully specified with concrete procedures.\\\",\\n        \\\"issues\\\": [\\n          \\\"The normalization verification procedure is described conceptually but not with exact checks (e.g., max value, non-integer check).\\\",\\n          \\\"The control mean consistency check is mentioned but not specified (e.g., compare control mean computed from raw vs. filtered cells, or from different batches).\\\",\\n          \\\"The sensitivity analysis of held-out perturbation metrics to preprocessing choices is recommended but not detailed (e.g., which choices to vary, how to report results).\\\",\\n          \\\"The analysis does not address validation of gene filtering for modeling (e.g., whether the selected genes capture the perturbation signal).\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"improvements\\\": [\\n          \\\"Specify the normalization verification checks: assert that X.max() is approximately log1p(10000) \\u2248 9.21 (within a tolerance, e.g., \\u00b10.1), that X.min() >= 0, and that the fraction of integer values is low (e.g., < 1%).\\\",\\n          \\\"Specify the control mean consistency check: compute control mean from (a) all control cells, (b) control cells passing QC, (c) control cells from each batch (if multiple batches); report the correlation and maximum absolute difference between these estimates.\\\",\\n          \\\"Specify the sensitivity analysis: vary QC thresholds (e.g., 1st/99th percentile vs. 2 MADs vs. 3 MADs), gene filtering (all genes vs. HVGs vs. expressed genes), and batch correction (none vs. control-anchored); report held-out perturbation metrics (pearson_delta, top-20 PCC) for each combination.\\\",\\n          \\\"Validate gene filtering for modeling: check that the selected genes include the target genes of training perturbations (if expressed) and that the top DE genes for training perturbations are captured; report the overlap between HVGs and known perturbation-responsive genes.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Preprocessing validation should include: (1) normalization verification (max value, non-integer check); (2) control mean consistency across QC thresholds and batches; (3) sensitivity analysis of held-out perturbation metrics to preprocessing choices; (4) gene filtering validation (coverage of perturbation-responsive genes).\\\",\\n        \\\"implementation\\\": \\\"Implement validation as a set of unit tests and a sensitivity analysis script: (1) unit tests for normalization checks; (2) a script that computes control mean under different QC thresholds and reports consistency metrics; (3) a script that runs the full pipeline with different preprocessing choices and reports held-out perturbation metrics; (4) a report summarizing the results and recommending a final preprocessing configuration.\\\",\\n        \\\"decision_support_validation\\\": \\\"The empty decision support blocks provide no specific validation requirements. The current\",\n  \"parse_error\": \"Failed to parse JSON response\"\n}\n\nRequirements:\n1. Keep the same high-level structure when possible.\n2. Resolve issues called out in the comments.\n3. Add explicit assumptions where information is missing.\n4. Return only valid JSON (no markdown).\n"
