      "content": "You are refining the Baseline Assessment for a scientific task analysis.\nApply the reviewer comments to improve accuracy, completeness, and implementation usefulness.\n\nSECTION TO REFINE:\n{\n  \"content\": \"{\\n  \\\"content\\\": {\\n    \\\"baseline_models\\\": {\\n      \\\"literature_review\\\": {\\n        \\\"existing_methods\\\": {\\n          \\\"methods\\\": [\\n            \\\"Control-mean (zero-delta) baseline: predict the mean expression of control cells for every held-out perturbation; delta prediction is zero. Control cells are defined by the exact control label(s) in obs metadata; these must be confirmed before analysis (see assumptions).\\\",\\n            \\\"Global-mean-delta baseline: predict the average delta (perturbation mean minus control mean) across all training perturbations for every held-out perturbation.\\\",\\n            \\\"Per-gene linear/ridge regression on perturbation one-hot encoding: fit gene-wise models on training perturbation means; for unseen perturbations, predictions reduce to the control mean (or training-perturbation mean) with gene-wise shrinkage.\\\",\\n            \\\"Latent linear models: PCA on training perturbation means followed by ridge regression from perturbation descriptors (if available) or nearest-neighbor transfer in PCA space.\\\",\\n            \\\"Nearest-neighbor baseline: match held-out perturbations to training perturbations using gene-level features (e.g., control-centered mean profiles, target-gene embeddings) and transfer the closest training perturbation effect; quantify neighbor distance to estimate predictability.\\\",\\n            \\\"Descriptor-based ridge/kernel regression: if target-gene features are available (e.g., expression level, essentiality, network centrality, gene embeddings), regress perturbation effects on descriptors.\\\",\\n            \\\"Low-rank matrix factorization (e.g., truncated SVD, NMF) of the perturbation-by-gene effect matrix to share statistical strength across perturbations.\\\",\\n            \\\"Graph/network propagation baselines: build a gene co-expression or PPI graph and propagate known perturbation effects to unseen perturbations using network similarity; validate against over-smoothing.\\\",\\n            \\\"Autoencoder/VAE latent-space models: train on single-cell expression, then model perturbation effects in latent space with simple regressors (only if sufficient perturbations exist).\\\",\\n            \\\"Set-transformer/attention models: treat perturbation as a token and predict gene-wise deltas via cross-attention over training perturbations (only if sufficient training perturbations exist).\\\"\\n          ],\\n          \\\"strengths\\\": [\\n            \\\"Control-mean and global-delta baselines are robust, fast, and establish lower bounds for pearson_delta and MSE.\\\",\\n            \\\"Linear/ridge and low-rank models are interpretable and computationally cheap, suitable for high-dimensional gene space with limited perturbations.\\\",\\n            \\\"Nearest-neighbor methods can capture similarity between perturbations if informative features exist; neighbor distance provides a predictability estimate.\\\",\\n            \\\"Graph-based methods incorporate biological prior knowledge and may generalize to unseen perturbations with similar network neighborhoods.\\\",\\n            \\\"Descriptor-based models can leverage target-gene properties (expression, essentiality, network centrality) to stratify predictability.\\\"\\n          ],\\n          \\\"limitations\\\": [\\n            \\\"No major perturbation-prediction methods were identified in the retrieved literature, so the benchmark relies on generic baselines and must be constructed carefully.\\\",\\n            \\\"Linear models cannot capture nonlinear or combinatorial perturbation effects.\\\",\\n            \\\"Nearest-neighbor methods depend heavily on informative perturbation descriptors; in CRISPRi K562, held-out perturbations may have no close training neighbors, especially essential genes or transcription factors with no analogs.\\\",\\n            \\\"Graph propagation requires high-quality networks and may over-smooth effects.\\\",\\n            \\\"Deep generative/attention models require large training data and risk overfitting given the limited number of perturbations.\\\",\\n            \\\"Most methods struggle with unseen-perturbation generalization because perturbation effects are highly gene- and context-specific; perturbations with no measurable effect or lethal phenotypes are particularly challenging.\\\"\\n          ],\\n          \\\"biological_relevance\\\": \\\"CRISPRi perturbations in K562 target specific genes; prediction should capture downstream transcriptional consequences. Baselines that shrink toward control mean are biologically conservative but may miss real effects. Predictability should be stratified by target-gene properties (expression, essentiality, network centrality) because essential genes may produce strong or lethal phenotypes not captured by mean expression alone. Graph/network methods can leverage known regulatory relationships, but validation must focus on top DE genes and pathway-level effects rather than global correlation alone. Operationalize direct vs. downstream effects by separating target-gene self-knockdown from regulon modules in the model and validation.\\\"\\n        },\\n        \\\"model_comparison\\\": {\\n          \\\"comparison_criteria\\\": [\\n            \\\"Pearson correlation of predicted vs. true delta from control per perturbation (primary).\\\",\\n            \\\"MSE on mean expression and on delta, with calibration (slope/intercept) reported.\\\",\\n            \\\"Top-20 DE gene recovery: MSE, PCC, R2 on top-20 DE genes, where top-20 DE genes are defined by the largest absolute true delta as implied by the evaluator.\\\",\\n            \\\"Computational cost and scalability to ~10k genes and hundreds of perturbations.\\\",\\n            \\\"Robustness to biological and technical variability (batch, cell-cycle, library size) and to control-cell subsampling.\\\",\\n            \\\"Biological interpretability: whether predicted effects align with known pathways and gene function, including sign and magnitude of predicted target-gene delta.\\\",\\n            \\\"Stratified performance by target-gene properties (expression, essentiality, network centrality) and by neighbor distance to training perturbations.\\\"\\n          ],\\n          \\\"performance_metrics\\\": [\\n            \\\"pearson_delta (per perturbation, then averaged; report undefined/zero handling: assign zero or exclude and report fraction of undefined cases).\\\",\\n            \\\"MSE (global and per perturbation) and calibration slope/intercept of predicted vs. true delta.\\\",\\n            \\\"Top-20 DE MSE, PCC, R2.\\\",\\n            \\\"Spearman rank correlation of gene effects.\\\",\\n            \\\"Precision@k and recall@k for top-k DE genes (k=20, 50, 100).\\\",\\n            \\\"Per-gene correlation across perturbations.\\\",\\n            \\\"Bootstrap confidence intervals for pearson_delta and top-20 DE metrics.\\\"\\n          ],\\n          \\\"biological_interpretability\\\": \\\"Linear, low-rank, and nearest-neighbor models are most interpretable (effect = weighted sum of training effects, module loadings, or direct transfer). Graph methods provide pathway-level interpretation. Deep models are less interpretable unless attention or gene-level attributions are used. Define interpretability metrics: sign and magnitude of predicted target-gene delta, enrichment of known regulon members in top predicted DE genes (permutation test), and module loading coherence. Decompose predicted deltas into target-gene component, module components, and residual to separate direct from downstream effects. Report per-perturbation top modules and enriched pathways for held-out predictions as a standard output.\\\",\\n          \\\"computational_requirements\\\": \\\"Control-mean and global-delta baselines require negligible computation. Ridge/PCA, low-rank, and nearest-neighbor methods scale to thousands of genes and hundreds of perturbations with standard CPU resources. Graph propagation may require network preprocessing but remains feasible. Deep attention/VAE models require GPU training and careful hyperparameter tuning; given the small number of perturbations, they risk overfitting and are not recommended as first-line baselines. Quantify memory requirements and recommend sparse representations or reduced gene space for modeling.\\\"\\n        }\\n      },\\n      \\\"recommendations\\\": {\\n        \\\"model_selection\\\": [\\n          \\\"Start with control-mean (zero delta) and global-mean-delta baselines as mandatory sanity checks.\\\",\\n          \\\"Implement ridge regression on perturbation means with gene-wise shrinkage as a strong linear baseline.\\\",\\n          \\\"Implement low-rank matrix factorization (e.g., truncated SVD) of the perturbation-by-gene effect matrix to share statistical strength.\\\",\\n          \\\"Implement nearest-neighbor transfer in a reduced latent space (PCA on training perturbation means) and report neighbor distances to estimate predictability.\\\",\\n          \\\"If perturbation descriptors (e.g., target-gene expression, essentiality, network centrality, gene embeddings) are available, implement descriptor-based ridge or kernel regression.\\\",\\n          \\\"Only then consider deep models (set transformer, VAE) if sufficient training perturbations exist and validation shows gains over linear baselines.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"implementation_priority\\\": [\\n          \\\"1. Confirm and document the exact control label(s) from obs metadata before any analysis; if multiple control types exist, define the union and justify the choice. Reproduce the evaluator exactly: compute control mean from training control cells, define delta as perturbation mean minus control mean on the log1p CP10k scale, and evaluate on held-out perturbations using pearson_delta, MSE, and top-20 DE metrics.\\\",\\n          \\\"2. Build a robust preprocessing pipeline: verify normalization state (max value consistent with log1p CP10k, non-integer values), filter genes consistently, and ensure gene order matches cellforge_input.h5ad var_names. Document handling of zero-expression genes: exclude from correlation metrics where variance is zero but retain in output.\\\",\\n          \\\"3. Implement and tune linear/ridge and low-rank baselines with leave-one-perturbation-out cross-validation over training perturbations. Use nested cross-validation (inner loop for hyperparameters, outer loop for evaluation) to avoid leakage during tuning.\\\",\\n          \\\"4. Add nearest-neighbor and descriptor-based baselines; quantify neighbor distance to training perturbations.\\\",\\n          \\\"5. Evaluate deep models only if they beat linear baselines in cross-validation.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"biological_validation\\\": \\\"Validate predictions by checking enrichment of known K562 CRISPRi effects (e.g., knockdown of essential genes, pathway members) in top predicted DE genes. Confirm that predicted target knockdown appears for expressed targets and that top modules match known regulons. Compare predicted deltas for held-out perturbations to known gene-function relationships where available. Use pathway enrichment and gene-set enrichment analysis (MSigDB, Reactome) as secondary biological checks with permutation tests for enrichment significance. Add K562-specific biological checks: expected expression of BCR-ABL programme genes, haemoglobin genes, and known essential genes; verify these in control cells. Stratify biological relevance assessment by target-gene class (essential, TF, housekeeping, low-expression) to set expectations for predictability.\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"assumptions\\\": [\\n        \\\"The exact control label(s) in obs metadata are not confirmed; we assume they will be identified and documented before analysis. If multiple control types exist, their union will be used and justified.\\\",\\n        \\\"The number and biological composition of held-out perturbations are unknown; we assume they are representative of the training perturbation classes (essential genes, transcription factors, housekeeping, low-expression) to set feasibility expectations.\\\",\\n        \\\"The matrix is pre-normalized (log1p CP10k); we assume no renormalization is needed and verify empirically (max value, non-integer values).\\\"\\n      ]\\n    },\\n    \\\"evaluation_framework\\\": {\\n      \\\"metrics\\\": {\\n        \\\"primary_metrics\\\": [\\n          \\\"pearson_delta: Pearson correlation between predicted and true delta from control, per held-out perturbation, averaged across perturbations; handle undefined correlations (e.g., zero-variance predictions) explicitly by assigning zero or excluding and reporting the fraction of undefined cases.\\\",\\n          \\\"MSE on mean expression (n_perts x n_genes).\\\",\\n          \\\"Top-20 DE gene MSE, PCC, and R2 (computed per perturbation and averaged), where top-20 DE genes are defined by the largest absolute true delta.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"secondary_metrics\\\": [\\n          \\\"MSE on delta (perturbation mean minus control mean).\\\",\\n          \\\"Spearman rank correlation of gene effects.\\\",\\n          \\\"Precision@k and recall@k for top-k DE genes (k=20, 50, 100).\\\",\\n          \\\"Calibration error: slope/intercept of predicted vs. true delta.\\\",\\n          \\\"Per-gene correlation across perturbations.\\\",\\n          \\\"Bootstrap confidence intervals for pearson_delta and top-20 DE metrics.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"biological_metrics\\\": [\\n          \\\"Pathway/gene-set enrichment of top predicted DE genes vs. known CRISPRi targets, with permutation-based significance.\\\",\\n          \\\"Recovery of known downstream targets of the perturbed gene.\\\",\\n          \\\"Directional consistency of predicted effects for known activating/repressing relationships (predominant repression expected for CRISPRi).\\\",\\n          \\\"Network proximity of predicted DE genes to the perturbed gene in a PPI/regulatory network.\\\",\\n          \\\"Sign and magnitude of predicted target-gene delta for expressed targets.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"validation_metrics\\\": [\\n          \\\"Cross-validation over training perturbations: hold out a subset of training perturbations and compute the same metrics.\\\",\\n          \\\"Bootstrap confidence intervals for pearson_delta and top-20 DE metrics.\\\",\\n          \\\"Sensitivity to normalization and gene filtering choices.\\\",\\n          \\\"Robustness to control-cell subsampling and batch effects.\\\",\\n          \\\"Stratified performance by target-gene expression, essentiality, network centrality, and neighbor distance to training perturbations.\\\"\\n        ]\\n      },\\n      \\\"validation_strategy\\\": {\\n        \\\"cross_validation\\\": \\\"Use leave-one-perturbation-out or k-fold cross-validation over the training perturbations to tune model hyperparameters and estimate generalization. Because held-out perturbations are entirely unseen, cross-validation must mimic this setting: never use the held-out perturbation's cells or mean in training. Use nested cross-validation (inner loop for hyperparameters, outer loop for evaluation) to avoid leakage during tuning. Stratify folds by target-gene expression, essentiality, and network centrality to assess performance gradients. Report mean and standard deviation of metrics across folds.\\\",\\n        \\\"biological_validation\\\": \\\"For each held-out perturbation, compare predicted top DE genes to known biology of the targeted gene (e.g., if the target is a transcription factor, check enrichment of its known targets). Use independent pathway databases (MSigDB, Reactome) and, where possible, external CRISPRi screens in K562 as a sanity check, but do not use external data for training. Use permutation tests to assess enrichment significance. Verify that control cells express expected K562 markers (BCR-ABL programme genes, haemoglobin genes, known essential genes) and that training perturbations show target-gene knockdown where the target is expressed.\\\",\\n        \\\"independent_testing\\\": \\\"The provided held-out_perturbations.json is the primary independent test set. Additionally, create an internal held-out set from training perturbations (e.g., 20% of perturbations) to estimate performance before final submission. Ensure no data leakage: control mean must be computed only from training control cells, gene filtering must be consistent across train and test, and held-out perturbation labels and cells must be excluded from all fits, including feature construction and hyperparameter tuning.\\\",\\n        \\\"robustness_testing\\\": \\\"Evaluate model sensitivity to: (1) control-cell subsampling (bootstrap control mean), (2) gene filtering thresholds, (3) normalization choices (CP10k vs. library-size normalization), (4) random seeds for stochastic models, and (5) batch/plate effects if metadata are available. Quantify batch-perturbation confounding by cross-tabulating batch and perturbation labels and computing association statistics before deciding on batch correction. Report variance of metrics across these perturbations.\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"benchmarks\\\": {\\n        \\\"baseline_models\\\": [\\n          \\\"Control mean (zero delta)\\\",\\n          \\\"Global mean delta (average delta across all training perturbations)\\\",\\n          \\\"Gene-wise linear regression on perturbation one-hot encoding (predicts training mean, reduces to control mean for unseen)\\\",\\n          \\\"Ridge regression on perturbation means with gene-wise shrinkage\\\",\\n          \\\"Low-rank matrix factorization (e.g., truncated SVD) of the perturbation-by-gene effect matrix\\\",\\n          \\\"Nearest-neighbor transfer in PCA space of training perturbation means, with neighbor distance reported\\\",\\n          \\\"Descriptor-based ridge/kernel regression if perturbation features (target expression, essentiality, network centrality, gene embeddings) are available\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"state_of_the_art\\\": [\\n          \\\"No state-of-the-art perturbation-prediction methods were identified in the retrieved literature; the benchmark should be constructed from the baselines above and any recent general-purpose methods (e.g., linear mixed models, matrix factorization) applied to this dataset.\\\",\\n          \\\"If external methods are considered, they must be retrained only on cellforge_input.h5ad to respect the task constraints.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"biological_benchmarks\\\": [\\n          \\\"Known CRISPRi knockdown effects in K562 for housekeeping/essential genes.\\\",\\n          \\\"Pathway-level enrichment of predicted DE genes for held-out perturbations with permutation-based significance.\\\",\\n          \\\"Consistency with known gene regulatory relationships (e.g., transcription factor targets).\\\",\\n          \\\"Sign and magnitude of predicted target-gene delta for expressed targets.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"performance_targets\\\": [\\n          \\\"Beat control-mean baseline on pearson_delta (i.e., positive correlation on average).\\\",\\n          \\\"Achieve top-20 DE PCC significantly above zero and above the global-delta baseline.\\\",\\n          \\\"Maintain MSE no worse than the control-mean baseline (since predicting zero delta minimizes MSE if effects are small), or report calibrated shrinkage that trades MSE for pearson_delta.\\\",\\n          \\\"Demonstrate stable performance across cross-validation folds (low variance) and across bootstrap control subsamples.\\\"\\n        ]\\n      }\\n    },\\n    \\\"performance_analysis\\\": {\\n      \\\"model_performance\\\": {\\n        \\\"accuracy_metrics\\\": [\\n          \\\"Expected: control-mean baseline yields pearson_delta near zero (undefined or zero) and low MSE; global-delta baseline may yield slightly positive pearson_delta if perturbations share common effects.\\\",\\n          \\\"Linear/ridge and low-rank models may achieve modest positive pearson_delta if perturbation effects are additive or low-rank and training perturbations are representative.\\\",\\n          \\\"Nearest-neighbor methods may outperform linear models if held-out perturbations have close training neighbors, but likely fail for distinct perturbations; report neighbor distance as a predictability estimate.\\\",\\n          \\\"Top-20 DE metrics are expected to be challenging; even small positive PCC indicates some signal.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"biological_relevance\\\": \\\"Models that predict zero delta are biologically conservative but miss real effects. Models that capture shared stress/global responses may achieve higher pearson_delta but may not recover perturbation-specific DE genes. Biological relevance should be assessed by whether predicted DE genes are enriched for the perturbed gene's known pathway and whether predicted target knockdown appears for expressed targets. Stratify by target-gene class (essential, TF, housekeeping, low-expression) to set expectations for predictability.\\\",\\n        \\\"generalization\\\": \\\"Generalization to unseen perturbations is the core challenge. Performance will likely be low for perturbations with no close training analogs, especially essential genes or transcription factors with no analogs. Cross-validation over training perturbations will overestimate performance if perturbations are clustered; use leave-cluster-out or leave-one-out and stratify by target-gene properties to better mimic unseen setting.\\\",\\n        \\\"robustness\\\": \\\"Performance is expected to be sensitive to control-mean estimation (cell numbers), gene filtering, and normalization. Deep models may be less robust due to overfitting. Linear models with shrinkage and low-rank models are more stable. Use bootstrap resampling of control cells to estimate uncertainty in control mean and propagate to delta predictions.\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"comparative_analysis\\\": {\\n        \\\"baseline_comparison\\\": \\\"Control-mean and global-delta baselines are essential reference points. Any model that does not beat them on pearson_delta and top-20 DE metrics provides no added value. Ridge regression typically improves over ordinary least squares by stabilizing gene-wise estimates. Low-rank models share statistical strength across perturbations. Nearest-neighbor methods can outperform linear models when perturbation effects are locally similar in latent space; report neighbor distances.\\\",\\n        \\\"state_of_the_art_comparison\\\": \\\"Without identified state-of-the-art methods, comparison is against the constructed baselines. If deep models are implemented, they must be compared under identical preprocessing and evaluation; given limited training perturbations, they are unlikely to outperform well-tuned linear baselines.\\\",\\n        \\\"biological_comparison\\\": \\\"Biological comparison should focus on pathway-level recovery rather than global correlation. A model with lower pearson_delta but better recovery of known targets may be\",\n  \"error\": \"Failed to parse JSON response\"\n}\n\nREVIEWER COMMENTS:\n{\n  \"note\": \"{\\n  \\\"dataset\\\": {\\n    \\\"biological\\\": {\\n      \\\"relevance\\\": {\\n        \\\"assessment\\\": \\\"The dataset analysis demonstrates strong biological relevance for the unseen-perturbation prediction task in K562 CRISPRi. It correctly identifies the key biological constraints: CRISPRi partial knockdown, cell-state heterogeneity, and the need to separate direct target repression from downstream regulon effects. The emphasis on K562-specific markers (BCR-ABL programme, haemoglobin genes) and stratified predictability by target-gene class (essential, TF, housekeeping, low-expression) is appropriate and aligns with the known biology of the Adamson et al. screen.\\\",\\n        \\\"issues\\\": [\\n          \\\"The control label(s) are not yet confirmed from obs metadata; the analysis assumes their existence and identity, which is a critical unresolved dependency.\\\",\\n          \\\"The distinction between direct target-gene self-knockdown and downstream regulon effects is described conceptually but not operationalized into concrete model components or validation metrics.\\\",\\n          \\\"The expected number and biological composition of held-out perturbations are unknown, limiting the ability to set feasibility expectations.\\\",\\n          \\\"The analysis does not specify how to handle perturbations with no measurable transcriptional effect beyond 'predict near-zero delta with calibrated shrinkage'\\u2014the calibration method is not defined.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"improvements\\\": [\\n          \\\"Confirm and document the exact control label(s) from obs metadata before any analysis; if multiple control types exist (e.g., NTC and safe-targeting), define the union and justify the choice.\\\",\\n          \\\"Operationalize the direct-vs-downstream decomposition: define a target-gene self-knockdown component (e.g., predicted delta for the target gene itself constrained to be non-positive) and a regulon/module component, and report each separately in validation.\\\",\\n          \\\"If the held-out perturbation list is available, characterize its composition (essential genes, TFs, etc.) and compare to training perturbation classes to estimate the proportion of out-of-distribution targets.\\\",\\n          \\\"Define a concrete calibrated shrinkage procedure: e.g., shrink predicted deltas by a factor alpha in [0,1] tuned via leave-one-perturbation-out CV to maximize pearson_delta subject to an MSE constraint, with alpha stratified by target-gene class and neighbor distance.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Biological validation should include: (1) confirmation that control cells express expected K562 markers (BCR, ABL1, HBB, HBG1, HBG2, known essential genes); (2) target-gene knockdown sign and magnitude checks for expressed targets in training perturbations; (3) permutation tests for enrichment of known regulon members among top predicted DE genes for held-out perturbations; (4) stratified reporting by target-gene class.\\\",\\n        \\\"biological_validation\\\": \\\"The analysis proposes appropriate K562-specific checks but should specify thresholds and statistical tests (e.g., permutation p-value < 0.05 for regulon enrichment, target-gene delta sign concordance rate > 0.8 for expressed targets). These criteria should be pre-registered before model evaluation to avoid post-hoc rationalization.\\\",\\n        \\\"decision_support_alignment\\\": \\\"The decision support blocks (model selection, evaluation strategy, data preparation, implementation plan, risk assessment) are empty. All biological relevance recommendations are therefore provisional. Once decision support is populated, the biological relevance criteria (e.g., required target knockdown sign, pathway enrichment thresholds) must be reconciled with the specified evaluation strategy and risk tolerance. The current analysis correctly flags this dependency.\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"interpretability\\\": {\\n        \\\"assessment\\\": \\\"The dataset analysis provides a thoughtful framework for interpretability, including decomposition of predicted deltas into target-gene, module, and residual components, and proposes quantified interpretability metrics (target-gene delta sign/magnitude, regulon enrichment significance, module loading coherence). This is well-aligned with the biological goal of understanding CRISPRi effects.\\\",\\n        \\\"issues\\\": [\\n          \\\"The proposed interpretability metrics are described but not fully specified with thresholds or reporting formats.\\\",\\n          \\\"The decomposition into target-gene, module, and residual components is not tied to a concrete model architecture or estimation procedure.\\\",\\n          \\\"Per-perturbation top modules and enriched pathways are proposed as standard output but the method for computing them (e.g., which gene sets, which enrichment test) is not specified.\\\",\\n          \\\"Interpretability for low-rank models is acknowledged as challenging but no concrete attribution method (e.g., SHAP, loading inspection) is recommended.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"improvements\\\": [\\n          \\\"Specify the model architecture that enables the decomposition: e.g., a linear model where the predicted delta for perturbation p is a sum of a target-gene-specific term (based on target identity/features), a module-loading term (based on co-expression modules), and a residual; report the variance explained by each component.\\\",\\n          \\\"Define the gene sets and enrichment method for per-perturbation pathway reporting (e.g., MSigDB Hallmark and Reactome via hypergeometric test or GSEA, with permutation-based FDR control).\\\",\\n          \\\"For low-rank models, recommend inspecting module loadings and perturbation scores, and computing gene-level attributions (e.g., contribution of each module to each perturbation's predicted delta).\\\",\\n          \\\"Include a standard interpretability report template: for each held-out perturbation, report target-gene predicted delta, top 5 enriched pathways with p-values, and top 10 predicted DE genes with module assignments.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Interpretability validation should include: (1) checking that predicted target-gene delta is non-positive for expressed targets (CRISPRi repression); (2) permutation-based significance of regulon enrichment; (3) consistency of module loadings across perturbations targeting genes in the same complex/pathway; (4) stability of top predicted DE genes across bootstrap resamples.\\\",\\n        \\\"biological_validation\\\": \\\"Interpretability claims should be validated against known K562 CRISPRi biology: e.g., if the target is a transcription factor, predicted DE genes should be enriched for its known targets; if the target is in a complex, co-complex members should show coherent effects. These checks should be reported as secondary validation, not used for model selection.\\\",\\n        \\\"decision_support_considerations\\\": \\\"The empty decision support blocks provide no constraints on interpretability requirements. Once populated, the model selection and evaluation strategy may specify whether interpretability is a hard requirement or a secondary consideration. The current analysis should be revisited to ensure the interpretability framework aligns with the decision support priorities (e.g., if the decision support emphasizes predictive accuracy over interpretability, the decomposition may be simplified).\\\"\\n      }\\n    },\\n    \\\"technical\\\": {\\n      \\\"quality\\\": {\\n        \\\"assessment\\\": \\\"The technical quality assessment is comprehensive, covering library-size variation, batch effects, ambient RNA, doublets, and guide-assignment confidence. The recommendation to verify the normalization state empirically (max value, non-integer values) is sound. The emphasis on not renormalizing a pre-normalized matrix is correct and critical for submission validity.\\\",\\n        \\\"issues\\\": [\\n          \\\"Concrete QC thresholds are not specified; the analysis recommends MAD-based filters but does not provide the exact procedure or parameter values.\\\",\\n          \\\"The handling of zero-expression genes is mentioned (exclude from correlation metrics, retain in output) but the fraction of such genes and their impact on MSE is not quantified.\\\",\\n          \\\"Batch-perturbation confounding is flagged as a risk but no concrete method for quantification (e.g., chi-square test, Cramer's V) is specified.\\\",\\n          \\\"The analysis assumes the matrix is log1p CP10K but does not specify the exact verification procedure (e.g., check that max value is consistent with log1p(10000) \\u2248 9.21, and that values are non-integer).\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"improvements\\\": [\\n          \\\"Specify concrete QC procedures: e.g., filter cells with total counts below the 1st percentile or above the 99th percentile (or MAD-based thresholds), genes detected below the 1st percentile, and mitochondrial fraction above a data-driven threshold (e.g., 3 MADs above median if mt genes are annotated). Document all thresholds and the number of cells/perturbations removed.\\\",\\n          \\\"Quantify the fraction of zero-expression genes in controls and their contribution to MSE; consider reporting MSE on expressed genes only as a secondary metric.\\\",\\n          \\\"Specify the batch-perturbation confounding test: e.g., cross-tabulate batch and perturbation labels, compute Cramer's V or chi-square p-value, and report the proportion of perturbations with unbalanced batch distribution.\\\",\\n          \\\"Define the normalization verification procedure explicitly: check that X.max() is approximately log1p(10000) \\u2248 9.21 (or consistent with the stated normalization), that values are non-integer, and that the minimum is 0 (or near 0).\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Technical validation should include: (1) comparison of control mean estimates across batches and QC thresholds; (2) bootstrap stability of control mean (e.g., standard deviation across 100 bootstrap resamples); (3) comparison of held-out perturbation performance with and without QC filtering; (4) assessment of whether QC disproportionately removes cells from certain perturbations or cell states (e.g., compare cell-cycle phase composition before and after QC).\\\",\\n        \\\"technical_validation\\\": \\\"The analysis correctly recommends bootstrap resampling of control cells to estimate control mean uncertainty. This should be extended to propagate uncertainty to delta predictions and to report confidence intervals for pearson_delta and top-20 metrics. Additionally, a sensitivity analysis across QC thresholds (e.g., 1st/99th percentile vs. 2 MADs vs. 3 MADs) should be performed to ensure conclusions are robust.\\\",\\n        \\\"decision_support_quality\\\": \\\"The empty decision support blocks provide no pre-specified quality thresholds or risk tolerance. The current analysis correctly defers threshold setting to the data distribution and recommends validation against held-out perturbation metrics. Once decision support is populated, the quality thresholds and exclusion criteria should be reconciled with the risk assessment (e.g., if the risk assessment prioritizes avoiding false positives, more aggressive QC may be warranted).\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"reproducibility\\\": {\\n        \\\"assessment\\\": \\\"The dataset analysis includes strong reproducibility recommendations: deterministic seeding, pinned versions, unit tests, bitwise comparison of repeated runs, and leakage checks. This is well-aligned with best practices for computational biology.\\\",\\n        \\\"issues\\\": [\\n          \\\"The specific versions of Python, anndata, scanpy, numpy, scipy, scikit-learn, and pandas are not pinned in the analysis (though the problem investigation mentions pinning).\\\",\\n          \\\"The unit tests for output schema are mentioned but not specified in detail (e.g., exact assertions for perts order, means shape, dtype, gene order).\\\",\\n          \\\"The leakage checks are described conceptually but no concrete procedure (e.g., hash-based verification that held-out perturbation cells are excluded) is specified.\\\",\\n          \\\"The reproducibility of stochastic methods (e.g., bootstrap, cross-validation folds) is not fully addressed.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"improvements\\\": [\\n          \\\"Specify exact package versions in a requirements.txt or conda environment file, and log them at runtime.\\\",\\n          \\\"Define concrete unit tests: e.g., assert that perts equals the heldout list exactly (order and values), means.shape == (n_perts, n_genes), means.dtype == float32 or float64, var_names order matches the input, and all predicted means are finite and within the range of the input log1p CP10K values.\\\",\\n          \\\"Implement a leakage check: e.g., verify that the set of perturbation labels used in training does not intersect with the held-out set, and that no cells with held-out perturbation labels are present in the training data (by construction, but verify programmatically).\\\",\\n          \\\"For stochastic methods, fix seeds at the start of each run and document the seed; for cross-validation, use a deterministic fold assignment (e.g., sorted perturbation labels, then round-robin assignment) to ensure reproducibility across runs.\\\",\\n          \\\"Run the full pipeline twice with the same seed and compare outputs bitwise (or within a numerical tolerance for floating-point operations) to confirm determinism.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Reproducibility validation should include: (1) bitwise or near-bitwise comparison of outputs across two runs with the same seed; (2) verification that the pipeline produces identical results when run on a different machine with the same environment; (3) documentation of all random seeds and their usage; (4) a manifest of input file hashes and output file hashes.\\\",\\n        \\\"technical_validation\\\": \\\"The analysis correctly recommends running the pipeline twice with fixed seeds. This should be extended to a full reproducibility checklist: environment file, seed documentation, input/output hashes, and a README with step-by-step instructions to reproduce the su