n). If the held-out perturbation list is available, characterize its composition (essential genes, TFs, etc.) and compare to training perturbation classes to estimate proportion of out-of-distribution targets.\\\",\\n        \\\"types\\\": [\\n          \\\"CRISPRi (dCas9-KRAB) gene repression\\\",\\n          \\\"Single-gene perturbations (one target per held-out key)\\\",\\n          \\\"Control perturbations (non-targeting and safe-targeting if present)\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"doses\\\": [\\n          \\\"Not applicable / not reported; CRISPRi is expected to produce partial knockdown rather than complete knockout\\\",\\n          \\\"Effective knockdown strength varies by guide and target, introducing dose-like heterogeneity\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"timepoints\\\": [\\n          \\\"Single endpoint (not specified); assume one harvest timepoint\\\",\\n          \\\"Possible batch/lane variation in culture duration should be checked in obs metadata\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"biological_controls\\\": [\\n          \\\"Non-targeting and safe-targeting controls define the baseline expression and delta reference; confirm exact labels from obs\\\",\\n          \\\"Multiple guides per target provide internal replication\\\",\\n          \\\"Control cells should be matched for batch, lane, and cell-cycle composition where possible\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"decision_support_considerations\\\": \\\"Because perturbations are unseen, the critical design consideration is feature representation of perturbations (gene identity, functional annotations, co-expression modules) and a robust control baseline. Empty decision-support blocks mean no pre-specified constraints on model class, but the evaluator's pearson_delta metric requires an accurate control mean, so control estimation should be treated as a first-class modeling component. Biological relevance criteria (e.g., required target knockdown sign, pathway enrichment thresholds) must be defined once decision support is populated.\\\"\\n      }\\n    },\\n    \\\"samples\\\": {\\n      \\\"size\\\": \\\"Not specified in the task metadata; must be read from cellforge_input.h5ad (n_obs, n_vars). Expected to be thousands to hundreds of thousands of cells across hundreds to thousands of perturbations, typical of the Adamson et al. K562 CRISPRi screen.\\\",\\n      \\\"distribution\\\": \\\"Cells are pooled and unevenly distributed across perturbations and guides; per-perturbation cell counts are expected to be highly variable (long-tailed). Control cells are typically a sizable fraction. Held-out perturbations have zero cells by construction.\\\",\\n      \\\"characteristics\\\": [\\n        \\\"Cell-level covariates: total counts, detected genes, guide assignment confidence, cell-cycle phase, batch/lane\\\",\\n        \\\"Perturbation-level covariates: target gene ID, guide count, target expression level, target-gene class (essential/TF/housekeeping/low-expression)\\\",\\n        \\\"Gene-level covariates: mean expression, detection rate, variance\\\"\\n      ],\\n      \\\"biological_variability\\\": \\\"Cell-to-cell heterogeneity in K562 (cell cycle, differentiation state, clonal variation) plus genuine gene-specific knockdown effects. Perturbation effect sizes vary widely; some perturbations produce minimal transcriptional change.\\\",\\n      \\\"technical_variability\\\": \\\"Batch/lane effects, guide-capture efficiency, library size, ambient RNA, doublet/multiple-guide assignment, and CRISPRi knockdown efficiency variation.\\\",\\n      \\\"decision_support_requirements\\\": \\\"Empty decision-support blocks provide no sample-size or power constraints; nonetheless, per-perturbation cell counts and guide counts should be summarized to weight training and to flag low-confidence perturbations when analyzing training data. Estimate the number of training perturbations and cells from the data to set expectations for model complexity.\\\"\\n    },\\n    \\\"validation\\