dance\\\",\\n          \\\"QC pass rates (counts, genes detected, mitochondrial fraction if available)\\\",\\n          \\\"Correlation between replicate pseudo-bulks\\\",\\n          \\\"Bootstrap standard deviation of control mean\\\",\\n          \\\"Batch-perturbation association statistics (Cramer's V or chi-square p-value)\\\",\\n          \\\"Metric mean and standard deviation across QC thresholds and batch correction options\\\",\\n          \\\"Fraction of zero-expression genes and their contribution to MSE (report MSE on expressed genes only as secondary metric)\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"results\\\": \\\"Not computed; requires loading cellforge_input.h5ad.\\\",\\n        \\\"interpretation\\\": \\\"Technical validation should confirm that the stored matrix is already CP10k + log1p (not raw counts) and that control cells are sufficient and well-matched for delta computation. Define handling of zero-expression genes: exclude genes from correlation metrics where variance is zero in either predicted or true delta (report fraction of excluded genes and fraction of cases with undefined correlation; assign correlation zero if predicted delta is all zeros), but retain in output; document this in the evaluation plan. Specify concrete QC procedures: filter cells with total counts below 1st percentile or above 99th percentile (or MAD-based thresholds), genes detected below 1st percentile, and mitochondrial fraction above data-driven threshold (e.g., 3 MADs above median if mt genes are annotated); document all thresholds and number of cells/perturbations removed.\\\",\\n        \\\"decision_support_technical_validation\\\": \\\"Empty decision-support blocks; recommend documenting the exact normalization state and control definition before modeling. Reproducibility standards (required logging, version pinning) must be confirmed against the eventual implementation plan and risk assessment. Implement full reproducibility checklist: environment file with exact package versions (Python, anndata, scanpy, numpy, scipy, scikit-learn, pandas), seed documentation, input/output file hashes, and README with step-by-step instructions.\\\"\\n      }\\n    }\\n  },\\n  \\\"data_properties\\\": {\\n    \\\"distribution\\\": {\\n      \\\"biological\\\": {\\n        \\\"patterns\\\": [\\n          \\\"Log1p CP10k expression with strong zero-inflation and overdispersion typical of scRNA-seq\\\",\\n          \\\"Gene-level mean-variance relationship dominated by a few highly expressed genes (e.g., ribosomal, MALAT1, GAPDH)\\\",\\n          \\\"Perturbation effects concentrated in target gene and functionally related genes/pathways\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"variability\\\": \\\"Biological variance arises from cell state, cell cycle, and perturbation effect size; effect sizes are expected to be modest for most CRISPRi knockdowns in K562.\\\",\\n        \\\"outliers\\\": [\\n          \\\"Cells with unusually high counts or mitochondrial content\\\",\\n          \\\"Perturbations with very few cells or low guide confidence\\\",\\n          \\\"Genes with extreme expression or detection artifacts\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"interpretation\\\": \\\"The data are sparse, log-normalized, and perturbation effects are typically small relative to cell-to-cell noise, so mean-based predictions must be estimated from sufficient cells per perturbation. Distinguish direct target repression from downstream regulon effects when interpreting patterns.\\\",\\n        \\\"biological_significance\\\": \\\"Recovering the target-gene knockdown and coherent pathway responses is the biological signal of interest; top-20 DE genes capture this signal. Verify expected K562 markers (BCR-ABL programme, haemoglobin genes, essential genes) in control cells.\\\",\\n        \\\"decision_support_implications\\\": \\\"Empty decision-support blocks; modeling should prioritize shrinkage of per-perturbation means and use of gene-level priors to handle small effect sizes. Stratify biological relevance assessment by target-gene class.\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"technical\\\": {\\n        \\\"patterns\\\": [\\n          \\\"Library-size variation partially removed by CP10k but residual depth effects may remain\\\",\\n          \\\"Batch/lane effects on gene detection and expression\\\",\\n          \\\"Ambient RNA and doublets causing spurious guide assignments\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"variability\\\": \\\"Technical variance from sequencing depth, capture efficiency, and guide assignment confidence; may be confounded with perturbation if batches are unbalanced.\\\",\\n        \\\"artifacts\\\": [\\n          \\\"Doublets/multiplets with ambiguous guide identity\\\",\\n          \\\"Low-confidence guide calls\\\",\\n          \\\"Ambient contamination\\\",\\n          \\\"Batch-correlated expression shifts\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"interpretation\\\": \\\"Because the matrix is already normalized, additional batch correction must be applied cautiously to avoid removing perturbation signal; batch correction should be validated on control cells and against held-out perturbation metrics.\\\",\\n        \\\"mitigation\\\": [\\n          \\\"Filter low-confidence guide assignments and doublets\\\",\\n          \\\"Include batch as a covariate or use control-anchored correction\\\",\\n          \\\"Use robust per-perturbation mean estimation (e.g., trimmed means or pseudobulk)\\\",\\n          \\\"Quantify batch-perturbation confounding before deciding on correction\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"decision_support_considerations\\\": \\\"No decision-support constraints provided; batch handling should be evaluated via held-out perturbation performance rather than assumed beneficial.\\\"\\n      }\\n    },\\n    \\\"features\\\": {\\n      \\\"biological\\\": {\\n        \\\"types\\\": [\\n          \\\"Protein-coding genes (primary feature space)\\\",\\n          \\\"Target genes of perturbations (knockdown signature)\\\",\\n          \\\"Co-expressed gene modules and pathways\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"relevance\\\": \\\"All genes are required in the output, but only a subset carries perturbation signal; target genes and pathway members are most relevant for delta and top-20 metrics. Operationalize direct (target-gene self-knockdown) vs. downstream (regulon/module) effects in feature construction.\\\",\\n        \\\"selection\\\": [\\n          \\\"Use all genes for output completeness\\\",\\n          \\\"For model training, use highly variable genes (HVGs, top 2000-5000 by variance) or genes expressed in at least 1% of control cells, computed on training data only; document number of genes used for modeling vs. output\\\",\\n          \\\"Prioritize genes with detectable expression in K562\\\",\\n          \\\"Tie feature selection to cross-validated held-out perturbation performance; compare feature sets (all genes, HVGs, modules) under identical evaluation\\\",\\n          \\\"Avoid features that leak held-out perturbation identity\\\",\\n          \\\"Validate gene filtering: check selected genes include target genes of training perturbations (if expressed) and top DE genes for training perturbations; report overlap between HVGs and known perturbation-responsive genes\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"interpretation\\\": \\\"Feature relevance is gene- and perturbation-dependent; a model must output the full gene vector while internally focusing on informative genes.\\\",\\n        \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Validate feature utility via held-out perturbation pearson_delta and top-20 metrics, and via regulon enrichment permutation tests.\\\",\\n        \\\"decision_support_features\\\": \\\"Empty decision-support blocks; feature selection should be driven by cross-validated held-out perturbation performance.\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"technical\\\": {\\n        \\\"types\\\": [\\n          \\\"Gene identifiers and ordering (must match exactly)\\\",\\n          \\\"Detection rate and mean expression per gene\\\",\\n          \\\"Library-size and batch covariates in obs\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"quality\\\": \\\"Gene ordering and normalization must be preserved exactly\",\n  \"error\": \"Failed to parse JSON response\"\n}\n\nProblem Investigation:\n{\n  \"content\": \"{\\n  \\\"content\\\": {\\n    \\\"problem_definition\\\": {\\n      \\\"formal_definition\\\": {\\n        \\\"biological_context\\\": \\\"K562 chronic myeloid leukemia cells under CRISPRi (dCas9-KRAB) knockdown of individual genes. The training file cellforge_input.h5ad contains single-cell log1p CP10K expression for perturbations observed in training; held-out perturbations in heldout_perturbations.json have zero cells in training. The task is to predict, for each unseen CRISPRi target, the mean expression vector over all genes in the same gene order and normalization as the input. Control label(s) in obs must be confirmed programmatically from obs metadata before any analysis; if multiple control types exist (e.g., 'control', 'NTC', 'non-targeting', 'safe-targeting'), document all types, define the union of control cells, and justify the choice (e.g., if safe-targeting controls show distinct expression profiles, report and consider excluding them). CRISPRi knockdown strength varies by guide and target; this is an unobserved per-perturbation latent variable approximated via target-gene features and calibrated shrinkage. Direct target repression is distinguished from downstream regulon effects: the model decomposes predicted deltas into a target-gene self-knockdown component (predicted delta for the target gene itself, constrained to be non-positive when the target is expressed), gene-module (regulon) components, and a residual. Each component is reported separately in validation.\\\",\\n        \\\"input_output_mapping\\\": \\\"Input: (i) annotated scRNA-seq AnnData with cells x genes, obs including perturbation label and confirmed control label(s), var gene names/order, X log1p(CP10K); (ii) heldout_perturbations.json list of unseen perturbation keys. Output: predictions.npz with perts (held-out keys in listed order) and means (n_perts x n_genes float), where means[p,g] = predicted mean log1p(CP10K) expression of gene g in K562 cells carrying perturbation p. Delta is defined explicitly on the log1p(CP10K) scale as delta[p,g] = mean_pert[p,g] - mean_control[g], where mean_control is computed over all control cells in the training file (optionally batch-matched if batch effects are detected and validated). The evaluator implicitly compares this delta; top-20 DE genes are defined by the largest absolute true delta per perturbation.\\\",\\n        \\\"biological_constraints\\\": [\\n          \\\"All predictions must be on the same log1p(CP10K) scale as the input; no renormalization.\\\",\\n          \\\"Gene order must exactly match var_names of cellforge_input.h5ad.\\\",\\n          \\\"CRISPRi acts primarily by transcriptional repression of the target gene and, through partial knockdown, of downstream regulons; predicted direct-target effects should be non-positive when the target is expressed.\\\",\\n          \\\"K562-specific expression program (e.g., BCR-ABL programme genes, haemoglobin genes), copy-number heterogeneity, and low per-cell counts constrain realistic effect sizes.\\\",\\n          \\\"Held-out perturbations are unseen; predictions must generalize from gene-level features and from observed perturbation effects, not from memorized labels.\\\",\\n          \\\"Control mean must be estimated from the provided control cells only; no external screen data may be used.\\\",\\n          \\\"Essential genes may produce strong or lethal phenotypes not fully captured by mean expression; predictions for essential targets should be treated as lower-confidence and shrunk more aggressively.\\\",\\n          \\\"Predicted values must be finite and on the log1p CP10K scale; after inverse transform, means must be non-negative.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"evaluation_criteria\\\": [\\n          \\\"pearson_delta: per-perturbation Pearson correlation between predicted and true change-from-control vectors; rewards correct direction and relative magnitude of gene-level shifts. Undefined correlations (e.g., zero-variance predictions) are assigned zero and the fraction of undefined cases is reported.\\\",\\n          \\\"MSE: mean squared error of predicted vs true mean expression (or delta) across genes and perturbations; penalizes scale and baseline errors. Report MSE on all genes and on expressed genes only (genes with nonzero control mean) as a secondary metric.\\\",\\n          \\\"Top-20 DE metrics (MSE/PCC/R2): correctness of the strongest differentially expressed genes (largest absolute true delta), which carry most biological signal.\\\",\\n          \\\"Biological validity: predicted target-gene knockdown sign and magnitude (concordance rate >0.8 for expressed targets), enrichment of known K562/CRISPRi regulon members among top predicted DE genes (permutation test, p<0.05), and consistency with essential-gene and pathway annotations.\\\",\\n          \\\"Reproducibility: deterministic pipeline, fixed seeds, pinned versions, documented preprocessing, unit tests, and stable predictions under resampling.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"success_metrics\\\": [\\n          \\\"Mean pearson_delta across held-out perturbations above a control-mean baseline; target threshold set from baseline performance (e.g., >0.1) and documented.\\\",\\n          \\\"MSE lower than the variance of true perturbation means around control mean.\\\",\\n          \\\"Top-20 DE PCC and R2 above baseline (e.g., PCC > 0.2) and consistent with known K562 biology.\\\",\\n          \\\"Correct sign and magnitude of knockdown for the CRISPRi target gene where it is expressed; concordance rate >0.8 across expressed targets.\\\",\\n          \\\"Stable rankings of top predicted DE genes across bootstrap resamples of training cells.\\\"\\n        ]\\n      },\\n      \\\"key_variables\\\": {\\n        \\\"biological\\\": [\\n          \\\"Perturbation identity (CRISPRi target gene) and its baseline expression in K562.\\\",\\n          \\\"Gene-gene regulatory relationships (transcription factor targets, co-expression modules, protein complexes).\\\",\\n          \\\"Cell-state heterogeneity in K562 (cell cycle, copy-number variation, differentiation state).\\\",\\n          \\\"Essentiality and network centrality of the target gene.\\\",\\n          \\\"Baseline control expression mean and variance per gene.\\\",\\n          \\\"Target-gene class (essential, transcription factor, housekeeping, low-expression) to stratify predictability.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"technical\\\": [\\n          \\\"Library size and CP10K normalization, log1p transform.\\\",\\n          \\\"Guide RNA efficiency and CRISPRi knockdown strength (unobserved per perturbation).\\\",\\n          \\\"Batch/experiment effects and donor/replicate structure in obs.\\\",\\n          \\\"Gene filtering and var order in the AnnData.\\\",\\n          \\\"Number of cells per observed perturbation (uneven support).\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"relationships\\\": [\\n          \\\"Delta_pert = f(target gene features, network neighbourhood, control expression).\\\",\\n          \\\"Perturbations targeting genes in the same complex/module produce correlated deltas.\\\",\\n          \\\"Effect magnitude scales with target expression and network centrality.\\\",\\n          \\\"Gene-level deltas are approximately additive in log space for weak perturbations.\\\",\\n          \\\"Direct target repression is separable from downstream module effects via decomposition.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"constraints\\\": [\\n          \\\"No held-out perturbation cells may be used at any stage, including feature construction and hyperparameter tuning. Verify programmatically that training perturbation labels do not intersect held-out labels.\\\",\\n          \\\"Control mean must be computed from control cells in the provided file.\\\",\\n          \\\"Predictions must be finite, on log1p CP10K scale, and non-negative after inverse transform.\\\",\\n          \\\"Computational budget must allow full training on the provided AnnData without external downloads.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"validation_requirements\\\": [\\n          \\\"Held-out-perturbation cross-validation within the training file (simulate unseen perturbations by leaving out entire perturbation labels).\\\",\\n          \\\"Baseline comparisons: control mean, target-only knockdown, and simple linear models.\\\",\\n          \\\"Bootstrap stability of top-20 DE gene predictions.\\\",\\n          \\\"Biological sanity checks against known K562 CRISPRi hits and pathway annotations (MSigDB, Reactome) using permutation tests.\\\",\\n          \\\"Stratified CV by target expression, essentiality, network centrality, and target-gene class to report performance gradients.\\\",\\n          \\\"Leakage checks: verify held-out perturbation labels and cells are excluded from all fits; hash-based verification of training/held-out partition.\\\"\\n        ]\\n      },\\n      \\\"scope\\\": {\\n        \\\"biological_scope\\\": \\\"Unseen-perturbation prediction of mean transcriptional response to CRISPRi knockdown in a single cancer cell line (K562). Focus on gene-level mean shifts, not cell-state distributions or guide-level heterogeneity. Predictability varies by perturbation class: essential genes, transcription factors, and genes with no close training analogs are expected to be harder; predictions for these are lower-confidence and more shrunk. Perturbations with no measurable effect or lethal phenotypes are handled by predicting near-zero delta with calibrated shrinkage and flagging as lower-confidence. Mean effects may miss distributional changes (e.g., cell-state composition shifts), which limits biological interpretation for some perturbations. The expected number and biological composition of held-out perturbations should be characterized from heldout_perturbations.json if available (e.g., overlap with essential genes, TFs, training perturbation classes); otherwise, stratified performance reporting provides feasibility expectations and the proportion of out-of-distribution targets is estimated from feature similarity to training perturbations.\\\",\\n        \\\"technical_scope\\\": \\\"Train only on cellforge_input.h5ad; build gene and perturbation feature representations from training data; predict held-out perturbation mean vectors; output predictions.npz in the required format. Predictions are constrained to be finite and on the log1p CP10K scale, with non-negativity enforced after inverse transform.\\\",\\n        \\\"limitations\\\": [\\n          \\\"Cannot model guide-specific knockdown efficiency for unseen perturbations; knockdown strength is approximated via target features and calibrated shrinkage.\\\",\\n          \\\"Cannot capture perturbation-specific cell-state composition changes beyond mean effects.\\\",\\n          \\\"Limited by the coverage and quality of observed perturbations in the training file.\\\",\\n          \\\"No external screen or literature-derived effect sizes may be used.\\\",\\n          \\\"Linear/additive assumptions may miss strong non-linear or synthetic interactions.\\\",\\n          \\\"Perturbations with no measurable effect or lethal phenotypes are not explicitly modelled; strategy is to predict near-zero delta with calibrated shrinkage.\\\",\\n          \\\"Number and biological composition of held-out perturbations may be unknown; feasibility depends on training perturbation coverage.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"assumptions\\\": [\\n          \\\"Control cells in the file are representative of the unperturbed K562 baseline; control label(s) are confirmed and documented from obs metadata. If multiple control types exist, the union is used unless batch-matching or expression profiling indicates otherwise.\\\",\\n          \\\"Gene order and normalization are consistent between training and evaluation.\\\",\\n          \\\"Held-out perturbations share biological context (K562, CRISPRi) with training perturbations, but may include out-of-distribution targets (essential genes, TFs with no close analogs).\\\",\\n          \\\"Mean delta is the primary quantity of interest; higher moments are not scored.\\\",\\n          \\\"Gene features derived from training expression and co-expression are transferable to unseen perturbations.\\\",\\n          \\\"If batch effects are present, control mean can be batch-matched without leakage, validated by held-out perturbation performance.\\\",\\n          \\\"The training file contains a sufficient number of perturbations and cells to support feature-based transfer; if not, model complexity is reduced.\\\",\\n          \\\"The held-out perturbation list may be unavailable at analysis time; if so, feasibility estimates rely on stratified CV and feature-similarity analysis of training perturbations.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"biological_validation\\\": \\\"Validate predicted deltas against known K562 CRISPRi biology: target-gene knockdown sign and magnitude (concordance rate >0.8 for expressed targets), enrichment of regulon members among top predicted DE genes (permutation test, p<0.05), essential-gene effects, and concordance with co-perturbation clusters. Check that control cells express expected K562 markers (BCR, ABL1, HBB, HBG1, HBG2, known essential genes). Use held-out-perturbation cross-validation as the primary internal proxy for the official evaluator, stratified by target expression, essentiality, network centrality, and target-gene class. Use pathway enrichment (MSigDB Hallmark and Reactome via hypergeometric test or GSEA, with permutation-based FDR control) and network proximity of predicted DE genes to the perturbed gene as secondary checks, requiring permutation-based significance. Pre-register validation thresholds before model evaluation to avoid post-hoc rationalization.\\\"\\n      }\\n    },\\n    \\\"key_challenges\\\": {\\n      \\\"biological\\\": {\\n        \\\"challenges\\\": [\\n          \\\"Unseen perturbations have no cells, so no direct supervision of their effects.\\\",\\n          \\\"CRISPRi knockdown strength varies by target and guide and is unobserved.\\\",\\n          \\\"K562 has strong copy-number heterogeneity and cell-cycle structure that confound mean effects.\\\",\\n          \\\"Perturbation effects can be non-linear and context-dependent (e.g., essential genes).\\\",\\n          \\\"Many perturbations have weak or near-zero transcriptional effects, making delta prediction noisy.\\\",\\n          \\\"Held-out perturbations may have no close training analogs, limiting feature-based transfer.\\\",\\n          \\\"Direct target repression and downstream regulon effects are entangled in mean deltas.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"impact\\\": \\\"Naive models collapse to control mean and yield near-zero pearson_delta; over-predicting large effects harms MSE and top-20 metrics. Inability to separate direct from downstream effects reduces interpretability and biological validity.\\\",\\n        \\\"mitigation\\\": [\\n          \\\"Use gene-level features (expression, co-expression, network centrality, essentiality proxies) to transfer information to unseen targets.\\\",\\n          \\\"Shrink predicted deltas toward zero with regularization tuned by held-out-perturbation CV; specify a shrinkage factor alpha in [0,1] multiplied to predicted deltas and tuned to maximize pearson_delta subject to MSE constraints. Stratify alpha by target-gene class and neighbor distance (e.g., cosine similarity to nearest training perturbation).\\\",\\n          \\\"Model effect magnitude as a function of target expression and network degree.\\\",\\n          \\\"Use robust control mean estimation (trimmed mean or median-of-means) and per-gene variance weighting; select method by bootstrap stability.\\\",\\n          \\\"Quantify similarity between held-out and training perturbations using gene-level features to estimate predictability and set shrinkage per perturbation.\\\",\\n          \\\"Decompose predicted deltas into target-gene self-knockdown (constrained non-positive for expressed targets), module components, and residual; report variance explained by each component.\\\",\\n          \\\"Flag essential genes and no-effect perturbations as lower-confidence and shrink more aggressively.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Leave-one-perturbation-out CV on observed perturbations, stratified by effect size, target expression, essentiality, network centrality, and target-gene class, with pearson_delta and top-20 metrics computed identically to the official evaluator. Verify target-gene knockdown sign and magnitude where the target is expressed (concordance rate >0.8). Report decomposition components separately.\\\",\\n        \\\"biological_considerations\\\": \\\"Preserve the biology that CRISPRi primarily represses the target and its regulon; enforce non-positive direct-target effects where appropriate and allow downstream positive/negative changes. Predictions for essential genes are flagged as lower-confidence. Validate against K562-specific markers and known CRISPRi hits.\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"technical\\\": {\\n        \\\"challenges\\\": [\\n          \\\"High-dimensional output (n_genes per perturbation) with limited number of observed perturbations.\\\",\\n          \\\"Sparse, zero-inflated single-cell data and log1p CP10K scale.\\\",\\n          \\\"Uneven cell numbers per perturbation and potential batch effects.\\\",\\n          \\\"Strict requirement to match gene order and normalization exactly.\\\",\\n          \\\"No external data allowed, limiting pretrained transfer.\\\",\\n          \\\"Undefined correlations for zero-variance predictions in pearson_delta.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"impact\\\": \\\"Overfitting to observed perturbations, unstable predictions, and format errors that invalidate submission. Undefined correlations can bias metric computation if not handled.\\\",\\n        \\\"mitigation\\\": [\\n          \\\"Low-rank / factor models and ridge-regularized regression from perturbation features to delta vectors.\\\",\\n          \\\"Dimensionality reduction (PCA, NMF, gene modules) before regression; project back to full gene space.\\\",\\n          \\\"Explicit format checks: perts order equals heldout list, means shape and dtype float, gene order equals var_names.\\\",\\n          \\\"Deterministic seeding, pinned versions, and unit tests for preprocessing and output.\\\",\\n          \\\"Verify normalization state empirically (max value consistent with log1p CP10K, non-integer values) before modeling.\\\",\\n          \\\"Handle undefined correlations by assigning zero and reporting the fraction of undefined cases.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Unit tests on output schema; CV metrics; sanity check that predicted control-mean baseline reproduces input control mean within numerical tolerance; run pipeline twice with same seed to confirm identical outputs; compute input/output file hashes and document seeds.\\\",\\n        \\\"implementation_requirements\\\": \\\"Python with anndata, scanpy, numpy, scipy, scikit-learn, pandas; CPU-feasible pipeline; fixed random seeds; pinned and logged versions of Python, anndata, scanpy, numpy, scipy, scikit-learn, pandas (e.g., in requirements.txt or conda environment file, logged at runtime); unit tests for output format, gene order, and leakage checks.\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"data_quality\\\": {\\n        \\\"issues\\\": [\\n          \\\"Low and variable library sizes per cell.\\\",\\n          \\\"Zero inflation and dropout in log1p CP10K data.\\\",\\n          \\\"Potential doublets and low-quality cells.\\\",\\n          \\\"Batch/experiment structure in obs; batch-perturbation confounding not quantified.\\\",\\n          \\\"Held-out perturbations may be enriched for weak or essential genes.\\\",\\n          \\\"QC thresholds not derived from data distribution.\\\",\\n          \\\"Genes with zero expression in controls affect delta computation and correlation metrics.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"impact\\\": \\\"Noisy control mean and noisy per-perturbation means in training, biasing feature learning and calibration. Zero-expression genes can produce undefined correlations or inflated MSE.\\\",\\n        \\\"mitigation\\\": [\\n          \\\"Specify concrete QC threshold procedures: MAD-based filters on total counts and genes detected (e.g., 3 MADs from median), mitochondrial fraction thresholds if mt genes are annotated (e.g., 3 MADs above median), and doublet score cutoffs. Set thresholds at data-driven tails after inspecting distributions. Document all thresholds and number of cells/perturbations removed.\\\",\\n          \\\"Robust control mean via trimmed mean or median-of-means; select method by bootstrap stability.\\\",\\n          \\\"Weight genes by detection rate and variance in loss/metrics; exclude zero-variance genes from correlation metrics but retain in output. Quantify fraction of zero-expression genes in controls and their contribution to MSE; report MSE on expressed genes only as a secondary metric.\\\",\\n          \\\"Quantify batch-perturbation confounding by cross-tabulation and association statistics (e.g., Cramer's V or chi-square p-value) before deciding on batch correction; report proportion of perturbations with unbalanced batch distribution. Apply batch correction only if confounding is quantified and correction improves held-out perturbation metrics.\\\",\\n          \\\"Define criteria for excluding low-quality perturbations from training (e.g., <20 cells or low guide concordance) and document excluded perturbations; weight training perturbations by cell count and guide confidence.\\\",\\n          \\\"Document handling of zero-expression genes: exclude from correlation metrics where variance is zero in either predicted or true delta, but retain in output; report fraction excluded.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"validation\\\": \\\"Compare control mean estimates across batches and QC thresholds; bootstrap stability of gene features and predictions; compare held-out perturbation performance with and without QC filtering and with different thresholds; assess whether QC disproportionately removes cells from certain perturbations or cell states (e.g., compare cell-cycle phase and clustering composition before and after QC).\\\",\\n        \\\"biological_validation\\\": \\\"Check that top expressed genes and control mean match expected K562 markers (e.g., BCR, ABL1, HBB, HBG1,\",\n  \"error\": \"Failed to parse JSON response\"\n}\n\nBaseline Assessment:\n{\n  \"content\": \"{\\n  \\\"content\\\": {\\n    \\\"baseline_models\\\": {\\n      \\\"literature_review\\\": {\\n        \\\"existing_methods\\\": {\\n          \\\"methods\\\": [\\n            \\\"Control-mean (zero-delta) baseline: predict the mean expression of control cells for every held-out perturbation; delta prediction is zero. Control cells are defined by the exact control label(s) in obs metadata; these must be confirmed before analysis (see assumptions).\\\",\\n            \\\"Global-mean-delta baseline: predict the average delta (perturbation mean minus control mean) across all training perturbations for every held-out perturbation.\\\",\\n            \\\"Per-gene linear/ridge regression on perturbation one-hot encoding: fit gene-wise models on training perturbation means; for unseen perturbations, predictions reduce to the control mean (or training-perturbation mean) with gene-wise shrinkage.\\\",\\n            \\\"Latent linear models: PCA on training perturbation means followed by ridge regression from perturbation descriptors (if available) or nearest-neighbor transfer in PCA space.\\\",\\n            \\\"Nearest-neighbor baseline: match held-out perturbations to training perturbations using gene-level features (e.g., control-centered mean profiles, target-gene embeddings) and transfer the closest training perturbation effect; quantify neighbor distance to estimate predictability.\\\",\\n            \\\"Descriptor-based ridge/kernel regression: if target-gene features are available (e.g., expression level, essentiality, network centrality, gene embeddings), regress perturbation effects on descriptors.\\\",\\n            \\\"Low-rank matrix factorization (e.g., truncated SVD, NMF) of the perturbation-by-gene effect matrix to share statistical strength across perturbations.\\\",\\n            \\\"Graph/network propagation baselines: build a gene co-expression or PPI graph and propagate known perturbation effects to unseen perturbations using network similarity; validate against over-smoothing.\\\",\\n            \\\"Autoencoder/VAE latent-space models: train on single-cell expression, then model perturbation effects in latent space with simple regressors (only if sufficient perturbations exist).\\\",\\n            \\\"Set-transformer/attention models: treat perturbation as a token and predict gene-wise deltas via cross-attention over training perturbations (only if sufficient training perturbations exist).\\\"\\n          ],\\n          \\\"strengths\\\": [\\n            \\\"Control-mean and global-delta baselines are robust, fast, and establish lower bounds for pearson_delta and MSE.\\\",\\n            \\\"Linear/ridge and low-rank models are interpretable and computationally cheap, suitable for high-dimensional gene space with limited perturbations.\\\",\\n            \\\"Nearest-neighbor methods can capture similarity between perturbations if informative features exist; neighbor distance provides a predictability estimate.\\\",\\n            \\\"Graph-based methods incorporate biological prior knowledge and may generalize to unseen perturbations with similar network neighborhoods.\\\",\\n            \\\"Descriptor-based models can leverage target-gene properties (expression, essentiality, network centrality) to stratify predictability.\\\"\\n          ],\\n          \\\"limitations\\\": [\\n            \\\"No major perturbation-prediction methods were identified in the retrieved literature, so the benchmark relies on generic baselines and must be constructed carefully.\\\",\\n            \\\"Linear models cannot capture nonlinear or combinatorial perturbation effects.\\\",\\n            \\\"Nearest-neighbor methods depend heavily on informative perturbation descriptors; in CRISPRi K562, held-out perturbations may have no close training neighbors, especially essential genes or transcription factors with no analogs.\\\",\\n            \\\"Graph propagation requires high-quality networks and may over-smooth effects.\\\",\\n            \\\"Deep generative/attention models require large training data and risk overfitting given the limited number of perturbations.\\\",\\n            \\\"Most methods struggle with unseen-perturbation generalization because perturbation effects are highly gene- and context-specific; perturbations with no measurable effect or lethal phenotypes are particularly challenging.\\\"\\n          ],\\n          \\\"biological_relevance\\\": \\\"CRISPRi perturbations in K562 target specific genes; prediction should capture downstream transcriptional consequences. Baselines that shrink toward control mean are biologically conservative but may miss real effects. Predictability should be stratified by target-gene properties (expression, essentiality, network centrality) because essential genes may produce strong or lethal phenotypes not captured by mean expression alone. Graph/network methods can leverage known regulatory relationships, but validation must focus on top DE genes and pathway-level effects rather than global correlation alone. Operationalize direct vs. downstream effects by separating target-gene self-knockdown from regulon modules in the model and validation (see recommendations).\\\"\\n        },\\n        \\\"model_comparison\\\": {\\n          \\\"comparison_criteria\\\": [\\n            \\\"Pearson correlation of predicted vs. true delta from control per perturbation (primary).\\\",\\n            \\\"MSE on mean expression and on delta, with calibration (slope/intercept) reported.\\\",\\n            \\\"Top-20 DE gene recovery: MSE, PCC, R2 on top-20 DE genes, where top-20 DE genes are defined by the largest absolute true delta as implied by the evaluator.\\\",\\n            \\\"Computational cost and scalability to ~10k genes and hundreds of perturbations.\\\",\\n            \\\"Robustness to biological and technical variability (batch, cell-cycle, library size) and to control-cell subsampling.\\\",\\n            \\\"Biological interpretability: whether predicted effects align with known pathways and gene function, including sign and magnitude of predicted target-gene delta.\\\",\\n            \\\"Stratified performance by target-gene properties (expression, essentiality, network centrality) and by neighbor distance to training perturbations.\\\"\\n          ],\\n          \\\"performance_metrics\\\": [\\n            \\\"pearson_delta (per perturbation, then averaged; report undefined/zero handling: assign zero or exclude and report fraction of undefined cases).\\\",\\n            \\\"MSE (global and per perturbation) and calibration slope/intercept of predicted vs. true delta.\\\",\\n            \\\"Top-20 DE MSE, PCC, R2.\\\",\\n            \\\"Spearman rank correlation of gene effects.\\\",\\n            \\\"Precision@k and recall@k for top-k DE genes (k=20, 50, 100).\\\",\\n            \\\"Per-gene correlation across perturbations.\\\",\\n            \\\"Bootstrap confidence intervals for pearson_delta and top-20 DE metrics.\\\"\\n          ],\\n          \\\"biological_interpretability\\\": \\\"Linear, low-rank, and nearest-neighbor models are most interpretable (effect = weighted sum of training effects, module loadings, or direct transfer). Graph methods provide pathway-level interpretation. Deep models are less interpretable unless attention or gene-level attributions are used. Define interpretability metrics: sign and magnitude of predicted target-gene delta, enrichment of known regulon members in top predicted DE genes (permutation test), and module loading coherence. Decompose predicted deltas into target-gene component, module components, and residual to separate direct from downstream effects. Report per-perturbation top modules and enriched pathways for held-out predictions as a standard output. Specify the decomposition model: e.g., a linear model where predicted delta for perturbation p is sum of a target-gene-specific term (based on target identity/features), a module-loading term (based on co-expression modules), and a residual; report variance explained by each component. For low-rank models, recommend inspecting module loadings and perturbation scores and computing gene-level attributions (contribution of each module to each perturbation's predicted delta).\\\",\\n          \\\"computational_requirements\\\": \\\"Control-mean and global-delta baselines require negligible computation. Ridge/PCA, low-rank, and nearest-neighbor methods scale to thousands of genes and hundreds of perturbations with standard CPU resources. Graph propagation may require network preprocessing but remains feasible. Deep attention/VAE models require GPU training and careful hyperparameter tuning; given the small number of perturbations, they risk overfitting and are not recommended as first-line baselines. Quantify memory requirements and recommend sparse representations or reduced gene space for modeling.\\\"\\n        }\\n      },\\n      \\\"recommendations\\\": {\\n        \\\"model_selection\\\": [\\n          \\\"Start with control-mean (zero delta) and global-mean-delta baselines as mandatory sanity checks.\\\",\\n          \\\"Implement ridge regression on perturbation means with gene-wise shrinkage as a strong linear baseline.\\\",\\n          \\\"Implement low-rank matrix factorization (e.g., truncated SVD) of the perturbation-by-gene effect matrix to share statistical strength.\\\",\\n          \\\"Implement nearest-neighbor transfer in a reduced latent space (PCA on training perturbation means) and report neighbor distances to estimate predictability.\\\",\\n          \\\"If perturbation descriptors (e.g., target-gene expression, essentiality, network centrality, gene embeddings) are available, implement descriptor-based ridge or kernel regression.\\\",\\n          \\\"Only then consider deep models (set transformer, VAE) if sufficient training perturbations exist and validation shows gains over linear baselines.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"implementation_priority\\\": [\\n          \\\"1. Confirm and document the exact control label(s) from obs metadata before any analysis; if multiple control types exist (e.g., NTC and safe-targeting), define the union and justify the choice. Reproduce the evaluator exactly: compute control mean from training control cells, define delta as perturbation mean minus control mean on the log1p CP10k scale, and evaluate on held-out perturbations using pearson_delta, MSE, and top-20 DE metrics.\\\",\\n          \\\"2. Build a robust preprocessing pipeline: verify normalization state (max value consistent with log1p CP10k, non-integer values), filter genes consistently, and ensure gene order matches cellforge_input.h5ad var_names. Document handling of zero-expression genes: exclude from correlation metrics where variance is zero but retain in output.\\\",\\n          \\\"3. Implement and tune linear/ridge and low-rank baselines with leave-one-perturbation-out cross-validation over training perturbations. Use nested cross-validation (inner loop for hyperparameters, outer loop for evaluation) to avoid leakage during tuning.\\\",\\n          \\\"4. Add nearest-neighbor and descriptor-based baselines; quantify neighbor distance to training perturbations.\\\",\\n          \\\"5. Evaluate deep models only if they beat linear baselines in cross-validation.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"biological_validation\\\": \\\"Validate predictions by checking enrichment of known K562 CRISPRi effects (e.g., knockdown of essential genes, pathway members) in top predicted DE genes. Confirm that predicted target knockdown appears for expressed targets and that top modules match known regulons. Compare predicted deltas for held-out perturbations to known gene-function relationships where available. Use pathway enrichment and gene-set enrichment analysis (MSigDB, Reactome) as secondary biological checks with permutation tests for enrichment significance. Add K562-specific biological checks: expected expression of BCR-ABL programme genes, haemoglobin genes, and known essential genes; verify these in control cells. Stratify biological relevance assessment by target-gene class (essential, TF, housekeeping, low-expression) to set expectations for predictability. Operationalize direct-vs-downstream decomposition: define a target-gene self-knockdown component (e.g., predicted delta for the target gene itself constrained to be non-positive) and a regulon/module component, and report each separately in validation. If the held-out perturbation list is available, characterize its composition (essential genes, TFs, etc.) and compare to training perturbation classes to estimate the proportion of out-of-distribution targets. Define a concrete calibrated shrinkage procedure: shrink predicted deltas by a factor alpha in [0,1] tuned via leave-one-perturbation-out CV to maximize pearson_delta subject to an MSE constraint, with alpha stratified by target-gene class and neighbor distance. Specify thresholds and statistical tests (e.g., permutation p-value < 0.05 for regulon enrichment, target-gene delta sign concordance rate > 0.8 for expressed targets); pre-register these criteria before model evaluation.\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"assumptions\\\": [\\n        \\\"The exact control label(s) in obs metadata are not confirmed; we assume they will be identified and documented before analysis. If multiple control types exist (e.g., NTC and safe-targeting), their union will be used and justified.\\\",\\n        \\\"The number and biological composition of held-out perturbations are unknown; we assume they are representative of the training perturbation classes (essential genes, transcription factors, housekeeping, low-expression) to set feasibility expectations. If the held-out list becomes available, its composition will be characterized and compared to training classes.\\\",\\n        \\\"The matrix is pre-normalized (log1p CP10k); we assume no renormalization is needed and verify empirically (max value approximately log1p(10000) \\u2248 9.21, non-integer values, minimum >= 0).\\\",\\n        \\\"The decision support blocks (model selection, evaluation strategy, data preparation, implementation plan, risk assessment) are currently empty; all recommendations are provisional and must be reconciled with the populated decision support blocks once available.\\\"\\n      ]\\n    },\\n    \\\"evaluation_framework\\\": {\\n      \\\"metrics\\\": {\\n        \\\"primary_metrics\\\": [\\n          \\\"pearson_delta: Pearson correlation between predicted and true delta from control, per held-out perturbation, averaged across perturbations; handle undefined correlations (e.g., zero-variance predictions) explicitly by assigning zero or excluding and reporting the fraction of undefined cases.\\\",\\n          \\\"MSE on mean expression (n_perts x n_genes).\\\",\\n          \\\"Top-20 DE gene MSE, PCC, and R2 (computed per perturbation and averaged), where top-20 DE genes are defined by the largest absolute true delta.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"secondary_metrics\\\": [\\n          \\\"MSE on delta (perturbation mean minus control mean).\\\",\\n          \\\"Spearman rank correlation of gene effects.\\\",\\n          \\\"Precision@k and recall@k for top-k DE genes (k=20, 50, 100).\\\",\\n          \\\"Calibration error: slope/intercept of predicted vs. true delta.\\\",\\n          \\\"Per-gene correlation across perturbations.\\\",\\n          \\\"Bootstrap confidence intervals for pearson_delta and top-20 DE metrics.\\\",\\n          \\\"MSE on expressed genes only (secondary, given zero-expression genes).\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"biological_metrics\\\": [\\n          \\\"Pathway/gene-set enrichment of top predicted DE genes vs. known CRISPRi targets, with permutation-based significance.\\\",\\n          \\\"Recovery of known downstream targets of the perturbed gene.\\\",\\n          \\\"Directional consistency of predicted effects for known activating/repressing relationships (predominant repression expected for CRISPRi).\\\",\\n          \\\"Network proximity of predicted DE genes to the perturbed gene in a PPI/regulatory network.\\\",\\n          \\\"Sign and magnitude of predicted target-gene delta for expressed targets.\\\",\\n          \\\"Target-gene delta sign concordance rate for expressed targets (threshold > 0.8).\\\",\\n          \\\"Regulon enrichment permutation p-value < 0.05.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"validation_metrics\\\": [\\n          \\\"Cross-validation over training perturbations: hold out a subset of training perturbations and compute the same metrics.\\\",\\n          \\\"Bootstrap confidence intervals for pearson_delta and top-20 DE metrics.\\\",\\n          \\\"Sensitivity to normalization and gene filtering choices.\\\",\\n          \\\"Robustness to control-cell subsampling and batch effects.\\\",\\n          \\\"Stratified performance by target-gene expression, essentiality, network centrality, and neighbor distance to training perturbations.\\\",\\n          \\\"Control mean consistency across QC thresholds and batches (correlation and maximum absolute difference).\\\",\\n          \\\"Sensitivity analysis of held-out perturbation metrics to preprocessing choices (QC thresholds, gene filtering, batch correction).\\\"\\n        ]\\n      },\\n      \\\"validation_strategy\\\": {\\n        \\\"cross_validation\\\": \\\"Use leave-one-perturbation-out or k-fold cross-validation over the training perturbations to tune model hyperparameters and estimate generalization. Because held-out perturbations are entirely unseen, cross-validation must mimic this setting: never use the held-out perturbation's cells or mean in training. Use nested cross-validation (inner loop for hyperparameters, outer loop for evaluation) to avoid leakage during tuning. Stratify folds by target-gene expression, essentiality, and network centrality to assess performance gradients. Use deterministic fold assignment (e.g., sorted perturbation labels, then round-robin assignment) to ensure reproducibility. Report mean and standard deviation of metrics across folds.\\\",\\n        \\\"biological_validation\\\": \\\"For each held-out perturbation, compare predicted top DE genes to known biology of the targeted gene (e.g., if the target is a transcription factor, check enrichment of its known targets). Use independent pathway databases (MSigDB Hallmark and Reactome) and, where possible, external CRISPRi screens in K562 as a sanity check, but do not use external data for training. Use permutation tests to assess enrichment significance (p-value < 0.05). Verify that control cells express expected K562 markers (BCR, ABL1, HBB, HBG1, HBG2, known essential genes) and that training perturbations show target-gene knockdown where the target is expressed. Report stratified results by target-gene class (essential, TF, housekeeping, low-expression).\\\",\\n        \\\"independent_testing\\\": \\\"The provided held-out_perturbations.json is the primary independent test set. Additionally, create an internal held-out set from training perturbations (e.g., 20% of perturbations) to estimate performance before final submission. Ensure no data leakage: control mean must be computed only from training control cells, gene filtering must be consistent across train and test, and held-out perturbation labels and cells must be excluded from all fits, including feature construction and hyperparameter tuning. Implement a leakage check: verify programmatically that the set of perturbation labels used in training does not intersect with the held-out set, and that no cells with held-out perturbation labels are present in the training data.\\\",\\n        \\\"robustness_testing\\\": \\\"Evaluate model sensitivity to: (1) control-cell subsampling (bootstrap control mean, e.g., 100 resamples), (2) gene filtering thresholds (all genes vs. HVGs vs. expressed genes), (3) normalization choices (CP10k vs. library-size normalization), (4) random seeds for stochastic models, and (5) batch/plate effects if metadata are available. Quantify batch-perturbation confounding by cross-tabulating batch and perturbation labels and computing association statistics (e.g., Cramer's V or chi-square p-value) before deciding on batch correction. Report variance of metrics across these perturbations.\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"benchmarks\\\": {\\n        \\\"baseline_models\\\": [\\n          \\\"Control mean (zero delta)\\\",\\n          \\\"Global mean delta (average delta across all training perturbations)\\\",\\n          \\\"Gene-wise linear regression on perturbation one-hot encoding (predicts training mean, reduces to control mean for unseen)\\\",\\n          \\\"Ridge regression on perturbation means with gene-wise shrinkage\\\",\\n          \\\"Low-rank matrix factorization (e.g., truncated SVD) of the perturbation-by-gene effect matrix\\\",\\n          \\\"Nearest-neighbor transfer in PCA space of training perturbation means, with neighbor distance reported\\\",\\n          \\\"Descriptor-based ridge/kernel regression if perturbation features (target expression, essentiality, network centrality, gene embeddings) are available\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"state_of_the_art\\\": [\\n          \\\"No state-of-the-art perturbation-prediction methods were identified in the retrieved literature; the benchmark should be constructed from the baselines above and any recent general-purpose methods (e.g., linear mixed models, matrix factorization) applied to this dataset.\\\",\\n          \\\"If external methods are considered, they must be retrained only on cellforge_input.h5ad to respect the task constraints.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"biological_benchmarks\\\": [\\n          \\\"Known CRISPRi knockdown effects in K562 for housekeeping/essential genes.\\\",\\n          \\\"Pathway-level enrichment of predicted DE genes for held-out perturbations with permutation-based significance.\\\",\\n          \\\"Consistency with known gene regulatory relationships (e.g., transcription factor targets).\\\",\\n          \\\"Sign and magnitude of predicted target-gene delta for expressed targets.\\\",\\n          \\\"Target-gene delta sign concordance rate > 0.8 for expressed targets.\\\",\\n          \\\"Regulon enrichment permutation p-value < 0.05.\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"performance_targets\\\": [\\n          \\\"Beat control-mean baseline on pearson_delta (i.e., positive correlation on average).\\\",\\n          \\\"Achieve top-20 DE PCC significantly above zero and above the global-delta baseline.\\\",\\n          \\\"Maintain MSE no worse than the control-mean\",\n  \"error\": \"Failed to parse JSON response\"\n}\n\nPlease provide comprehensive final recommendations in the following JSON format, incorporating decision support insights:\n{\n    \"model_selection\": {\n        \"recommended_models\": {\n            \"primary\": [\"string\"],\n            \"secondary\": [\"string\"],\n            \"rationale\": \"string\",\n            \"biological_justification\": \"string\",\n            \"decision_support_alignment\": \"string\"\n        },\n        \"implementation_priority\": {\n            \"high_priority\": [\"string\"],\n            \"medium_priority\": [\"string\"],\n            \"low_priority\": [\"string\"],\n            \"rationale\": \"string\",\n            \"decision_support_priority\": \"string\"\n        },\n        \"biological_interpretability\": {\n            \"requirements\": [\"string\"],\n            \"approaches\": [\"string\"],\n            \"validation\": \"string\",\n            \"decision_support_interpretability\": \"string\"\n        }\n    },\n    \"evaluation_strategy\": {\n        \"framework\": {\n            \"design\": \"string\",\n            \"components\": [\"string\"],\n            \"metrics\": [\"string\"],\n            \"decision_support_framework\": \"string\"\n        },\n        \"validation\": {\n            \"biological\": [\"string\"],\n            \"technical\": [\"string\"],\n            \"cross_validation\": \"string\",\n            \"decision_support_validation\": \"string\"\n        },\n        \"benchmarks\": {\n            \"baselines\": [\"string\"],\n            \"state_of_the_art\": [\"string\"],\n            \"performance_targets\": [\"string\"],\n            \"decision_support_benchmarks\": \"string\"\n        }\n    },\n    \"data_preparation\": {\n        \"preprocessing\": {\n            \"strategies\": [\"string\"],\n            \"quality_control\": [\"string\"],\n            \"normalization\": [\"string\"],\n            \"decision_support_preprocessing\": \"string\"\n        },\n        \"requirements\": {\n            \"data_types\": [\"string\"],\n            \"quality_standards\": [\"string\"],\n            \"validation_requirements\": [\"string\"],\n            \"decision_support_requirements\": \"string\"\n        },\n        \"limitations\": {\n            \"identified\": [\"string\"],\n            \"mitigation\": [\"string\"],\n            